me players are also selling stocks to buy dollars due to Vietnam's rising trade deficit," says trader. Adds, players likely to stay on sidelines ahead of key government meeting later this week; investors waiting for government to step in with new financial measures to help boost economy in 4Q. Volume thin with 33.2 million shares valued at VND897 trillion. Among most active, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) ends flat at VND32,200, Saigon Securities (SSI.VH) ends down 1.5% at VND27,000, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends down 3.0% at VND16,500. Tips support for key index at 445. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0441 GMT [Dow Jones] If HSI ends today in positive territory, index will have risen 17 of 20 trading sessions so far in September, notes futures trader. "Market's strength is unmistakable," he says; although market clearly overbought, says traders better not go short as market can go from strength to strength. HSI +1.3% at 22,392.09 midday, taking cue from Wall Street; volume solid at HK$46.10 billion, in part boosted by combined HK$3.16 billion from today's 2 debutantes; HSI's year-to-date high of 22,671 eyed next. Chalco (2600.HK) leads blue-chips' rally, +5.2% at HK$7.70 on top of 4.3% gain yesterday, which came on parent's CNY10 billion plan to invest in rare earth resources. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.2% at 1462.46, trapped in narrow 6-point range throughout morning session in subdued trading; some 426 million shares traded by midday, among lowest levels in recent days. Range-bound trading pattern likely to persist rest of day as participants mostly content to stay on sidelines in absence of fresh leads, dealers say. RHB Research says while KLCI's longer-term uptrend intact, with support at 1450, index must break 1467 (10-day moving average) to regain bullish momentum in the near-term; says in meantime, "local market sentiment will continue to be influenced by volatility in the overseas markets." Among gainers, Gamuda (5398.KU) +1.6% at MYR3.86 after reporting 77% jump in 4Q FY10 earnings, UEM Land (5148.KU) +5.4% at MYR2.13 on upbeat outlook for property demand. Among decliners, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 2.8% at MYR2.08 on profit-taking after recent rally to record high. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares holding above water but further gains limited as investors cautious after solid gains recently. STI +0.5% at 3112.62 midday, with immediate resistance at year-to-date high of 3125. Market breadth at over 3 gainers for every decliner, but overall volume light at 1.06 billion shares worth S$931.9 million, with Genting Singapore (G13.SG), \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  (S21.SG) most active. Genting Singapore +0.5% at S$1.96, \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  +4.7% at US$0.45, recouping part of yesterday's hefty losses prompted by Genting Singapore CEO's stake reduction via sale in open market. "The market is not doing that badly. Some profit-taking is to be expected as prices cannot go up everyday," says broker at Singapore bank. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.8% at 9568.20, helped by gains in Asian markets, strong China PMI data, say market analysts. "Strong Chinese markets are suggesting that economic sentiment is good," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Also says as for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, there are hopes for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (as USD/JPY trading below 84) and additional monetary easing measures from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , therefore investors may stay on sidelines or now. Index resistance seen at 9600. Exporters generally higher on strong tankan data; \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  (7201.TO) up 3.0% at Y747, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 2.1% at Y2,648, \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  (9101.TO) up 2.9% at Y349. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) off 7.7% at Y2,106 on over 6X normal full-day volume on capital increase reports, dragging entire Topix electric power & gas sector down 2.8%. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs up on view that additional BOJ policy-easing now "just a matter of time," as tankan survey suggests economy will likely fall into a lull with business sentiment index for large manufacturers expected to fall to minus 1 in December survey, 1st deterioration in 7 quarters, Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Naomi Hasegawa says. "There are just so may bad factors for the economy going into the second half of fiscal 2010," she says, such as end to government incentive program to buy fuel-efficient cars, strong JPY. Lead December futures last 143.25, up 0.20 while 10-year yield last down 2.5 bps at 0.935%.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, reversing early-session gains, as investors take profit following recent gains. Bellwether PSEi off 0.3% to 4111.05 in heavy volume vs 4142 intraday high. "The market has been up a lot so there's need for a healthy profit-taking," says Jose Vistan, AB Capital Securities research head. Expects benchmark index to test 4000 support in near term; next resistance at 4130. Among most actives, Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH) unchanged at PHP3.80; Energy Development (EDC.PH) off 3.5% at PHP6.25, Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH) off 1.7% at PHP70.60. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds up on positive cues from U.S. Treasurys but liquidity crunch will cap gains, says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.73 vs 99.63 at Tuesday's close. "Tight liquidity will limit the gains for the 10-year paper and markets will await for the weekly inflation and Friday's auction cut-off for cues," says dealer. Tips 2020 bond yield in 7.80%-7.85%, last 7.84%. Overnight interbank call rate surges to 7.30% vs 7% at last close and 130 bps above RBI's repo rate of 6.00%. Liquidity tight after outflow of INR400 billion-INR500 billion for companies' advance tax payments, possibly large withdrawals by companies for previous loans. RBI to auction INR110 billion government bonds Friday. Dealer notes, any buying by banks to build books at last trading day of July-September quarter is limited as most lenders holding government bonds above mandatory statutory liquidity ratio of 25%. Indian money markets shut Thursday for banks' half yearly book closing. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.2% at 970.26 buoyed by firmer regional bourses, though off from fresh 14-year-high of 971.23 tapped earlier. SET Index likely to be volatile as market looks for fresh drivers. Sentiment remains positive on back of growing economy but index may reverse to negative territory on profit-taking, says Finansia Syrus Securities' analyst Somchai Anektaweepon. Resistance pegged at 975, support at 954. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +3.9% at THB5.35, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +3.9% at THB133, BANPU (BANPU.TH) +1.1% at THB712, \uc2 Jasmine International\uc2  (JAS.TH) +2.9% at THB1.42, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +0.6% at THB331. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economy looks just fine, should do even better next year, RBA board member Jillian Broadbent tells Dow Jones; "Australia is fine. We will probably get higher growth in 2011. China will continue to grow and the U.S. will continue to struggle." Comments on China are especially important, given RBA itself has recently drawn attention to ongoing strength in Asia, forceful flow-through effect for Australia. What's more, says any volatility on financial markets won't necessarily reflect underlying fundamentals; "The markets are going to be much more volatile than the fundamental economics suggest they should be." See: "=INTERVIEW: Reserve Bank Of Australia's Broadbent Expects Higher Australia Growth In 2011." (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 0052ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
