. Market breadth at over 3 gainers for every decliner, but overall volume light at 1.06 billion shares worth S$931.9 million, with Genting Singapore (G13.SG), \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  (S21.SG) most active. Genting Singapore +0.5% at S$1.96, \uc2 Genting Hong Kong\uc2  +4.7% at US$0.45, recouping part of yesterday's hefty losses prompted by Genting Singapore CEO's stake reduction via sale in open market. "The market is not doing that badly. Some profit-taking is to be expected as prices cannot go up everyday," says broker at Singapore bank. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.8% at 9568.20, helped by gains in Asian markets, strong China PMI data, say market analysts. "Strong Chinese markets are suggesting that economic sentiment is good," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Also says as for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, there are hopes for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (as USD/JPY trading below 84) and additional monetary easing measures from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , therefore investors may stay on sidelines or now. Index resistance seen at 9600. Exporters generally higher on strong tankan data; \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  (7201.TO) up 3.0% at Y747, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 2.1% at Y2,648, \uc2 Nippon Yusen\uc2  (9101.TO) up 2.9% at Y349. \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2  (9501.TO) off 7.7% at Y2,106 on over 6X normal full-day volume on capital increase reports, dragging entire Topix electric power & gas sector down 2.8%. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs up on view that additional BOJ policy-easing now "just a matter of time," as tankan survey suggests economy will likely fall into a lull with business sentiment index for large manufacturers expected to fall to minus 1 in December survey, 1st deterioration in 7 quarters, Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Naomi Hasegawa says. "There are just so may bad factors for the economy going into the second half of fiscal 2010," she says, such as end to government incentive program to buy fuel-efficient cars, strong JPY. Lead December futures last 143.25, up 0.20 while 10-year yield last down 2.5 bps at 0.935%.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, reversing early-session gains, as investors take profit following recent gains. Bellwether PSEi off 0.3% to 4111.05 in heavy volume vs 4142 intraday high. "The market has been up a lot so there's need for a healthy profit-taking," says Jose Vistan, AB Capital Securities research head. Expects benchmark index to test 4000 support in near term; next resistance at 4130. Among most actives, Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH) unchanged at PHP3.80; Energy Development (EDC.PH) off 3.5% at PHP6.25, Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH) off 1.7% at PHP70.60. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds up on positive cues from U.S. Treasurys but liquidity crunch will cap gains, says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.73 vs 99.63 at Tuesday's close. "Tight liquidity will limit the gains for the 10-year paper and markets will await for the weekly inflation and Friday's auction cut-off for cues," says dealer. Tips 2020 bond yield in 7.80%-7.85%, last 7.84%. Overnight interbank call rate surges to 7.30% vs 7% at last close and 130 bps above RBI's repo rate of 6.00%. Liquidity tight after outflow of INR400 billion-INR500 billion for companies' advance tax payments, possibly large withdrawals by companies for previous loans. RBI to auction INR110 billion government bonds Friday. Dealer notes, any buying by banks to build books at last trading day of July-September quarter is limited as most lenders holding government bonds above mandatory statutory liquidity ratio of 25%. Indian money markets shut Thursday for banks' half yearly book closing. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.2% at 970.26 buoyed by firmer regional bourses, though off from fresh 14-year-high of 971.23 tapped earlier. SET Index likely to be volatile as market looks for fresh drivers. Sentiment remains positive on back of growing economy but index may reverse to negative territory on profit-taking, says Finansia Syrus Securities' analyst Somchai Anektaweepon. Resistance pegged at 975, support at 954. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +3.9% at THB5.35, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +3.9% at THB133, BANPU (BANPU.TH) +1.1% at THB712, \uc2 Jasmine International\uc2  (JAS.TH) +2.9% at THB1.42, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +0.6% at THB331. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economy looks just fine, should do even better next year, RBA board member Jillian Broadbent tells Dow Jones; "Australia is fine. We will probably get higher growth in 2011. China will continue to grow and the U.S. will continue to struggle." Comments on China are especially important, given RBA itself has recently drawn attention to ongoing strength in Asia, forceful flow-through effect for Australia. What's more, says any volatility on financial markets won't necessarily reflect underlying fundamentals; "The markets are going to be much more volatile than the fundamental economics suggest they should be." See: "=INTERVIEW: Reserve Bank Of Australia's Broadbent Expects Higher Australia Growth In 2011." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] ICE Dollar Index, which tracks greenback against trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , falls to eight-month low below 78.861. Broad-based USD-selling comes as recent weakness in U.S. data fuels expectations for further Fed easing, prompting investors to unload USD on view U.S. rates to stay low longer than expected. While USD recently tended to benefit on weak U.S. data as investors took refuge in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as safe haven, trend appears to be breaking down, as bad data now adding to speculation Fed may conduct more quantitative easing, say \uc2 Barclays Capital\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts David Forrester and Yuki Sakasai in research note. "We note that there are nascent signs of a return of the positive correlation between the USD and our U.S. Data Surprises Index -- i.e. bad U.S. economic data are bad news for the dollar." USD-selling Wednesday comes after Conference Board report showed consumer confidence fell sharply in September. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 0045ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
