\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei Up 0.8%; China PMI, Asian Mkt Gains Help\b0\par\par\uc2 1,589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 06:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0437 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.8% at 9568.20, helped by gains in Asian markets, strong China PMI data, say market analysts. "Strong Chinese markets are suggesting that economic sentiment is good," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Also says as for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, there are hopes for possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (as USD/JPY trading below 84) and additional monetary easing measures from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , therefore investors may stay on sidelines or now. Index resistance seen at 9600. Exporters generally higher on strong tankan data; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nissan Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (7201.TO) up 3.0% at Y747, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sony\f28 \uc2 \f2  (6758.TO) up 2.1% at Y2,648, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nippon Yusen\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9101.TO) up 2.9% at Y349. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electric Power\f28 \uc2 \f2  (9501.TO) off 7.7% at Y2,106 on over 6X normal full-day volume on capital increase reports, dragging entire Topix electric power & gas sector down 2.8%. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs up on view that additional BOJ policy-easing now "just a matter of time," as tankan survey suggests economy will likely fall into a lull with business sentiment index for large manufacturers expected to fall to minus 1 in December survey, 1st deterioration in 7 quarters, Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities senior strategist Naomi Hasegawa says. "There are just so may bad factors for the economy going into the second half of fiscal 2010," she says, such as end to government incentive program to buy fuel-efficient cars, strong JPY. Lead December futures last 143.25, up 0.20 while 10-year yield last down 2.5 bps at 0.935%.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0433 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end lower, reversing early-session gains, as investors take profit following recent gains. Bellwether PSEi off 0.3% to 4111.05 in heavy volume vs 4142 intraday high. "The market has been up a lot so there's need for a healthy profit-taking," says Jose Vistan, AB Capital Securities research head. Expects benchmark index to test 4000 support in near term; next resistance at 4130. Among most actives, Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH) unchanged at PHP3.80; Energy Development (EDC.PH) off 3.5% at PHP6.25, Metropolitan Bank (MBT.PH) off 1.7% at PHP70.60. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0431 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds up on positive cues from U.S. Treasurys but liquidity crunch will cap gains, says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.73 vs 99.63 at Tuesday's close. "Tight liquidity will limit the gains for the 10-year paper and markets will await for the weekly inflation and Friday's auction cut-off for cues," says dealer. Tips 2020 bond yield in 7.80%-7.85%, last 7.84%. Overnight interbank call rate surges to 7.30% vs 7% at last close and 130 bps above RBI's repo rate of 6.00%. Liquidity tight after outflow of INR400 billion-INR500 billion for companies' advance tax payments, possibly large withdrawals by companies for previous loans. RBI to auction INR110 billion government bonds Friday. Dealer notes, any buying by banks to build books at last trading day of July-September quarter is limited as most lenders holding government bonds above mandatory statutory liquidity ratio of 25%. Indian money markets shut Thursday for banks' half yearly book closing. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +1.2% at 970.26 buoyed by firmer regional bourses, though off from fresh 14-year-high of 971.23 tapped earlier. SET Index likely to be volatile as market looks for fresh drivers. Sentiment remains positive on back of growing economy but index may reverse to negative territory on profit-taking, says Finansia Syrus Securities' analyst Somchai Anektaweepon. Resistance pegged at 975, support at 954. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +3.9% at THB5.35, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +3.9% at THB133, BANPU (BANPU.TH) +1.1% at THB712, \uc2 Jasmine International\uc2  (JAS.TH) +2.9% at THB1.42, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +0.6% at THB331. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0423 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economy looks just fine, should do even better next year, RBA board member Jillian Broadbent tells Dow Jones; "Australia is fine. We will probably get higher growth in 2011. China will continue to grow and the U.S. will continue to struggle." Comments on China are especially important, given RBA itself has recently drawn attention to ongoing strength in Asia, forceful flow-through effect for Australia. What's more, says any volatility on financial markets won't necessarily reflect underlying fundamentals; "The markets are going to be much more volatile than the fundamental economics suggest they should be." See: "=INTERVIEW: Reserve Bank Of Australia's Broadbent Expects Higher Australia Growth In 2011." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] ICE Dollar Index, which tracks greenback against trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , falls to eight-month low below 78.861. Broad-based USD-selling comes as recent weakness in U.S. data fuels expectations for further Fed easing, prompting investors to unload USD on view U.S. rates to stay low longer than expected. While USD recently tended to benefit on weak U.S. data as investors took refuge in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as safe haven, trend appears to be breaking down, as bad data now adding to speculation Fed may conduct more quantitative easing, say \uc2 Barclays Capital\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts David Forrester and Yuki Sakasai in research note. "We note that there are nascent signs of a return of the positive correlation between the USD and our U.S. Data Surprises Index -- i.e. bad U.S. economic data are bad news for the dollar." USD-selling Wednesday comes after Conference Board report showed consumer confidence fell sharply in September. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] REVIEW: Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to raise key policy interest rate by 12.5 bps to 1.50% at quarterly meeting Thursday, 8 out of 10 economists in Dow Jones poll say. Economists say local economy continues to recover, despite slowdown in global growth, rate hike may also help rein in soaring property prices. "(Taiwan) is on the path to normalization. The deterioration of the global economic outlook is not serious enough to warrant any pause in raising the interest rate," says Tim Condon, economist at ING. But SinoPac Securities tips \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stand pat. "The recovery of the U.S. economy isn't that strong. (Taiwan's) trading partners, particularly Japan and Europe, are not doing well. Domestically, inflationary pressure isn't strong either." Rate decision due 0800 GMT Thursday. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Lackluster metals trading in Shanghai with very low volumes is leaving LME metals mixed in Asia, despite gains overnight in response to renewed bout of USD weakness. However, traders have stops above $8,000/ton for copper and with April high of $8,043 within reach there's no reason why bullish funds won't push metals higher when London opens. LME 3-month copper at $7,975/ton, up $24 with 688 lots done. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,308/ton, flat with 212 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD shrugs off August trade deficit with broad USD moves "dominating this \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the moment" says \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sue Trinh; Pair last at 0.7393 vs 0.7389 early. "Overall, the U.S. dollar still remains at near 7-month lows on a DXY basis and we expect aggressive USD selling to pick up as the month-end passive real money managers are likely to be forced to sell U.S. dollars to rebalance their \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedges in the next few sessions." Says USD weakness likely to continue pushing Kiwi higher into end of week, positive equity market also supporting risk appetite. Tips initial resistance at 0.7420, followed by 0.7500, initial support around 0.7350 followed by 0.7210. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] HSBC China PMI for September indicates "conditions clearly stabilizing in the manufacturing sector," despite slowdown in recent months, says \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2  economist Brian Jackson in note; indicator rose to five month high of 52.9 in September. Strong PMI "should reinforce Beijing's view that there is no need to reverse policy tightening measures put in place earlier this year, with further gradual moves in that direction more likely in the months ahead," Jackson says. See: UPDATE: HSBC China September PMI Rises To Five-Month High (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0403 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR falls to near 5-month low as USD continues to slide on weak US data, while EUR holds firm against greenback, says dealer with state-run bank; USD/INR at 44.93 after touching low of 44.895 vs 45.16 in late Tuesday trade. "There are high expectations of strong capital flows into local stocks for big ticket share sales, like state-run \uc2 Coal India\uc2  which continues to fire up the rupee," says dealer. Tips 44.82 to offer strong support to pair, resistance capped at 45.07/45.08. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com) [ 09-29-10 0036ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
