\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Rise; Both Sides Protection Sought\b0\par\par\uc2 1,573 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 05:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0321 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options rise to 10.50%/11.20% vs 10.35%/11.05% in New York Tuesday; options dealer at major Japan bank says investors' growing interest in protection against both upside, downside risks suggests market participants increasingly uncertain about if, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities will again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; further falls in pair increasing demand for downside protection, while \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk augurs well for upside protection. Pair fell to 83.68 overnight, lowest since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Sept 15; has spent most of Asian morning below 84.00. Some traders had said a fall below 84.00 would increase chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but nothing seen. Dealer says one player buys 1-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 86.00 with implied vols of 11.00%, another player buys overnight USD-call/JPY-put contracts at 85.00 with 20.50%, another player buys 3-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at 89.00 with 12.05%. Amounts of deals unknown. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to hold 0.1% gain with heavyweight Telecom NZ (TEL.NZ) +1.5% at NZ$2.07 as investors prepare for tomorrow's AGM; NZX-50 "will probably stay up but it always moves around on a couple of stocks," says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Adrian Vance. Adds Albano Healthcare (ABN.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$5.30 after announcing to sell of share holding in National Hearing Care for more than A$26 million, considering possible capital return to shareholders in early 2011. Nuplex (NPX.NZ) down 3.0% to NZ$3.29, Fletcher Building down 2.3% to NZ$8.39 after companies went ex-dividend. Says \uc2 Pyne Gould Corp\uc2  (PGC.NZ) +2.4% to NZ$0.42 after annual report gave investors some more detail about execution about strategy; "I think they are moving along positively; they have a lot on with this merger with the other entities and they have to balance that off with the additional costs that come through from the crown guarantee," says Vance. NZX-50 last 3233.12. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.9% at 3504.596 in moderate volume, supported by IDR strength, gains in regional markets, traders say; tip resistance at 3520. "Healthy foreign buying continues to drive the main index higher amid a generally bullish sentiment," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Foreigners net buyers of IDR16 billion worth of local shares so far today. Among gainers, Telkom (TLKM.JK) +2.2% at IDR9,250 after ADRs rose in NY, gas, oil producer Medco (MEDC.JK) +8.9% at IDR3,375, while food producer \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) +2.9% at IDR5,400 on hopes for strong 3Q earnings. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0310 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed; key 10-year bond higher on gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, but 5-year note lower on concerns \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may hike policy rate tomorrow, says local bank trader. "The market liquidity is still ample," but gains likely limited on profit-taking, he says. 10-year yield last at 1.2040% vs 1.2083% yesterday, tipped in 1.1970%-1.2200% band for day. 5-year yield at 0.8825% vs 0.8765% yesterday, eyed in 0.8700%-0.9000% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones]Lead December JGB futures may rise to 143.39 as "the market is probably going to focus on the fact that the outlook in the tankan was lower than expected," says Christian Carrillo, senior rates strategist at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . Contract up 0.23 at 143.28 midday. Benchmark 10-year yield down 2.5 bps at 0.935%. Yet while tankan adds to expectations BOJ will ease monetary policy further, possibly as early as at meeting next week, says JGB buying may be limited ahead. "Even taking into account expectations for quantitative easing, I think JGBs are quite expensive now." With budget concerns simmering in background as government moves to increase stimulus spending, says 10-year yield likely to find some support around 0.900% in coming weeks, eventually settling around 1.00%. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open down as broad USD weakness to support local unit, says dealer with foreign bank; pair likely to open at 45.05 vs 45.16 late yesterday. "Dollar is weaker in all G-10 countries. We also expect capital flows to continue into local stocks as a couple of share sale offers are lined up which should help rupee to gain." Adds dollar demand from corporates, oil companies may kick in on sharp drops in pair. Tips pair in 44.90-45.20 band for session. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR5.73 billion worth of stocks yesterday. USD/INR 1-month NDFs at 45.25/45.35. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] September has brought re-acceleration in CNY gains vs USD, and annualised pace of rise since de-peg on June 19 now stands at around 6.5%, Standard Chartered says. "However, this development has come amid broad-based dollar weakness, and the yuan has actually been losing ground against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," house says. Expects general USD weakness to persist into year-end. "Against this backdrop, there may be a little more scope for yuan appreciation near-term," However, reckons USD/CNY forwards still discount too little CNY strength on 1-year horizon. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0258 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's 2011 budget (released Tuesday) favors GDP growth, in contrast with that of 2010, Standard Chartered says. "The 2011 budget plans to normalise spending growth to 5.7%, from the 3.0% contraction in the 2010 budget; this would support GDP growth." Adds, though infrastructure spending scheduled to decline in 2010, 2011, "this can also be seen as a normalisation of fiscal spending" after surging by 11% in 2008, 24% in 2009. Notes 2011 budget assumes ratio of government debt to GDP will decline to 35.2% from 36.1% in 2010, "which should be bullish for the bond market," report says. "The government is likely to repeat the usual front-loading of fiscal spending in 2011 to offset the anticipated slowdown in growth momentum," report says. House tips 2011 GDP growth at 4.1% vs official forecast of 5%. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely to open higher tracking overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys, says trader with primary dealership; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond yield likely in 7.83%-7.87% range vs 7.85% (99.63 in price terms) at Tuesday's close. "It seems like another rangebound day as tight liquidity may limit upside," says dealer. Banks borrowed INR703.10 billion from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday, higher than INR681.35 billion on Monday. Dealer adds some buying may kick-in for propping bank books as session is last trading day for July-September quarter, but traders may avoid big positions. Indian money markets shut on Thursday for banks' half yearly book closing. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] "Chinflation" is new risk for South Korea's economy, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says. Defines concept, for which initial coinage is uncertain, as inflation imported from China, via rising output costs in country, in particular labor costs; notes growing concern among Korean policymakers that "China could export inflation to Korea, even as external demand wanes" as shown by Korean current account surplus more than halving in August from July - a factor that should favor strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in Korea, reinforcing house's view that USD/KRW may slide towards 1,125 in short term. Adds "chinflation" also stoking similar fears, possibly with similar responses, elsewhere in Asia, even as \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may continue to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains under control. USD/KRW last 1,140. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares moving up slightly, helped by ADB raising its economic growth forecast for region, including Philippines. Main gauge PSEi up 0.1% at 4128.24 amid thin early trade. Investors also positioning themselves for corporate earning prospects, says Grace Cerdenia, 2TradeAsia.com research head. Tips next resistance at 4170, support 4100. Most active are Metro Pacific Investments (MPI.PH), +1.8% at PHP3.87; Energy Development (EDC.PH), off 0.6% at PHP6.44%; First Gen (FGEN.PH), +1.5% at PHP13.86. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds slightly lower amid increasing liquidity pressure ahead of week-long National Day holiday starts Friday, when public's demand for cash typically rises. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.54 vs 126.56 last close, while interbank 7-day weighted average repo, which up 10 bps yesterday, +6 bps at 2.62%. Shanghai-based trader with local broker says market volume relatively low in recent days as many traders have already left for holidays; "the bond index will likely stay rangebound today and tomorrow." (ming.wang@dowjones.com) [ 09-28-10 2321ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t0002g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\png