\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Korea Bonds Barely Move; Price Burden Weighs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 03:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures barely move with prices widely deemed too expensive following recent sharp gains. "It's not easy" to decide whether to buy or sell now, says local bank trader; tight supply-demand conditions in months to come indicate market to remain bullish for longer period of time, but players feel yields have fallen too much. December bond futures up 2 ticks at 112.39, 3-year yield quoted unchanged at 3.37%, 5-year also unchanged at 3.80%. August industrial output data tomorrow, inflation readings Friday to provide some cues for future direction; "The BOK may feel pressured (to raise rates) if the headline inflation touches 3% this month. If BOK stands pat again in October, there will likely be no change to the policy rate for the rest of the year," trader says. 5-year notes unlikely to move more than 5 bps in either direction today. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB falls to fresh 13-year low at 30.50 vs 30.595 late yesterday in tandem with broad USD weakness. But suspected BOT \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 30.50 helps limit falls, say dealers. "Selling pressure isn't that heavy this morning as many players anticipated some serious \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 30.50, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to be defending aggressively," says Bangkok-based dealer. If 30.50 fails to hold, next support pegged at 30.45. Such a low level could also attract some USD demand from importers, which should partly help stabilize pair. Resistance for day at 30.60. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0133 GMT [Dow Jones] Asian names selling good amounts of EUR/USD in past 30 minutes from above 1.3580, driving pair as low as 1.3566, says Singapore dealer. Pair last 1.3572. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR still under pressure, but suspected Bank Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps pair steady, now being quoted at 8,945-8,955 vs 8,950 last done late yesterday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of having bought around $50 million at 8,950, and now suspected of placing bid at 8,945 via state banks, according to 3 dealers. "The dollar is still under pressure against the rupiah, as investors' appetite for riskier assets is improving," dealer says. Pair tipped in narrow 8,945-8,955 range for day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.4% at 1465.68, tracking gains on Wall Street overnight; however, absence of fresh fundamental cues may keep key index confined to narrow band of 1460-1470 in subdued trading rest of day, dealer says, adding range-bound trading may persist in coming days. "Investors will stay generally cautious until the 2011 budget," dealer says. Government due to unveil 2011 budget on October 15. Among gainers, Gamuda (5398.KU) +1.6% at MYR3.86 after reporting 77% rise in FY10 earnings, Ingress (7122.KU) +2.4% at MYR0.845 after Thai unit gets contract to supply parts to Honda, Suzuki. Among decliners, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 1.4% at MYR2.11 on profit-taking after reaching record high earlier this week. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's August trade data, particularly weaker-than-expected exports, reaffirms case RBNZ likely to remain on sidelines until global developments improve, says ANZ economist Mark Smith; "The weakening trend in commodity export prices suggest there could be more (export weakness) to come." Adds driving decrease in exports, down 11.5% on month, was a 10% fall in value of commodity-based exports with falls for meat, dairy, forestry; "Other exports held up reasonably well, increasing by 3.1% over the month." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks rebound from yesterday's pullback, drawing strength from Wall Street's overnight gains. STI +0.3% at 3107.65, with resistance expected at year-to-date high of 3125. "There were no signs of quarterly window dressing as of yesterday, but we do not discount buying on dips and a bit more activity in the last two days of 3Q10," says CIMB. Gainers outnumbering decliners by almost 8 to 1 in broad market. Among blue chips, Wilmar (F34.SG) +1.3% at S$6.15, CityDev (C09.SG) +1.1% at S$12.52, \uc2 Singapore Press Holdings\uc2  (T39.SG) +1.0% at S$4.17. Genting Singapore (G13.SG) most active in market, down 0.5% at S$1.94, extending yesterday's 3.5% fall, on follow-through selling prompted by CEO's recent sale of shares in open market. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW slides further to just above key 1,140 level, which \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities widely believed to want to support amid broad USD weakness. Traders say BOK likely buying USD in light volumes just to smoothen KRW's rising pace, looks to be aiming to prevent pair from sliding below 1,140. "Selling by offshore players is consistently seen, while some are playing short and the BOK looks to be supporting the pair little by little so that it won't fall below 1,140 at once," says local bank trader; tips further falls in afternoon with exporters, after waiting until last minute to sell at higher levels, can't help but step in. Foreign bank trader says "we can't look only to the BOK's (suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) now. You see that a fall is limited during today's session and find the next day that it slides further despite such (suspected) BOK action." Adds pair likely to end just above 1,140 level vs 1,143.65 now, 1,146.3 yesterday. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold, silver both hovering just below overnight high levels in early Asia trade after WSJ article Tuesday indicated U.S. Federal Reserve was considering open-ended commitment to Quantitative Easing. Though plan being considered was described as "small scale approach" by Fed sources, article indicated that at around $100 billion/month it could get close to financing entire U.S. fiscal deficit for 2011 -- hardly likely to be considered small scale by gold bulls who are buying the metal as hedge against fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness. "The final quarter of the year threatens to be a torrid one for sterling, the euro, and the dollar," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Spot gold at $1,308.20/oz, down 80 cents and just off record of $1,310.15; spot silver at $21.76/oz, up 2 cents and just off 30-year high of $21.77. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may move lower as investors likely to take profit following recent gains. Benchmark PSEi may move towards 4100 support after rising 0.03% yesterday to end at 4123.95. "The market is in a corrective phase, we've been at an all-time high this week and a potential correction is in the air," says SB Equities research head Erwin Balita. Next resistance pegged at 4130. Still, some investors may take interest in infrastructure-related issues as President Aquino garnered $2.4 billion in fresh investments from recent U.S. trip. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ tankan shows big manufacturers expect USD/JPY to average 89.66 this fiscal year, well above current rate at 83.98. "The higher assumed rate suggests a likely impact on business profits in the future if the exchange rate stays lower," says RuiXue Xu, debt m