\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Steady; Bank Indonesia Support Suspected\b0\par\par\uc2 1,587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 03:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR still under pressure, but suspected Bank Indonesia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeps pair steady, now being quoted at 8,945-8,955 vs 8,950 last done late yesterday. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected of having bought around $50 million at 8,950, and now suspected of placing bid at 8,945 via state banks, according to 3 dealers. "The dollar is still under pressure against the rupiah, as investors' appetite for riskier assets is improving," dealer says. Pair tipped in narrow 8,945-8,955 range for day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.4% at 1465.68, tracking gains on Wall Street overnight; however, absence of fresh fundamental cues may keep key index confined to narrow band of 1460-1470 in subdued trading rest of day, dealer says, adding range-bound trading may persist in coming days. "Investors will stay generally cautious until the 2011 budget," dealer says. Government due to unveil 2011 budget on October 15. Among gainers, Gamuda (5398.KU) +1.6% at MYR3.86 after reporting 77% rise in FY10 earnings, Ingress (7122.KU) +2.4% at MYR0.845 after Thai unit gets contract to supply parts to Honda, Suzuki. Among decliners, AirAsia (5099.KU) down 1.4% at MYR2.11 on profit-taking after reaching record high earlier this week. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] New Zealand's August trade data, particularly weaker-than-expected exports, reaffirms case RBNZ likely to remain on sidelines until global developments improve, says ANZ economist Mark Smith; "The weakening trend in commodity export prices suggest there could be more (export weakness) to come." Adds driving decrease in exports, down 11.5% on month, was a 10% fall in value of commodity-based exports with falls for meat, dairy, forestry; "Other exports held up reasonably well, increasing by 3.1% over the month." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0117 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks rebound from yesterday's pullback, drawing strength from Wall Street's overnight gains. STI +0.3% at 3107.65, with resistance expected at year-to-date high of 3125. "There were no signs of quarterly window dressing as of yesterday, but we do not discount buying on dips and a bit more activity in the last two days of 3Q10," says CIMB. Gainers outnumbering decliners by almost 8 to 1 in broad market. Among blue chips, Wilmar (F34.SG) +1.3% at S$6.15, CityDev (C09.SG) +1.1% at S$12.52, \uc2 Singapore Press Holdings\uc2  (T39.SG) +1.0% at S$4.17. Genting Singapore (G13.SG) most active in market, down 0.5% at S$1.94, extending yesterday's 3.5% fall, on follow-through selling prompted by CEO's recent sale of shares in open market. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0113 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW slides further to just above key 1,140 level, which \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities widely believed to want to support amid broad USD weakness. Traders say BOK likely buying USD in light volumes just to smoothen KRW's rising pace, looks to be aiming to prevent pair from sliding below 1,140. "Selling by offshore players is consistently seen, while some are playing short and the BOK looks to be supporting the pair little by little so that it won't fall below 1,140 at once," says local bank trader; tips further falls in afternoon with exporters, after waiting until last minute to sell at higher levels, can't help but step in. Foreign bank trader says "we can't look only to the BOK's (suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) now. You see that a fall is limited during today's session and find the next day that it slides further despite such (suspected) BOK action." Adds pair likely to end just above 1,140 level vs 1,143.65 now, 1,146.3 yesterday. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0111 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold, silver both hovering just below overnight high levels in early Asia trade after WSJ article Tuesday indicated U.S. Federal Reserve was considering open-ended commitment to Quantitative Easing. Though plan being considered was described as "small scale approach" by Fed sources, article indicated that at around $100 billion/month it could get close to financing entire U.S. fiscal deficit for 2011 -- hardly likely to be considered small scale by gold bulls who are buying the metal as hedge against fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness. "The final quarter of the year threatens to be a torrid one for sterling, the euro, and the dollar," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Spot gold at $1,308.20/oz, down 80 cents and just off record of $1,310.15; spot silver at $21.76/oz, up 2 cents and just off 30-year high of $21.77. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may move lower as investors likely to take profit following recent gains. Benchmark PSEi may move towards 4100 support after rising 0.03% yesterday to end at 4123.95. "The market is in a corrective phase, we've been at an all-time high this week and a potential correction is in the air," says SB Equities research head Erwin Balita. Next resistance pegged at 4130. Still, some investors may take interest in infrastructure-related issues as President Aquino garnered $2.4 billion in fresh investments from recent U.S. trip. (rhea.sandique-carlos@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ tankan shows big manufacturers expect USD/JPY to average 89.66 this fiscal year, well above current rate at 83.98. "The higher assumed rate suggests a likely impact on business profits in the future if the exchange rate stays lower," says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . "There's a big difference between the big manufacturers' forecast around Y90, and the current rate around Y84." Analysts say Japanese government, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for first time in more than six years earlier this month, may face increasing pressure to do so again if persistent yen strength drags on corporate profits. In sign that USD/JPY remains very far from preferred rate for many manufacturers, \uc2 Suzuki Motor Corp\uc2 . Chairman and President \uc2 Osamu Suzuki\uc2  recently said having USD/JPY at 95 would be good for keeping Japanese manufacturers globally competitive. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY tad higher on buying by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers, also by non-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds which concerned about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk as pair around its lowest level since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into market on September 15, says senior dealer at major fund in Tokyo. But upside limited as many other investors willing to sell USD as U.S. data overnight not positive, prompting them to price in further Fed easing ahead. Pair last 84.04, may rise to 84.15. Adds JPY crosses rising due to higher Nikkei, while BOJ tankan gives few cues to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  players. EUR/JPY last 114.15, may rise to 114.30; AUD/JPY last 81.31, may rise to 81.50; NZD/JPY last 62.10, may rise to 62.30. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may stay in tight range as DJIA's modest rise unlikely to provide much support to local market, says Francis Lun at Fulbright. "Investors are likely to remain on the sidelines, as the current level of the Hang Seng Index doesn't look particularly attractive (HSI +7.7% month-to-yesterday)." Tips index to mostly tread 22,100-22,200 range today. Land auction of two small sites today may not provide much indication of overall sentiment for property market, as this likely to reflect developers' wariness on investments in non-prime sites (Fanling, Chai Wan). HSI ended down 1.0% at 22,109.95 Tuesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to Tuesday's high at 84.35 as Asian share prices increase, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 , suggesting stock market rises indicate more upbeat view of global economic growth. Pair hit intraday high at 84.09, last at 83.91. Nikkei +0.9% at 9576.25 on better-than-expected BOJ quarterly tankan business sentiment survey. Says EUR/JPY may rise to 114.50 as cross breaks key level at 114.00 earlier, potentially triggering buying stop orders. Cross last 113.97. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to continue falling to new low under current system today following another drop of USD vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, but PBOC could support USD/CNY in market; USD/CNY fixing expected to set near 6.7000 vs 6.7051 yesterday; tipped to trade 6.6880-6.6950 band for session vs 6.6906 at yesterday's close. "Given the euro's unabated rise against the dollar, people think the yuan will have further room to rise in the near term," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank; "but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar if it falls below CNY6.6900" Adds USD bids in anonymous automatic price matching system late yesterday suspected to be PBOC's attempt to bring USD/CNY above 6.6900 before close. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 09-28-10 2131ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM