\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Tankan Assumed  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Rate Suggests Pain Ahead -RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 03:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ tankan shows big manufacturers expect USD/JPY to average 89.66 this fiscal year, well above current rate at 83.98. "The higher assumed rate suggests a likely impact on business profits in the future if the exchange rate stays lower," says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . "There's a big difference between the big manufacturers' forecast around Y90, and the current rate around Y84." Analysts say Japanese government, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for first time in more than six years earlier this month, may face increasing pressure to do so again if persistent yen strength drags on corporate profits. In sign that USD/JPY remains very far from preferred rate for many manufacturers, \uc2 Suzuki Motor Corp\uc2 . Chairman and President \uc2 Osamu Suzuki\uc2  recently said having USD/JPY at 95 would be good for keeping Japanese manufacturers globally competitive. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY tad higher on buying by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  importers, also by non-\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds which concerned about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk as pair around its lowest level since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into market on September 15, says senior dealer at major fund in Tokyo. But upside limited as many other investors willing to sell USD as U.S. data overnight not positive, prompting them to price in further Fed easing ahead. Pair last 84.04, may rise to 84.15. Adds JPY crosses rising due to higher Nikkei, while BOJ tankan gives few cues to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  players. EUR/JPY last 114.15, may rise to 114.30; AUD/JPY last 81.31, may rise to 81.50; NZD/JPY last 62.10, may rise to 62.30. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0101 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may stay in tight range as DJIA's modest rise unlikely to provide much support to local market, says Francis Lun at Fulbright. "Investors are likely to remain on the sidelines, as the current level of the Hang Seng Index doesn't look particularly attractive (HSI +7.7% month-to-yesterday)." Tips index to mostly tread 22,100-22,200 range today. Land auction of two small sites today may not provide much indication of overall sentiment for property market, as this likely to reflect developers' wariness on investments in non-prime sites (Fanling, Chai Wan). HSI ended down 1.0% at 22,109.95 Tuesday. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may rise to Tuesday's high at 84.35 as Asian share prices increase, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 , suggesting stock market rises indicate more upbeat view of global economic growth. Pair hit intraday high at 84.09, last at 83.91. Nikkei +0.9% at 9576.25 on better-than-expected BOJ quarterly tankan business sentiment survey. Says EUR/JPY may rise to 114.50 as cross breaks key level at 114.00 earlier, potentially triggering buying stop orders. Cross last 113.97. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to continue falling to new low under current system today following another drop of USD vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, but PBOC could support USD/CNY in market; USD/CNY fixing expected to set near 6.7000 vs 6.7051 yesterday; tipped to trade 6.6880-6.6950 band for session vs 6.6906 at yesterday's close. "Given the euro's unabated rise against the dollar, people think the yuan will have further room to rise in the near term," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank; "but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the dollar if it falls below CNY6.6900" Adds USD bids in anonymous automatic price matching system late yesterday suspected to be PBOC's attempt to bring USD/CNY above 6.6900 before close. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures may rise to 143.40 vs last up 0.15 at 143.20, as weaker-than-expected outlook in BOJ tankan increases expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease monetary policy further at its October meeting, says RuiXue Xu, debt markets strategist at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . "This tankan suggests that the BOJ will likely move next week." Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may expand its six-month fixed-rate liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would support JGBs. Says benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.93% later in Tokyo trade vs last down 1.5 bps at 0.945%. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to consolidate in tight 8150-8250 range after finishing flat session at 8189.44 yesterday, as investors mostly stay on sidelines ahead of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy meeting tomorrow, says Capital Securities assistant VP Diana Wu. Notes "people are all eyeing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy meeting," for possible policy rate hike, likely further measures to curb overheating housing market. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0046 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore government bonds likely to rise tracking rally in Treasurys on weaker U.S. consumer confidence, manufacturing data but gains likely limited by stronger local dollar, trader at international bank says. "The sentiment in the U.S has dipped, increasing demand for government bonds as expectations of Fed taking steps to boost the economy are rising. That should lift Singapore government bonds too," trader says, tips yields down 2-3 bps. Yesterday, 2-year Singapore government bond yield +1 bp to 0.41%, 10-year unchanged at 2.02% (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] Outcome of today's HK land auction of 2 sites may not provide much indication of overall sentiment for property market, as this likely to reflect developers' wariness on investments in non-prime sites: Fanling in New Territories while Chai Wan site, though located on HK island, next to a cemetery which can't be developed into luxury homes. Based on Dow Jones poll, Fanling site expected to be sold at average HK$374 million, Chai Wan at HK$716 million. Among HK property developers, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) ends down 1.2% at HK$114.20 yesterday, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) down 1.2% at HK$128.90, Henderson Land (0012.HK) down 2.0% at HK$53.75. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 edges up 0.6% to 4695.5 after Tankan report and ahead of the China HSBC Manufacturing PMI at 0230 GMT, with IG Markets saying a stronger-than-expected reading from data for the world's second-largest economy likely to see mining stocks bid higher. Bellwethers BHP +1.1% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  +1.2% in morning trade. "Lower commodity prices yesterday prompted some profit taking but we're now seeing a bit of a scramble to put positions back on" says Peter Copeland, senior institutional trader at BBY. \uc2 Sigma Pharmaceuticals\uc2  flat after announcing 1H net loss of A$218.5 million. \uc2 Telstra\uc2  +1.5% after CEO \uc2 David Thodey\uc2  says company likely to reach agreement on NBN by Nov. 19. ASX shares -1% as CEO Robert Elstone says he still plans to step down around the time his contract expires in July next year. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD hits new record low at 1.3162 overnight, bounces back in early Asian trade, last 1.3178 vs 1.3228 late Asia yesterday; trader at international bank says pair may rise above 1.3200 if MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The tolerance band of MAS has shifted following gains in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the basket which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracks. The U.S. dollar is weakening but the MAS won't tolerate a very steep fall in the greenback," trader says; tips 1.3170-1.3230 range. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may consolidate but biased down as investors retreat to sidelines on widespread caution ahead of long holiday starting Friday. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to test support at 2600, after ending down 0.6% at 2611.35 yesterday. "The holiday factor dominates the market today and tomorrow, as investors are worried about Beijing coming out with policy shifts during the break, as well as movements in global markets while China is closed," says Li Xiaoxuan at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Adds some profit taking expected ahead of holidays for stocks that have outperformed recently. Shenzhen index ends down 0.6% at 1163.32. (jean.yung@dowjones.com) [ 09-28-10 2108ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100929e69t0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
