\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Edge Lower\b0\par\par\uc2 2,684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2010\par 00:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10835.28  -22.86  -0.21% \par Nasdaq     2376.56   -3.03  -0.13% \par S&P 500    1144.73   -2.97  -0.26% \par FTSE 100   5569.27   -9.17  -0.16% \par Xetra DAX  6246.92  -29.17  -0.46% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  99 28/32 flat  0.438% flat \par 5Y Tsy  99 27/32 -4/32 1.282% +2.3BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 3/32 -8/32 2.497% +2.8BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.70-72     -0.19% \par Range         84.07-83.51 \par EUR/USD       1.3626-28    +0.29% \par Range         1.3647-1.3567 \par AUD/USD       0.9692-95    +0.19% \par Range         0.9731-0.9659 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.80-11.20% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    12.29-12.61% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    14.15-14.45% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,310.30   +$2.00 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,306.10-$1,314.80 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $77.86   +$1.68 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Stocks stumbled modestly as declines in the more economically sensitive materials and financial sectors offset a rise in oil prices and energy stocks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average lost 22.86 points, or 0.21%, to 10835.28, while the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index slipped 2.97 points to 1144.73 and the Nasdaq Composite dropped 3.03 points to 2376.56. With just one day left in the month, all three indexes have posted strong returns, and remain on pace for the best September in decades. The Nasdaq, in particular, is 12.4% higher on the month.\par \par \uc2 "There haven't been any thematic changes in the state of the economy, and I think that's what was holding us back today," said David Kelly, chief market strategist for \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  Funds, adding that investors remained concerned about a raft of remaining overhangs, from upcoming elections and tax policies to Treasury buying by the Federal Reserve. "That's the problem: all this uncertainty about Washington drags on the economy and on the stock market."\par \par \uc2 Still, Kelly said September's strong gains--including the Dow's 8.1% advance--reflected a belief that double dip fears were "overblown."\par \par \uc2 The materials sector was the biggest drag on the market Wednesday, with DuPont, \uc2 Dow Chemical\uc2  and \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  falling 2.5%, 1.7% and 1.1% respectively, amid the lingering uncertainties.\par \par \uc2 Financial stocks were also weak as the government made steps towards exiting its investments in \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and \uc2 American International Group\uc2 , made during the height of the financial crisis. Shares of \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  and AIG pared strong intraday gains to finish 1% and 0.4% higher respectively. But \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  lost 2.2% and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  shed 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 The Dow was able to mount a midday rally after the Department of Energy said crude-oil stockpiles fell by 475,000 barrels to 357.9 million barrels, more than an expected 300,000-barrel decline. The steep drop, which follows a summer of unusually high levels of stockpiling, helped oil prices jump briefly over $78 a barrel after falling as low as $75.60 a barrel earlier in the morning.\par \par \uc2 Energy stocks benefited from oil's climb, as \uc2 Sunoco\uc2  rose 3.4%, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  added 0.5% and BP rose 1.8% after incoming Chief Executive Officer Bob Dudley said he would restructure BP's upstream business and create a new safety division, with the power to audit any part of the company' \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  finished lower, off 0.8%.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended flat Wednesday, as gains by metals companies because of rising commodity prices offset concerns about further tightening measures in the property market.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely trade around 2600 Thursday, ahead of China's National Day holiday Friday through Oct. 7.\par \par \uc2 "Commodities have been leading gains this week as the softening dollar has lent support to metals prices, but uncertainty about Beijing's next move to cap housing prices won't let the Shanghai index rise much," said Zhang Xiang, an analyst at \uc2 Guodu Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "As investors decide whether to hold stocks or cash over the long holiday, they're looking to overseas markets for cues," said Shen Yang, an analyst at Orient Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Wednesday as better-than-expected business sentiment lifted such exporters as \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  despite the yen's rise, helping offset dilution fears stemming from capital raising reports on \uc2 Tokyo Electric Power\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Market analysts say that stock price rallies will likely be short-lived on future business sentiment concerns, but that the current mood may stand supported to some extent.\par \par \uc2 "Investors are not willing to take large positions now as they expect some kind of further action from the government," said Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. He noted that while the dollar trades below Y84, there are hopes for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and thus exporters may not fall significantly for the time being.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei may be supported around 9500, he added.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures rallied to a seven-week high in U.S. trading Wednesday after a government report showed U.S. oil and fuel inventories dropped.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures settled at a new record above $1,300 for the second-consecutive session Wednesday as expectations of more U.S. stimulus money boosted the metal as a perceived dollar and inflation hedge.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed higher on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday, supported by a weaker U.S. dollar and upbeat Chinese manufacturing data.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar declined Wednesday as poor prospects for the U.S. economy continued to feed speculation that the Federal Reserve will be forced to introduce further programs to stimulate economic activity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 More monetary easing is seen as negative for the dollar because U.S. interest rates would remain low for a longer period, thus making dollar-denominated assets less attractive to investors. A stream of disappointing U.S. data has supported the scenario under which the Fed will embark on a second round of quantitative easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro, having solidly breached $1.36, now stands at its highest point since April. The dollar has fallen every day this week, and is off roughly 7.5% since Sept. 13 against the euro, according to Brian Dolan, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com in Bedminster, N.J.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , fell Wednesday to its lowest level since January.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also hit Y83.50, its lowest level since the Japanese government's massive Y2 trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sept. 15, while the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has fallen against the Swiss franc to levels last seen in March 2008, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A short-term viewpoint is dominating the market now," said Marc Chandler, global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Right now, there are almost no other issues about which investors care besides the possibility of an increasingly cheap dollar. Issues relating to the euro-zone debt crisis have been cast aside for now, Chandler said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.3630 from $1.3590 from late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.70 from Y83.89, while the euro was at Y114.07 from Y114.00. The U.K. pound was at $1.5787 from $1.5802. The dollar was at CHF0.9778 from CHF0.9760. The ICE Dollar Index was at 78.759 from 78.904.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It isn't only the euro that will continue to gain from increased risk taking thanks to a cheap dollar, said Beat Siegenthaler, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Zurich.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Swiss franc will be a major beneficiary of any additional" asset purchases considered quantitative easing, he said. "Together with gold, it is the ultimate safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a world that fears \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debasement and competitive devaluation."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar dropped Wednesday in Tokyo to its lowest levels since Japan's Sept. 15 foray into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, the country's first in more than six years. Investors remained on high alert for signs of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem yen strength. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economy minister, Banri Kaieda, said Wednesday that the government remains ready to take "decisive" action to stop the sharp appreciation of the yen, but that such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was a "temporary measure."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "was an emergency escape, a temporary measure," Kaieda said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys fell Wednesday for the first time this week as comments from Federal Reserve officials tempered bets that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will step up government debt purchases.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed Bank of Boston President Eric Rosengren, who votes on monetary policy this year, said that further monetary stimulus hinges on upcoming data. Charles Plosser, the president of the Philadelphia Federal Reserve Bank who isn't a voter this year, said he opposes a second round of Treasury purchases of any size, known as quantitative easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remarks pushed the bond market to give up a brief rally made on a strong $29 billion sale in seven-year notes. Some investors cashed out of the bond market following its rally in the previous two sessions that had sent the benchmark 10-year note's yield to near a 20-month low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further quantitative-easing measures "aren't a slam dunk as the bond market is thinking," said Thomas Roth, executive director in the U.S. government bond trading group at Mitsubishi UFJ Securities (USA) Inc. in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1955 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was down 8/32 to yield 2.497%, and the 30-year bond was down 12/32 to yield 3.681%. The seven-year note was down 5/32 to yield 1.870%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year note's yield, a benchmark for consumer and corporate borrowings, touched 2.448% Tuesday, the weakest level since this year's trough of 2.418% on Aug. 25, which itself is the lowest level since January 2009. [ 29-09-10 2204GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The seven-year notes were sold at a yield of 1.890%, the lowest since the maturity was reintroduced in February 2009 after a 17-year hiatus. The auction Wednesday also completed a record $2.3 trillion supply of Treasury notes and bonds, including inflation-linked securities, for the fiscal year ending Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Treasury certainly went three-for-three this week," said Dan Greenhaus, chief economic and bond strategist at Miller, Tabak & Co. in New York. "Despite record-low yields, those participating in the auctions remain focused on the stability of the Treasury market and, for whatever reason, remain of the belief that yields at current levels are attractive."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended slightly higher Wednesday as local stocks reversed earlier gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash-rich investors are increasing bond positions for coupon earnings during the week-long National Day holiday which starts Friday, a Shenzhen-based analyst with a local insurer said, tipping the Shanghai exchange government bond index in a narrow 126.5-126.6 band Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds rose Wednesday as worsening sentiment about the economy shown in key Bank of Japan data fueled the view that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will ease policy further next week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The tankan suggests the BOJ will likely move next week," said RuiXue Xu, a rates strategist at RBS Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors are focusing on what easing steps the BOJ may take. If it increases buying of JGBs, the sovereign bond's benchmark yield should fall sharply, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If we find the BOJ's balance sheet will expand, it would be no wonder for the 10-year yield to hit a level below 0.900%," said Naoki Tsuchiyama, a market economist at Mizuho Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts added that JGBs will likely remain strong for the time being, as Japan's economic momentum is likely to lose steam due to the end of government stimulus measures and weakening overseas demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are just so many bad factors for the economy going into the second half of fiscal 2010," said Naomi Hasegawa, a senior strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Thursday, Japan's industrial output is likely to have risen 1.1% on the month in August, the first rise in three months, according to a Dow Jones, Nikkei poll of economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One-off factors such as unusually hot weather--which lifted demand for air-conditioners--were probably behind August's rebound, Dai-Ichi Life Research Institute chief economist Toshihiro Nagahama said. Even if the forecast is met, however, the outlook for Japan's export-led recovery will remain murky, as "overseas demand seems to be slowing sharply," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par BP's E&P Boss Steps Down In Dudley Shakeup \par Prudential Set To Buy Two AIG Units For $4.8B \par Workers Strike Over Europe Austerity Plans \par Dollar's Status As Reserve \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Stable \par Chase Suspends Foreclosures \par Business Groups Sue SEC Over Proxy Access \par Bernanke Says Dodd-Frank Act Addresses Critical Gaps \par BHP, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  May Revise Australian Venture \par Treasury Brainard Urges China To Boost Domestic Demand \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fedex\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Increase Shipping Rates \par Republicans End Net Neutrality Bill Negotiations \par WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Selling Down Stake In Industrial & Commerical Bank Of China \par US, China Eye Restart Of Military Relations \par US Ex-Im Bank Head: Can't Compete Vs China's Trade-Distorting Aid \par S Korea Manufacturers' October Outlook Index Lowest In 8 Months \par NZ's Adjusted August Dwelli