\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Posts Limited Losses On Easing Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 11:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Continued speculation about more monetary easing in the U.S. kept the dollar on the back foot Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 However, most of its losses were limited.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about the euro zone's sovereign debt problems kept a lid on the euro's gains against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and continued fears about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the dollar from falling too close to Y83.00.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro failed to push ahead against the dollar and how the dollar failed to fall much against the yen:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4646"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4646}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4647"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4647}}}\par \par \uc2 Investors also appeared reluctant to push major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  too far ahead of speeches by a long list of both U.S. Federal Reserve officials and members of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy committee.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, this didn't stop the dollar from being pushed lower after the U.S. Conference Board reported that consumer confidence had sunk even lower than expected and that S&P/CaseShiller reported that house prices had fallen back a little.\par \par \uc2 This encouraged another round of speculation that the Fed will resort to further quantitative easing, even if it is more limited in scope that the previous QE exercise last year.\par \par \uc2 The disappointing data from the U.S. contrasted with a better than expected improvement in Japanese business sentiment with the latest Tankan quarterly survey diffusion index rising all the way to 8 from 1. The market had been looking for a more modest gain to 6.\par \par \uc2 China also contributed to rise in general market sentiment, with \uc2 HSBC\uc2  reporting that its purchasing managers' index for the country's manufacturing sector rose to a five-month high.\par \par \uc2 These factors helped to push the euro over $1.36 for the first time in five months at the start of the European trading day. However, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon slipped back as euro-zone sovereign-debt yields continued to tick higher in several instances, close to recent record highs.\par \par \uc2 These signs of investor concern fly in the face of suggestions that the ECB will be able to unwind its unconventional monetary easing by the end of the year as originally planned.\par \par \uc2 Speeches by several ECB members later in the day are likely to be scrutinized for any sign of their confidence in the euro zone recovery.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also came under pressure against the yen. However, investors showed little appetite for pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much closer to Y83, given that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  launched its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise two weeks ago just below that level.\par \par \uc2 Also, while the main diffusion index may have been positive, the forward looking indicators of the Tankan survey point to a downturn.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the dollar fell to Y83.62 from Y83.89 late on Tuesday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The euro slipped to $1.3582 from $1.3590, falling back from an earlier high of $1.3639. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell, however, to Y113.60 from Y114.00.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was marginally better at CHF0.9773 from CHF0.9760 while the pound rose to $1.5829 from $1.5802.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 29-09-10 0910GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100929e69t000ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
