\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: BOJ Sep Tankan Improves For 6th Straight Quarter\b0\par\par\uc2 1,747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 01:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ September tankan's main diffusion index measuring large manufacturers' business sentiment comes in at 8, up from 1 in June, marking sixth consecutive quarter of improvement; result better than market expectations for reading of 6. DI for large non-manufacturers at 2, up from minus 5 three months ago, vs economists' median expectations for minus 2. Though survey results closely studied by BOJ in formulating policy, better-than-expected figures alone not expected to brush away market expectations for additional easing by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with concerns over impact on economy of yen's rise, uncertainty over overseas economic outlook. Looking ahead, large manufacturers DI expected to deteriorate to minus 1 in December, while index for large non-manufacturers expected to be at minus 2. Meanwhile, big manufacturers, non-manufacturers plan to raise business investment by combined 2.4% in fiscal year that began April, lower than economists' forecast for 4.0% rise. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] Hopes for more measures by Fed to help shore up sluggish U.S. economy may lift Singapore shares after they drive up U.S. stocks overnight, although any gains likely to be modest pending any concrete initiatives. Resistance for STI, down 0.5% at 3097.35 yesterday, expected at year-to-date high of 3125, while last week's 3069 low tipped as support. "There's no strong lead from Wall Street. But with tomorrow being the end of the quarter, there might be some buying on dips to dress up portfolios," says trader at local brokerage. ST Engineering (S63.SG) may be in focus as company secures contract to procure jets, ground-based training system worth combined S$543 million for Singapore's Air Force; shares down 0.6% at S$3.31 yesterday. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2353 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold last $1,308.70/oz, up $1.10 early in Asia, not far off last night's record high at $1,310.15/oz, hit after powering up from low of $1,281.50, in a move that ScotiaMocatta says sparked by weak U.S. consumer confidence numbers that energized fund buying. Technically, bank says bullish close yesterday keeps $1,369 target in sight, with uptrend channel parameters now $1,268 and $1,308; says should gold break convincingly up through top of channel a measured move would be $40 to $1,348. Meanwhile, \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says it's concerned by some technical signals, such as relative strength indicator at extreme levels, and while it remains concerned by these is "more impressed by the strength of the uptrend and the fact that momentum remains with the rally." BarCap says so long as gold holds above $1,258/$1,262, it remains positive, targeting $1,350/$1,375 area as seasonal charts are supportive. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2353 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate for now after settling down 34 cents yesterday at $76.18/bbl, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; but inside-day range pattern & spinning-top candlestick pattern reflecting indecision of traders completed yesterday. Support at $75.52 (Monday's low); breach would expose downside to $74.66 (Friday's low), then $73.58 (Thursday's low) and $72.75 (Sept. 17 reaction low). Resistance at $77.17 (Monday's high); breach would turn near-term outlook positive; targeting $77.53 (200-day moving average), then $78.04 (Sept. 13 reaction high), $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 Aug. 4-Aug. 25 decline) and psychological $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). November crude last up 18 cents at $76.36/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW tipped to extend losses into 4th session today from 1,146.3 late yesterday in Seoul as greenback losing value rapidly amid high expectations that U.S. Fed may introduce additional stimulus to boost economy, say market participants. "It's inevitable the pair will get a hit from the greenback's fast fall. The dollar's slide will likely continue until the U.S. (midterm) election in (early) November," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji; notes, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities, ahead of G20 summit in Seoul in November, may attempt to smoothen pace of KRW's run-up through "indirect measures" such as mobilizing state-rum firms in need of USD to buy now. Local bank trader says exporters could rush in today, tomorrow to exchange into KRW at quarter-end. Initial support pegged at 1,140 today. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2347 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely to open higher after positive lead from Wall Street. IG Markets is calling for 0.4% rise in index to 4691 vs last 4669.8. "With all U.S. sectors finishing in positive territory, we're likely to see fairly broad based gains locally," says IG Markets in report. Consumer staples, energy companies in particular could see some buying support after these U.S. sectors were top performers, with Nymex crude oil futures trading flat to slightly higher. Higher base metals prices likely to help miners including BHP (BHP.AU), \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU). And gold miners to be helped by higher gold price. \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) to be in focus after says can comfortably fund 28 cent dividend for FY11--if board chooses to do so. But IG Markets strategist Cameron Peacock says there remains speculation whether dividend sustainable heading into FY12 given continued erosion of capital base. Investors will keep eye on ASX Ltd. (ASX.AU) AGM from 0000 GMT for more details after it said yesterday it had talks with exchange groups. Asia data to watch today include Japan's Tankan Manufacturing index at 2350 GMT ahead of the open, and China HSBC Manufacturing PMI at 0230 GMT, with IG Markets saying a stronger-than-expected reading from the China data would likely see mining stocks bid higher. (rebecca.thurlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2339 GMT [Dow Jones]Kospi may rise to track positive U.S. leads amid hopes that weaker-than-expected U.S. data will lead to additional Fed steps to support economy. But Mirae Asset Securities analyst Lee Jin-woo says upward momentum could slow in absence of strong foreign buying or another positive news event; "There's already some caution with the index having risen quite a bit in recent sessions, so we may see limited upward move ahead of the key 1900 level." Tips 1840-1870 range for day. Says market may shift away from recent outperformers like autos, shipbuilders into relatively cheaper sectors like securities, construction plays; beneficiaries may include \uc2 Samsung Securities\uc2  (016360.SE), Woori Investment (005940.SE), GS Engineering (006360.SE) as it gathers itself for next upward push. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] Strength in annual trade balance that drove 12-month ended August surplus of NZ$866 million reflects extent of export led recovery in NZ to date, says ASB economist Jane Turner; "We expect the export sector to remain a key driver of growth over the next year." Says, however, August exports lower than expected, suggesting momentum for exports may be fading although export prices should continue to support export revenues even if volumes ease; "Indeed recent anecdotes suggest demand for forestry has eased slightly." Adds export commodity prices remain strong as dairy prices have held up at very high levels. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares +0.2% at 3236.34; "the market is drifting with a slight positive bias," says Macquarie Equities broker Brad Gordon. Says lack of corporate drivers keeping most investors sidelined, although positive close on Wall Street helping sentiment slightly. \uc2 Hallenstein Glasson\uc2  (HLG.NZ) still benefiting from strong FY result earlier in week, +0.9% at NZ$4.43. Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) ex-dividend, down 2.0% to NZ$8.42. Says investors will now begin to focus on upcoming reporting season in U.S. for cues. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 83.70 if BOJ's tankan survey of business confidence, due 2350 GMT, stronger than expected, says senior dealer at Japan trust bank; but pair unlikely to fall past that as investors cautious about potential yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. Says if tankan weaker than expected, pair may rise to 84.30, but would be capped by exporters' USD sales. Headline diffusion index for tankan likely 6 in September vs 1 in previous survey, according to Dow Jones poll of economists. USD/JPY last 83.88. Says EUR/USD biased up due to broad USD weakness, but investors may take profits in Asian session. EUR/USD may trade in 1.3530-1.3600 range vs 1.3569 last. Says if pair breaks 1.3600, that would trigger stop-loss buy orders above that level which could drive pair to 1.3650. EUR/JPY biased up, may trade in 112.90-114.80 range vs 113.81 last. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures may rise to 143.30 vs Tuesday close at 143.05, as mounting speculation BOJ may ease ahead likely to outweigh caution over possibility of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen gains, says Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities. Adds market has already priced in expectations that BOJ quarterly tankan sentiment survey, due at 2350 GMT, will show slower improvement in headline diffusion index, expectations for deterioration in sentiment ahead. Still, says if results meet expectations, "that will inevitably limit expectations for any rises in equities, which would support the bond market." Says benchmark 10-year yield may fall to 0.945% vs 0.955% Tuesday.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 300,000 Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 3rd consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 13.0 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 12.7 million shares. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-28-10 1958ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
