\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Posts Modest Rebound On Dollar; Fed Mulled\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 793 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 14:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)-- The euro gained modestly against the dollar Tuesday, shaking off rumbling of an imminent downgrade of Spanish government debt, as a policy official signaled the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on the road to normalizing monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  executive board member Juergen Stark said Tuesday the ECB was in the process of phasing out non-standard stimulus measures put in place during the financial crisis, confirming that some measures soon to mature won't be extended.\par \par \uc2 His positive euro-zone comments came on the heels of a successful auction of Spanish government debt, which also boosted confidence in the region after investors earlier speculated \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investor Service could be on the verge of downgrading Spanish debt.\par \par \uc2 "We're all very aware that there are still ongoing sovereign debt issues in Europe," said Jane Foley, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  in London."This is really making for a pretty rocky ride," she said of the euro's volatile trading Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The euro's modest gains came after the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had earlier declined against the dollar on a Wall Street Journal report a Federal Reserve plan to kickstart the flagging U.S. economy could offer less shock and awe than had been originally expected.\par \par \uc2 A smaller, more targeted round of asset purchases--dubbed quantitative easing--would undergird a dollar that has been weighed down by expectations the Fed stimulus program would be of massive, crisis proportions--something that is not necessary when the U.S. economy is growing, albeit slowly, said \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 's Foley.\par \par \uc2 Still, though the dollar found some support with the late Monday release of the article, "as long as the Fed have signaled that the door to QE is open, the likelihood is the dollar will retain a softer bias," Foley said.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday morning, the euro was at $1.3461 from $1.3444 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y84.12 from Y84.25, while the euro was at Y113.23 from Y113.25. The U.K. pound was at $1.5831 from $1.5826. The dollar was at CHF0.9861 from CHF0.9857.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 79.435 from 79.502.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the dollar fell to its lowest level against the yen since the massive mid-September Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen strength, falling to Y84.05 before regaining some of its poise.\par \par \uc2 Investors are on alert for Japan returning to the market to prop up the dollar, especially as the Sept. 30 half-way point for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fiscal year looms.\par \par \uc2 "Now that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has begun, we believe that it will be the market's nature to test," the Japanese government, pushing yen higher, said Camilla Sutton, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto. But, "officials will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sending a clear message, she said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, investors are zeroed in on the 9 a.m. Case-Shiller U.S. home prices data and the 10 a.m. U.S. consumer confidence figures.\par \par \uc2 If they paint a picture the U.S. economy is improving, but at a slow place, the U.S. dollar could come under pressure under renewed speculation the Fed might have a better case to engage in another round of QE, Foley said.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   Canada Morning \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar extended its losses against the greenback overnight, underperforming major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  peers in subdued trading early Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The decline comes as investors fret about the impact in Canada from a U.S. economic slowdown and see the Canadian dollar unable to sustain a break of the C$1.0200 level against the U.S. dollar. "We seem to hit a bit of a brick wall every time we get down to that level," said Steve Butler, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Scotia Capital in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar was at C$1.0329 early Tuesday, from C$1.0285 late Monday, according to CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  Governor Mark Carney's interview Friday on a U.S. business news network has been nagging at the market. Carney, sounding dovish on interest rates in Canada, "set the tone" for investors, Butler said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the interview, Carney said weakness in the U.S. economy is of concern for its northerly neighbor. And if the U.S. Federal Reserve embarks on a new round of quantitative easing, Canada would deal with the consequences.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 bradley.davis@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Karen Johnson in Toronto contributed to this article.) [ 09-28-10 0841ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0008l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
