\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei Off 0.6%; Tankan Awaited, BOJ Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 06:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0438 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.6% at 9541.86. Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities, says index trading essentially flat when factoring in ex-dividend selling effect (estimated to drag Nikkei index by around 60 points), citing relative stability in USD/JPY (now 84.24) on speculation of more BOJ monetary easing. "If the business sentiment outlook (for October-December period) turns pessimistic, that will result in (further) calls for more BOJ easing," he says, referring to quarterly BOJ tankan survey result due Wednesday. 28/33 Topix subindexes lower, with chip-linked shares underperforming; TDK (6762.TO) down 3.0% at Y4,780, Kyocera (6971.TO) down 1.7% at Y8,040. Exporters generally lower; \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 0.7% at Y2,604, \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) down 0.5% at Y3,000. \uc2 Nippon Steel\uc2  (5401.TO) up 1.8% at Y290, \uc2 JFE Holdings\uc2  (5411.TO) up 0.5% at Y2,649 on Posco's pricing plan and bullish GS report. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end steady despite foreign fund inflows as investors take profit after recent gains; benchmark PSEi flat at 4123.95. Market breadth negative, with losers beating gainers 78 to 60. "We've seen this pattern in recent days, where the market closes higher after an early bout with profit-taking. Foreign fund inflow is still evident. We'll probably see more of this in the coming days," says Jomar Lacson, research head at Campos, Lanuza & Co. PSEi tipped to move within 4050-4150 range Wednesday. Among gainers, Energy Development (EDC.PH) +2.4% to PHP6.48, \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH) +3.4% to PHP21. Losers are First Gen (FGEN.PH), down 1.6% to PHP13.66, PLDT (TEL.PH), down 0.1% to PHP2,620. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0436 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares end +1.1% at 455.13, with local investors buying after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement yesterday, that it will amend regulations to allow banks to mobilize more funds for lending from October. "Players share the view that though benchmark rate remains at 8% next month, banks will soon have to cut down interest rates to boost lending as many companies are reluctant to take more loans because of high borrowing costs," says Vietcombank fund management analyst. Tips key index to test 460 Wednesday. Today's volume average at 39 million shares valued at VND1.05 trillion. Among most active, \uc2 Sacombank\uc2  (STB.VH) ends +1.2% at VND17,000, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 0.6% at VND32,200, Petroland (PTL.VH) +2.8% at VND18,400. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold's bull run unlikely to continue, says Guy Verberne, investment strategist at ABN AMRO. "I think that the rise of gold is based on an irrational fear of inflation and an excessive pessimism over the global economic recovery," he says; adds, bond yields likely to start edging higher ahead of expected U.S. Federal Reserve raising of interest rates in 2012, this likely to take shine off gold, which does not yield any interest. "As soon as unemployment in the U.S. starts to come down it won't take the Fed long to hike rates," he says; notes that unlike the 1960s, 1970s when Fed failed to control inflation, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was now independent and would not risk inflationary period like late 1970s. Spot gold at $1,291.50/oz, down $3.10 since Monday's NY close. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 flat in afternoon trade at 4675.8, with gains in key banking stocks helping to offset weakness in heavyweight resource stocks. "That's a pretty good result considering the weaker lead from overnight and recent gains," says Helen Spencer, client adviser at Macquarie Private Wealth. "After recent gains, we are seeing some softness but certainly nothing of any conviction. Stocks that have run quite well over recent weeks are having a bit of a breather today," she said. Among those, \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) down 1.5% and ASX (ASX.AU) 1.9% lower, despite confirming that it's in talks with some other exchanges about possible tie ups. BHP (BHP.AU) down 0.3% while \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) up 0.9%. "There is more confidence in the market and that has been edging up in the past few months," Spencer says adding that there are reduced concerns that the U.S. will experience a double-dip recession. "The view is that it is a slow recovery but it is still a recovery. That has reduced the fear factor in the market," she says. "We've also had some quite positive data continue to come out of China, which is a good backdrop for global markets." (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays slightly up midday as market consolidates following relatively stable USD/CNY fixing (6.7051 vs 6.7098 yesterday). On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.6935 vs 6.6923 yesterday close; but Shenzhen-based trader at local bank says CNY demand may increase in afternoon as many expect CNY to continue appreciating near term, bank customers may need to exchange USD for CNY ahead of long National Day holiday starting Friday. "It's reasonable that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  guides the market to consolidate around the key 6.70 level after sharp yuan appreciation lately," says trader. "We think the yuan will continue to appreciate in the near term, but the pace of yuan gains has really been too fast, and it's possible yuan could give up some of the gains later on October." USD/CNY tipped in 6.6870-6.6955 range for session. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb JPY strength may be getting tacit support from U.S., which may "be considered a green light for Tokyo for now," says \uc2 UBS\uc2 . Cites reports that meeting of President Obama, PM Kan last week on sidelines of UN General Assembly did not touch on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Obama's meeting with Chinese Premier Wen was focused on CNY issues. But says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF, which controls decisions on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "will probably not want to test" U.S. tolerance too much, as China will use "a microscope to detect any signs of hypocrisy by the U.S. on the trade front." This means any further Japanese JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be aimed at tempering falls in USD/JPY, rather than pushing it up. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be very selective and the Japanese would want to maximise the effect of every yen spent." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR opens higher on weak local stocks, while EUR pares gains vs USD, says senior dealer at private bank. Pair last at 45.15 vs 45.01 late Monday. "The slip of the EUR/USD below 1.3500 and negative local stocks is supporting the pair above INR45.00." Notes, one bout of exporter selling above 45.20 spotted in recent sessions, so level will be closely watched. Says, with forwards expensive, importers only covering dollar needs for very near term. Tips 45-45.20 band for session. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex flat, last trading at 20,100.16 (off 0.1%), after earlier oscillating between 20,084.68-20,146.29, amid subdued regional markets, caution before September derivatives contracts expiry Thursday. "Its a tepid start. Barring some buying in news driven stocks the overall mood is cautious after the recent run up," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Angel Securities says the trend deciding level for day is 20,156; notes, if Sensex trades above this level during the first 30 minutes of trade then we may witness further rally up to 20,229, but if not, then it may correct down to 20,044-19,970 range. HDFC (500010.BY) last down 0.8% at INR722.05, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) down 0.7% at INR1,106.80. \uc2 Reliance Communications (532712.BY) \uc2 +2.4% at INR173.70 ahead of annual shareholders meet, Mahindra (500520.BY) +1.0% at INR700.65 on news it will raise prices of its vehicles by INR3,000-INR8,000 from Oct. 1. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0408 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF sells Y2.4311 trillion of 0.1% 2-year bonds with lowest price of 99.92 yielding 0.14%, slightly higher than market expectations of 99.91; bid-to-cover ratio improves to 5.75 from 4.75 at August auction, while tail at 0.000 vs 0.001 at last tender. Tender results were "better than expected" as there are no major factor for yield rises in front end of curve, and as investors' holdings of surplus cash still exceed amount of supply, says RBS Securities chief rates strategist Akito Fukunaga. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds open higher, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.67 vs 99.60 Monday close. "Bonds have opened higher on US Treasury cues, but I expect it to be a rangebound market. I don't think the limit hike for foreign funds will make much difference as they have been active only in the very short end segment (1-, 2-years)," adds dealer. Also, tight liquidity may weigh. Last week, government doubled foreign fund limit in government debt to $10 billion, but additional buying can only be in maturities of 5-year and above. RBI to sell INR40 billion of 7.17% 2015 bond, INR40 billion of 8.08% 2022 bond and INR30 billion of 8.26% 2027 paper Friday, in first auction of fiscal 2H. Dealer tips 10-year yield in 7.82%-7.87% band for session (7.85% currently). (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com) [ 09-28-10 0037ET ]\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD may target 1.3530 later in global day as investors expected to continue buying on mounting view that FOMC could take further easing steps ahead, while recent financial sector woes in Europe small in scale, confined to periphery, says Yuichiro Harada, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . After WSJ report that Fed officials considering open-ended, smaller purchases of Treasurys in contrast to large, finite buying conducted in 2009, says effect of such speculation is still negative for USD as it raises expectations for additional quantitative easing. "Yes, the eventual effect on the dollar would depend on the scale, but for now the mere talk of more quantitative easing may feed into dollar weakness." Meanwhile, says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  downgrade of state-owned \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  overnight not having any lasting negative effect on EUR due to relatively small scale of issue. EUR/USD last 1.3459, up from intraday low at 1.3427.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-28-10 0037ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
