\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: 2-Yr JGBs' Lowest Tender Price Eyed Around 99.91\b0\par\par\uc2 1,520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 05:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Lowest accepted price for 2-year JGBs for 0345 GMT auction result likely around 99.91, traders say. Latest issue carries coupon of 0.1%, unchanged from previous issue. But traders expect smooth auction as banks seek place to park surplus cash, and as increasing speculation for BOJ additional easing makes investors more comfortable buying policy-sensitive sector. Lead December JGB futures +0.25 at 143.08 at morning close. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0326 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.3% at 965.09 in robust trade as capital inflows continue; profit-taking after recent rally likely capping gains. Big cap banking plays buoyant on optimism over 3Q earnings, shrugging off lowering of certain transaction fees that will cost them hundreds of million bahts per year, effective 1Q next year. "Capital Inflows and anticipation of better bank third-quarter earnings are driving the market," says senior analyst with DBS Vickers Securities Arpaporn Sawaengpak. Resistance tipped at 970, support at 960-955 area. Among top actives, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) +2.0% at THB102.50, highest level since August 1997, while \uc2 Bank of Ayudhya\uc2  (BAY.TH) +0.4% at THB23.60, \uc2 True Corp\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +1.8% at THB4.66, \uc2 Thai Airways International\uc2  (THAI.TH) off 1.9% at THB38.50, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) +1.3% at THB114.50. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0325 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD shrugging off dour local economic news, as substantial support emanating from broadly weaker USD, improved investor sentiment and "rampaging Australian dollar," says Chris Hunter, dealer at Custom House, a \uc2 Western Union Company\uc2 . Pair last 0.7352 vs 0.7363 late yesterday; "The yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are certainly back in favor." Focus will remain offshore for direction although tomorrow's NZ trade data may garner some interest. Tips support to hold at 0.7320, resistance around 0.7370 with USD the main driver. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0323 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open tad lower by about 25-40 points, tracking losses in most regional markets, while investors expected to trim positions before monthly derivatives expiry Thursday, says dealer. Adds, index ended off highs yesterday, giving up more than 150 points from intraday peak, suggesting buying interest waning as valuations look over-stretched after about 12% rally so far this month. Still, Sensex likely to find strong support at 19,950-19,970. Strong liquidity on sustained foreign inflows may lend support. Foreign funds purchased provisional INR11.37 billion worth of local shares Monday vs INR13.29 billion Friday. Adds, software shares under pressure after INR hit fresh four-and-half month high against USD yesterday. Sensex ended +0.4% at 20,117.38 Monday. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0318 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +0.4% at 3483.960 in moderate volume, off 3458.603 earlier, as local institutional funds buy back shares amid hopes for strong 9-month corporate earnings, traders say; tip resistance at 3500. Trader at Erdhika Securities says "gains on the main index, however, look capped as foreign funds are net sellers so far"; foreigners net sell IDR47 billion worth of shares. Among gainers, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand\uc2  (CPIN.JK) +3.6% at IDR8,500, \uc2 Jasa Marga\uc2  (JSMR.JK) +3.2% at IDR3,125, while \uc2 Gudang Garam\uc2  (GGRM.JK) +2.6% at IDR50,100. But Astra (ASII.JK) down 0.3% at IDR57,850 on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0306 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open higher, in line with rebound in global dollar, may open around 45.07-45.10 vs 45.01 in late trade yesterday, says India-based British bank dealer. "Capital inflows have been the major driver in recent sessions for the rupee. If there is a bit of lull today, the dollar may see some bounce in local trading." Adds "exporters have been selling in bits and pieces and are still to come in, in a major way." Tips pair in 44.95-45.25 band for session. Foreign funds net buyers of a provisional INR11.37 billion worth of stocks yesterday, USD/INR 1-month NDFs at 45.27/45.34. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.2% at 3233.89, likely to stay in narrow range with no strong lead from offshore markets to help affirm clear direction, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker David Price; "There was no real direction from offshore, most of the markets have had pretty strong runs and there is no real corporate news." Adds, \uc2 AMP NZ Office Trust\uc2  (APT.NZ) down 1.3% at NZ$0.76 after KordaMentha report says company plans to change from a trust to a company, also changes management fee structure. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0252 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely to open higher tracking Treasury gains as investors fret over global economy, says senior trader at primary dealership; tips 7.80% 2020 bond to open around 99.70 vs 99.60 Monday close. "The market is now awaiting the auction with the new foreign fund limits likely in October. There has been a run up in some of the 5-year plus papers like the 7.99% 2017, 7.46% 2017 and 7.02% 2016 in anticipation of that," adds trader. Last week, government doubled foreign fund limit in government debt to $10 billion, but additional buying can only be in maturities of 5-year and above. Dealer adds auction papers announced for Friday in line with market expectations. RBI to sell INR40 billion of 7.17% 2015 bond, INR40 billion of 8.08% 2022 bond and INR30 billion of 8.26% 2027 paper Friday, in first auction of fiscal 2H. Dealer tips 10-year yield in tight 7.84%-7.86% band for session (7.86% last close). (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month at-the-money USD/JPY options fall to 10.30%/11.00% vs 10.50%/11.20% overnight in New York as spot remains in tight 84.18-84.35 range, says senior dealer at major Japan bank; adds, spot players remain cautious as they see chances of another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing if spot pair extends fall sharply. With possibility of another JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , concerns about pace of U.S. economic recovery, "investors are reluctant to take aggressive positions and therefore the spot pair is rangebound today, decreasing demand for hedge as a result." Reckons investors will keep close eye on U.S. data due later this week for near-term spot USD/JPY direction. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.9% at 454.17, with local investors buying on hopes banks will soon be able lower interest rates after amendments to \uc2 State Bank of Vietnam\uc2 's Circular 13 -- effective from October 10 -- will allow banks to use more funds to lend. "Sentiment is also improving after the government announced GDP +6.52% in Jan-Sep period, indicating that economic growth will reach the target of 6.7% for this year," says brokerage analyst. However, says sharp gains unlikely after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday said it would keep benchmark rate unchanged at 8% for October; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last raised benchmark rate, to 8% from 7%, December 1 2009. Tips key index in 450-455 range today. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares edge lower, hurt by profit-taking spurred by overnight losses on Wall Street; bellwether PSEi off 0.4% at 4106.24 in moderate volume, with decliners trouncing gainers 73 to 33; immediate PSEi support 4070, resistance 4150. Losers led by Megaworld (MEG.PH) down 0.9% to PHP2.28, Lepanto Consolidated (LC.PH) down 3.8% to PHP0.51, Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH) down 6.4% to PHP63.10. Grace Cerdenia, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2TradeAsia.com, says lack of major domestic developments also prodding investors to take profit on recent gains; also notes funds appear to be merely shifting to recent laggards. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea consumer sentiment index at 14-month low of 109 in September vs 110 in August on concerns about possible slowdown in recovery of global, domestic economies. Still, stays above key 100-point level for 17 months, indicating consumers largely optimistic about growth in coming months; "the consumer sentiment isn't that bad as the index has remained above the key level for more than a year, which is very rare. Having said that, the on-month decline reflects growing concerns about a possible slowdown in exports and local demand, affected by the slowing global economy," says NH Investment & Securities economist Kim Jong-su. Adds BOK may pause again in rate review next month amid stable inflation. (in-soo.nam@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 2327ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0002b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
