\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:USD/JPY Vols Down;Spot Remains In 84.18-84.35 Band\b0\par\par\uc2 1,469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 04:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0249 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month at-the-money USD/JPY options fall to 10.30%/11.00% vs 10.50%/11.20% overnight in New York as spot remains in tight 84.18-84.35 range, says senior dealer at major Japan bank; adds, spot players remain cautious as they see chances of another round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  increasing if spot pair extends fall sharply. With possibility of another JPY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , concerns about pace of U.S. economic recovery, "investors are reluctant to take aggressive positions and therefore the spot pair is rangebound today, decreasing demand for hedge as a result." Reckons investors will keep close eye on U.S. data due later this week for near-term spot USD/JPY direction. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.9% at 454.17, with local investors buying on hopes banks will soon be able lower interest rates after amendments to \uc2 State Bank of Vietnam\uc2 's Circular 13 -- effective from October 10 -- will allow banks to use more funds to lend. "Sentiment is also improving after the government announced GDP +6.52% in Jan-Sep period, indicating that economic growth will reach the target of 6.7% for this year," says brokerage analyst. However, says sharp gains unlikely after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday said it would keep benchmark rate unchanged at 8% for October; \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last raised benchmark rate, to 8% from 7%, December 1 2009. Tips key index in 450-455 range today. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares edge lower, hurt by profit-taking spurred by overnight losses on Wall Street; bellwether PSEi off 0.4% at 4106.24 in moderate volume, with decliners trouncing gainers 73 to 33; immediate PSEi support 4070, resistance 4150. Losers led by Megaworld (MEG.PH) down 0.9% to PHP2.28, Lepanto Consolidated (LC.PH) down 3.8% to PHP0.51, Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH) down 6.4% to PHP63.10. Grace Cerdenia, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 2TradeAsia.com, says lack of major domestic developments also prodding investors to take profit on recent gains; also notes funds appear to be merely shifting to recent laggards. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0236 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea consumer sentiment index at 14-month low of 109 in September vs 110 in August on concerns about possible slowdown in recovery of global, domestic economies. Still, stays above key 100-point level for 17 months, indicating consumers largely optimistic about growth in coming months; "the consumer sentiment isn't that bad as the index has remained above the key level for more than a year, which is very rare. Having said that, the on-month decline reflects growing concerns about a possible slowdown in exports and local demand, affected by the slowing global economy," says NH Investment & Securities economist Kim Jong-su. Adds BOK may pause again in rate review next month amid stable inflation. (in-soo.nam@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year JGB yield likely won't slide even if BOJ's quarterly tankan business sentiment survey due Wednesday highlights concern over economic slowdown ahead, as slower growth already priced into JGB market, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "The benchmark yield has already factored in an economic plateau and is declining accordingly." For that reason, tankan, also industrial production data for August due Thursday "are probably not going to spur further yield declines," Ishii says. While headline diffusion index of tankan expected to rise to 6 from previous survey's 1, according to Dow Jones survey, economists say report could show companies expect sentiment to fall back later in year, particular if JPY strength persists. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.8% at 9529.79 midday; trading volume low (just 584 million shares) as investors await quarterly BOJ tankan survey result due Wednesday. "It is difficult to take risks and buy exporter shares before the tankan release," says Yutaka Yoshii, general manager at \uc2 Mito Securities\uc2 , noting focus on firms' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate assumptions and outlook for employment and capital spending. Puts afternoon Nikkei range at 9470-9570. 29/33 Topix subindexes lower, with pharmaceutical subindex worst performer (down 2.4%) as many shares turn ex-dividend; \uc2 Takeda Pharmaceutical\uc2  (4502.TO) down 3.6% at Y3,885, Eisai (4523.TO) down 3.8% at Y2,988. Consumer lender Takefuji (8564.TO) ask-only (limit-down) at Y116 with company expected to file for bankruptcy protection later today. Real estate shares outperform on expectations for more BOJ monetary easing; Mitsubishi Estate (8802.TO) up 0.8% at Y1,370. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI flat at 22,338.01, after opening lower (early low at 22,308.96), as DJIA's modest fall doesn't provide direction for local market; index expected to remain in tight range today. Taifook says "a pullback looks overdue" after HSI's recent gains (+8.8% month-to-yesterday), but adds "a pullback by local property stocks looks more imminent with their significant outperformance of late against the broader market." Hang Seng Property Subindex +14.4% month-to-yesterday. Adds, there may be jitters ahead of HK chief executive's policy address early October in light of possible policies to cool housing market. Among HK property stocks, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) down 0.9% at HK$129.30, \uc2 Hang Lung Properties\uc2  (0101.HK) down 0.4% at HK$35.65. Worst blue-chip performer \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) down 4.1% at HK$11.22 after news of US$1.839 billion convertible bonds issue. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY slightly higher after PBOC sets USD/CNY central parity well above 6.7000 psychological level for 2nd day; on OTC, USD/CNY at 6.6941 vs 6.6923 last close, tipped at 6.6880-6.7000 for session. "The relatively stable fixing means the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking a break from yuan's sharp appreciation trend recently," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank; but adds "people in the market are unwilling to long dollars because they expect the yuan will continue to appreciate in the longer term." Offshore, 1-year USD/CNY at 6.5755/6.5795, up from 6.5739/6.5779 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up slightly as PBOC keeps bill yields unchanged in open market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , easing concerns about possible tightening measures to curb inflation, says Beijing-based dealer with local insurer; notes PBOC sells CNY5 billion 1-year bills at 2.0929%, CNY6 billion worth of 3-month bills at 1.5704%, both yields flat with rates on bills sold last week. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.53 vs 126.50 last close, tipped to consolidate in narrow 126.5-126.7 band before week-long National Day holiday starts Friday. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.3% at 1855.66, with market taking a breather in absence of positive cues to generate momentum. \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Lee Jae-mahn says market appears to be in a wait-and-see mode ahead of key domestic data like August Industrial Production Index, September CPI due later this week; tips 1850 support. "The market has risen sharply during the recent sessions, so there appears to be some profit-taking now in reaction to that." \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  off 3.7% at KRW155,500 after rising 6.3% last week, while \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE) also down 1.3% at KRW73,700 on profit-taking pressure. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan agricultural name offering good amounts of USD/JPY this morning, says Singapore-based dealer. USD/JPY last 84.23 vs 84.18 morning low. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] 10-year JGB yield marks 1-month low at 0.960% amid mounting expectations for further BOJ easing, with JGBs also supported by strength in U.S. Treasurys overnight. 10-year JGB yield likely to stay under pressure in near term, settling "in a range centered on 1%," says Makoto Yamashita, chief Japan interest rate strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . Given speculation Fed may also conduct more quantitative easing, says "we believe it will be difficult to sell bonds as long as increasing outright government bond purchases remain a future option for both central banks." (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 2249ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
