\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Keep  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Stocks Up-Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 02:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0011 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets notes Japan monetary authorities' sustained interest in keeping yen within a certain range, even resorting to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Says historically, the start to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  represented an entry opportunity in Japanese equity market (January 1999, September 2001), while the end of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  translated into exit opportunities (April 2000, June 2002, March 2004). While entry/exit opportunity pattern does not hold true for all incidents of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says it may be argued that Japanese equity market will likely remain on an uptrend as long as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continues. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0005 GMT [Dow Jones] Profit-taking in Singapore may surface in early trade with shares now at 27-month high, especially in wake of retreat in U.S. stocks. Support for STI, up 0.7% at 3113.46 yesterday (highest since June 2008), expected at last week's 3069 low. Any upside expected to meet resistance at 3146, higher end of breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "Even though the market could continue to trend upwards to the end of this month or even into early October, investors should be cautious of a sudden turn in sentiment," says \uc2 Asiasons WFG\uc2 ; adds "if (the U.S.) economy does not improve and the market continues to cheer for better-than-expected releases just because the estimates were low, there will be a point when sentiment will turn." Industrial property plays like Cache Logistics (K2LU.SG), Ascendas REIT (A17U.SG), Mapletree Logistics (M44U.SG) may slip as Global Logistic Properties' mega IPO, raising at least S$3.1 billion, may drain liquidity from smaller peers; listing expected October 18. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0005 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely rangebound amid dearth of fresh economic data; yields on benchmark 3-year MGS may keep slight upward bias as investors await issuance of new MYR3.0 billion 3-year Islamic bond; auction of the Government Investment Issue, or GII, scheduled for tomorrow, with bond to be issued Friday. Analyst expects GII to be sold at average 3.20%-3.25%, with bid-to-cover ratio of about 2.0X. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS ended mixed, with May 2013 +1 bp at 3.17%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.27%, September 2017 unchanged at 3.53%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.62%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0000 GMT [Dow Jones] RBS analysts expect Aussie business credit growth will recover to double-digit levels by FY12, and reckons that \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) and \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  (ANZ.AU) are better positioned for the environment over the next two to three years. "The strength of the economy and corporate balance sheets, management commentary and the RBS barometer of business credit all suggest an improvement in business credit is imminent," RBS says. But reckons the role of the corporate bond market should not be overstated. "While competitive pricing in offshore corporate bond markets means not all of the improvement in total business credit growth will end up on the banking sector's balance sheet, there are only a limited number of Australian companies that can access these markets," RBS says. "Given our view that the recovery in business credit growth will be driven initially by middle market lending, we think the banks are well placed for the early stages of the recovery in business credit." (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2353 GMT [Dow Jones] Tightness in supply side could help push metals prices higher, says major market participant, noting that inventories of all metals stocks were down except for nickel. Participant, who requests anonymity, says on chart, copper still looks good with expectation of buy stops over $8,000/metric ton, sell stops below $7,800. Triland Metals says general sentiment remains positive, expects to see copper revisit $8,000 this week. LME 3-month copper fell $34 on day yesterday to close at $7,910, lead lost $27 to end at $2,269, aluminum slipped $24.50 to $2,293, zinc declined $39.50 to $2,207.50, while tin added $50 to $23,650 and nickel gained $195 to $23,090. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2353 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to extend fall to a 3rd straight session after ending at 1,148.2 yesterday in Seoul, with support pegged at 1,140. Pair "on a steadily-sliding path. Unless there is an event, it will fall by KRW2 to KRW5 daily," says foreign bank trader; notes BOK's recent suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at slowing down pace of fall rather than maintaining certain level or reversing path. Local bank trader also tips further falls in pair today, saying "supply is still stronger than demand." News North Korea's leader promoted his youngest son to senior general in country's army unlikely to affect market as action viewed as natural process of taking step towards handing power to next generation and widely expected, say traders. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely to open lower, taking cues from weaker U.S. stocks, vs yesterday's close at 2.5-year high 1860.83 (up 0.8%); after index hit key 1860 level yesterday, it may not rise sharply higher amid a likely lack of upside momentum today, says Lee Kyung-soo at Taurus Investment & Securities. Notes it remains to be seen if domestic financial stocks will be affected by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  move to downgrade debt of \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  by 3 notches. Adds airline, shipbuilding, shipping stocks expected to remain strong on expectations of strong 3Q results. 1840-1870 band tipped for day. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says despite yesterday's Nikkei rally, with selling pressure mounting due to last-minute liquidation of margin long positions established in April, index will likely take a while before Japan share rally gains momentum, recommends entry into market in end-September. Adds, an extended winning streak could cause short-term technicals to indicate overbought conditions, while U.S. equities typically fall back following reporting season end. "Taking these factors into account, the ongoing recovery in Japanese shares might take a breather from mid- to end-October. Such a development would represent a correction, meaning limited downside risk." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2346 GMT [Dow Jones] Macquarie (MQG.AU) CEO Nicholas Moore sees emerging markets as the growth engine which will "more than offset the weakness" from Europe and the U.S. over the next few years. On a panel at the Forbes Global CEO conference in Sydney about navigating the recovery, Moore touts the group's exposure to emerging market economies despite the fact that some of the group's more recent acquisitions have been in the slower growing U.S. and European markets. Moore says the infrastructure cycle, specifically when governments look to the private sector to step in with infrastructure development, has been "a little softer globally than we would've expected". (cynthia.koons@dowjones.com )\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to trade with risks skewed to downside after retreating more than $1 from 9-session high $77.17/bbl yesterday, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart still positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; but spinning-top candlestick pattern reflecting indecision of traders completed yesterday. Support at $75.52 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to $74.66 (Friday's low), then $73.58 (Thursday's low) and $72.75 (Sept. 17 reaction low). But rise above $77.17 would turn near-term outlook positive; targeting $77.50 (200-day moving average), then $78.04 (Sept. 13 reaction high), $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 Aug. 4-Aug. 25 decline) and psychological $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). November crude last down 32 cents at $76.20/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2342 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold slips $1.70 early in Asia to $1,293.10, and according to ScotiaMocatta could be subject to further fall; cautions that technical signals over past 2 sessions warn of a reversal. "A lower close (today) would trigger short term selling of the metal," it says, adding though that gold remains in longer-term bullish up channel with channel support at $1,265, resistance $1,305. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  cuts its June 2011 target for Australia's S&P/ASX 200 index to 4750 points from 5000, reckons the nation's economy is at risk of overheating. "An economy approaching capacity constraints is generally a tough environment for stocks because something has to go wrong," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says. "If the pressure is eased by slower growth, earnings expectations are vulnerable. If the economy remains robust, rates have to rise--bringing the risk of a more severe downturn than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bargained for." Reckons that commodity investment will test economy's limits and will be largely insensitive to anything the RBA does. Adds the political environment suggests that fiscal policy is unlikely to help, leaving the adjustment to be borne mainly by consumer and housing activity. Adds that downside risk from a house price correction is more plausible than many think. "Against this background it is hard to see what can go right for stocks linked to discretionary consumer spending," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  says. S&P/ASX 200 last 4675.4. (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 2011ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100928e69s00009\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
