\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Higher As Data Boost Stimulus Expectations\b0\par\par\uc2 2,859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2010\par 00:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10858.14 +46.10  +0.43% \par Nasdaq     2379.59  +9.82  +0.41% \par S&P 500    1147.70  +5.54  +0.49% \par FTSE 100   5578.44  +5.02  +0.09% \par Xetra DAX  6276.09  -2.80  -0.04% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 28/32  +1/32 0.430% -1.5BP \par 5Y Tsy   100 3/32  +9/32 1.230% -5.9BP \par 10Y Tsy 101 13/32 +19/32 2.464% -6.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY       83.86-88     -0.49% \par Range         84.34-83.70 \par EUR/USD       1.3587-89    +1.03% \par Range         1.3595-1.3382 \par AUD/USD       0.9675-80    +0.72% \par Range         0.9686-0.9561 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.75-11.05% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    12.10-12.40% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    13.80-14.10% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,308.30 +$9.70 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,276.20-$1,311.80 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $76.18   -$0.34 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks climbed Tuesday despite drops in consumer confidence and central-Atlantic manufacturing conditions, as investors saw the economic weakness as a sign the Federal Reserve may be more likely to introduce more stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed up 46.10 points, or 0.43%, to 10858.14. \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  was the measure's best performer with an increase of 1.5%, while Intel was also strong with a 1.4% gain and \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  advanced 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite edged up 9.82, or 0.41%, to 2379.59, and the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index added 5.54, or 0.49%, to 1147.70.\par \par \uc2 The health-care sector led the gains, boosted by a report of strong growth in sales and volumes of prescription drugs from \uc2 Walgreen\uc2 . Shares of the drugstore operator jumped 11%.\par \par \uc2 Reports on housing, consumer confidence and manufacturing in the central Atlantic region all indicated the economy remains weak. However, investors took the disappointing data as an indication that the Fed might make more moves to support the economy even if inflation is a byproduct.\par \par \uc2 "That's the conventional wisdom of the market now, that if the economic reports are solid, the market will go up, and if they're not, the Fed will do more stimulus and then the market will still go up," said Phil Dow, director of equity strategy at RBC Wealth Management.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's trading also reflected end-of-month and end-of-quarter portfolio shuffling as the market approaches the conclusion of its best September since 1939. The Dow is up 8.4% for the month and up 4.1% for the year, having turned positive for 2010 this month.\par \par \uc2 "We've had a September to remember, certainly," said \uc2 Duncan Richardson\uc2 , chief equity investment officer at \uc2 Eaton Vance\uc2 . "Any of the gains in the year to date have occurred in the last month so you're going to have this odd and jerky effect coming into the end of the quarter."\par \par \uc2 Richardson said that those investors who didn't participate in this month's rally may be getting back into the market because of fear of missing out on further gains. Those who did participate are likely to be trimming positions because of concerns over how long the recent climb can last, he said.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Tuesday because of continued concerns about further tightening measures in the property market and a lackluster debut by Ningbo Port.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely trade around 2600 this week ahead of China's National Day holiday that runs from Oct. 1 through Oct. 7.\par \par \uc2 "The market should remain fairly stable ahead of the long holiday. Investors are trading cautiously because of uncertainties about policies related to real-estate developers and banks," said Zhang Zhuo, an analyst at Minsheng Securities.\par \par \uc2 Huang Bin, an analyst at Gold State Securities, said any new measures will likely be launched after the National Day holiday and could bring some relief to the property sector.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Tuesday as high-yielding drugmaker shares dropped on ex-dividend selling amid general caution ahead of Wednesday's release of the Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan survey.\par \par \uc2 Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities, said that pressure for the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to carry out additional monetary easing steps during its October policy board meeting will increase if the tankan shows a deterioration in the business sentiment outlook for the October-December quarter.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures settled lower Tuesday in U.S. trading, as worries about demand for oil and fuel products trumped a sharp fall in the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures settled above $1,300 for the first time Tuesday, as investors bet the metal will hold its value more strongly than \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks amid ongoing concerns about the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange swung back to positive territory after the dollar weakened against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to poor U.S. consumer confidence figures, market participants said Tuesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell broadly Tuesday as disappointing U.S. data sparked a fresh round of speculation that the Federal Reserve could soon embark on a stimulus plan to kickstart a flagging economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dropped to its lowest level since February as investors ditched the dollar in favor of its rivals, including the euro, which rose to its highest level since April.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also fell against the yen, dropping to its lowest level against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's massive mid-September \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem yen strength. The drop put investors on high alert for signs of Japan re-entering markets to prop the dollar, but analysts said there was no sign of Japanese authorities' hand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's up to the data to disprove the case for QE," said Alan Ruskin, global head of G-10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Deutsche Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, referring to quantitative easing, or the possible asset purchases by the Fed to stimulate growth. "Data like we've just had only go on to prove the case for QE," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Much worse-than-expected U.S. consumer confidence data, precipitated the falling dollar, which dropped to its lowest level since March 2008 against the Swiss franc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Tuesday in New York, the euro was at $1.3590, up from $1.3444 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y83.89, down from Y84.25, while the euro was at Y114.00, above Y113.25. The U.K. pound was at $1.5802, off from $1.5826. The dollar was at CHF0.9760, down from CHF0.9857.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , retreated to 78.904 from 79.502. It dropped below 79 for the first time since February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. consumer confidence fell sharply in September, as consumers saw no improvement in the labor markets, according to a report released Tuesday. The Conference Board, a private research group, said its index of consumer confidence fell to 48.5 this month from a revised 53.2 in August. The September reading was far worse than the 52.0 expected by economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires. It is the lowest reading since February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell broadly on the back of the disappointing data, which solidified the case for a fresh round of stimulus from the Fed, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. economy continues to show growth, albeit slow growth, even if recent data have been disappointing. Because of this, a Fed stimulus program of massive, crisis proportions likely isn't necessary, which should keep the dollar's losses in check, said Jane Foley, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, "as long as the Fed have signaled that the door to QE is open, the likelihood is the dollar will retain a softer bias," Foley said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the dollar fell, investors were on alert for Japan returning to the market to prop up the greenback, especially as the Sept. 30 midpoint for Japan's fiscal year looms, with Japanese companies likely to be repatriating funds. Analysts said there were no signs of Japan in the market after the dollar's drop.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The [Bank of Japan] is fighting data like this," Ruskin said of the weak U.S. figures, that led investors out of the dollar and into the yen. "The BOJ is basically fighting a broader dollar trend," making it difficult to fight the yen's rise, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the U.K. pound dropped sharply against the dollar before regaining some of its poise as the dollar's losses picked up steam. Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee member Adam Posen's said Tuesday that he sees a "clear" case for the U.K. to undertake another round of its own asset purchases to avoid a Japan-style protracted period of economic underperformance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pound fell to its lowest level since May against the euro, at GBP0.8468, compared with GBP0.8502 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys rallied for a second straight session Tuesday as weak consumer confidence and manufacturing data fueled bets that the Federal Reserve needs to step up government bond purchases to support the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such bets lifted demand for $35 billion, five-year notes auction that were sold at a record low yield of 1.26%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors continued to wager on large-scale purchases of Treasurys, known as quantitative easing, even as the Wall Street Journal reported that Fed officials are weighing a more open-ended, smaller-scale program that they could adjust as the recovery unfolds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The bond market is telling you that there are enough people who are convinced that the economy is in such bad shape that the Fed needs to buy Treasurys on a large scale," said Charles Comiskey, head of Treasury trading at Scotia Capital in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1948 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was up 19/32 to yield 2.464% and the 30-year bond was up 1 7/32 to yield 3.656%. The five-year note was 9/32 higher to yield 1.230%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yield on the 10-year note, a benchmark for consumer and corporate borrowings, touched 2.448%, the weakest level since this year's trough of 2.418% on Aug. 25. [ 28-09-10 2204GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders noted that the U.S. data, along with Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee member Adam Posen's comment that he sees a "clear" case for undertaking more asset purchases in the U.K., ignited optimism that large-scale bond buying is still on the table.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The possibility of data-dependent buying introduces a new convexity to the rates markets," said Christian Cooper, senior rates trader in New York at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jefferies & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "This implies that weaker-than-expected data will push more buyers into the bond market in anticipation of additional [quantitative easing] and vice versa for even nominally positive data."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys got an extra boost from worries about sovereign debt problems in peripheral countries in the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors fretted about the health of Ireland's banking system after state broadcaster RTE re-aired a comment from earlier this month by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  warning that Ireland's cost to bail out the Anglo Irish Bank Corp., one of the nation's largest banks, could exceed EUR35 billion, which could trigger further ratings downgrades.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Demand for this week's Treasury auctions has been strong. The five-year note auction Tuesday was 2.96 times oversubscribed, compared with the average of 2.8 for the previous four auctions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The lack of alternative high-quality dollar assets leaves Treasurys in high demand," said John Briggs, U.S. interest-rate strategist at RBS Securities Inc. in Stamford, Conn. "There has been continued suspicion that Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollars will make their way into the Treasury auctions this week."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended slightly higher after the PBOC kept bill yields unchanged in its open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Liquidity conditions improved after the PBOC injected funds into the money market, while CBRC's trial program to allow some county-level financial institutions that meet certain loan criteria to enjoy reserve requirement ratio reduction lifted sentiment, a Shenyin Wanguo Securities trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is tipped in a narrow 126.5-126.6 band in the coming two days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended higher across the curve Tuesday, supported by increasing expectations for additional easing by the Bank of Japan and sharp gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight, although trading was sluggish due to caution before the release of the BOJ tankan business sentiment survey.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The possibility is higher that the tankan will show that business managers grew more cautious about the economic outlook and the pace of improvement will moderate down the road, said Akito Fukunaga, chief rates strategist at RBS Securities. In that case, Fukunaga expects the 10-year cash JGB yield to sink to around 0.90%-0.95%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ's quarterly tankan survey's headline diffusion index, which measures mood among big manufacturers, is expected to rise to six in September from one the previous quarter, according to the median forecast of 11 Tokyo-based economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires. The index is calculated by subtracting the percentage of firms saying business conditions are good from those saying they are bad.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation is growing that Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may take further action as early as next month to ease negative impact of the surging yen on the overall economy and business sentiment. The BOJ's policy board is scheduled to hold a policy-setting meeting Oct. 4-5, and will meet again Oct. 28 to discuss semiannual forecasts for the economy and prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, some analysts are skeptical if the BOJ's accommodative policy stance alone will help push long-term yields further down.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While yields could still fall if U.S. rates or other external factors encourage such a decline, it is difficult to see much downside at this stage due to the BOJ's policy alone," said Chotaro Morita, chief strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Clearly, the policy effect of intermittent quantitative expansions has already reached its limit, at least in terms of pushing down yields," Morita said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also among the data for release Wednesday is China's manufacturing purchasing managers index.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "[The data are an] important factor for risk as continued Chinese performance will be vital to the overall global growth recovery," \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategists said in a note issued Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Consumer Confidence Drops Sharply \par Home Prices Climb In July \par US House Poised To Press China On \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Policy \par Durbin Says No Senate Vote On Yuan Before Election \par Greece Pursuing Radical EUR3B Privatization Plan \par Capital Markets Have Improved, Says Fed's Warsh \par More Americans Blame Obama For Economy \par Harrisburg Workers Will Be Paid, At Least This Week \par Large \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dollar Thrifty\f28 \uc2 \f2  Holder To Oppose Hertz Deal \par Whitney Sees State Debt As Next Big Crisis \par Apple, H-P Shares Move On Cook Rumor \par IMF's Strauss-Kahn: Risk Of Global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  War Low \par IMF's Lipsky: Moderate Global Economic Recovery To Continue \par =Zoom Raises Mobile-Phone Sales Target As Orders Stay Strong \par =INTERVIEW:Ford Treasurer Worries About Europe, Criticizes S Korea \par Takefuji Laywer: Number of Potential Borrowers Could Be Unexpectedly High \par =DATA SNAP: NZ August Goods Trade Deficit NZ$437M; Consensus Deficit NZ$425M \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2300  S Korea      Aug Balance of payments \par 2350  Japan        Sep Provisional Trade Statistics for 1st 10 days of \par                    Month \par 2350  Japan        3Q Tankan Survey of Enterprises in Japan \par 0030  Japan        Aug Detailed Import & Export Statistics \par 0230  China        Sep HSBC China Manufacturing PMI \par 0645  France       Sep Consumer confidence survey \par 0810  EU           Sep Euro-zone Retail PMI \par 0830  UK           Aug Bank of England's UK residents deposits & lending analysis \par 0830  UK           Aug Sectoral breakdown of aggregate M4 and M4 lending \par 0830  UK           Sep Monetary & Financial Statistics \par 0830  UK           Aug Bank of England lending to individuals figures \par 0830  UK           Aug BSA savings & mortgage lending figures \par 0830  UK           Jul Service sector \par 0900  EU           Sep Business & Consumer Surveys - Business Climate Indicator & \par                    Economic Sentiment Indicator \par 1100  US           Sep 24 MBA Weekly Mortgage Applications Survey \par 1415  US           Minneapolis Fed Pres Kocherlakota speaks in London \par 1430  US           Sep 24 EIA Weekly Petroleum Status Report \par 1620  US           Philadelphia Fed Pres Plosser speech on the economy \par 1715  US           Boston Fed Pres Rosengren speaks in New York \par 1900  EU           Eurofi - ECB President Jean Claude Trichet at the Eurofi \par                    Financial Forum \par 1900  US           Sep Agricultural Prices \par 2301  UK           Sep Consumer Confidence \par N/A   Pakistan     State Bank of Pakistan Monetary Policy Statement \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 28-09-10 2204GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100928e69s001n8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
