\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-BOJ divided on need to ease policy next week\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 20:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Some BOJ policymakers want to save policy ammunition\par \par \uc2 * BOJ tankan, due Wednesday, may affect policy decision\par \par \uc2 * Risk that Fed easing could undermine impact of BOJ policy (Recasts, adding analyst quotes)\par \par \uc2 By Rie Ishiguro\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 28 (Reuters) - The Bank of Japan is divided about the need to ease monetary policy next week, which may mean it delays taking action despite government calls for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to support the economy.\par \par \uc2 Governor Masaaki Shirakawa has repeatedly said the Bank of Japan is ready to act if needed, but sources say some BOJ officials want the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to save its dwindling arsenal of policy options in case the economy deteriorates further.\par \par \uc2 In addition, there is no set view in markets on what steps the BOJ would take if it was to act.\par \par \uc2 Several ministers have called on the BOJ to follow up government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen with monetary measures to support the economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets on Sept. 15 to sell an estimated 2 trillion yen ($23.73 billion) after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to a 15 year high against the dollar, raising concerns among policymakers that its strength could undermine exports.\par \par \uc2 The government is also lining up a supplementary budget, although several ministers said on Tuesday its size was undecided.\par \par \uc2 "The problem for the BOJ is that its policy options are limited and it's hard to make decisions when most or some people on its policy board acknowledge that recent easing has had small impacts on markets," Adrian Foster, head of financial markets research with \uc2 Rabobank International\uc2  in Hong Kong, said.\par \par \uc2 "It is not intellectually rewarding when they keep expanding fund supplies but see the market impact is very very small."\par \par \uc2 Government concerns stem from growing evidence that the economic recovery is faltering.\par \par \uc2 Economic growth slipped from a healthy annualised rate of 5.0 percent in the first quarter of 2010 to just 1.5 percent in the second quarter. Data on Monday showed annual export growth dropped in August for a sixth straight month.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the Sept. 15 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sources said the BOJ could loosen policy at its Oct. 4-5 meeting if the economic recovery was deemed by the board to be under threat.\par \par \uc2 They said an increase in government bond buying and another expansion of its cheap fund-supply tool were emerging as the most likely policy options.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei business daily said on Tuesday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may expand its fixed-rate loans and step up buying of short-term government debt.\par \par \uc2 CAUTION\par \par \uc2 But some BOJ officials want to wait until there is further evidence that a strong yen and slowing overseas growth are hurting the economy.\par \par \uc2 These officials argue that such evidence may be more apparent at the BOJ's Oct. 28 meeting, when the BOJ publishes a twice-yearly economic and price outlook report.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ will also closely look at its own quarterly business survey, or tankan, due on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of timing, the BOJ is worried that any impact from its easing would be offset by fresh policy action from the U.S. Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the Fed to announce its own government bond purchases as soon as its next meeting on Nov. 2-3, a prospect already weighing on the dollar against the yen.\par \par \uc2 Fed officials are considering a more open-ended, smaller-scale bond buying programme compared with 2009, the Wall Street Journal reported. Divisions in the BOJ's nine-member board have been apparent in recent meetings.\par \par \uc2 One board member voted against an expansion of the BOJ's cheap loans scheme at an emergency BOJ meeting on Aug. 30. Two members had opposed easing policy in March.\par \par \uc2 In the latest government call for action, Japan Economics Minister Banri Kaieda said the BOJ should consider lifting its ceiling on government bond holdings.\par \par \uc2 However, the BOJ has been wary of increasing its self-imposed ceiling. Indeed, Shirakawa has said there is hardly any evidence that massive expansion in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance sheets stimulates the economy and prices. ($1=84.29 Yen) (Additional reporting by Leika Kihara, Soham Chatterjee in Bangalore; Editing by Edmund Klamann and Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100927e69r001ew\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
