\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: CDS On Ireland Could Close At Record High Around 485 BP\b0\par\par\uc2 1,295 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 20:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1853 GMT [Dow Jones] Irish credit default swaps could close at a new record high Monday night around 485BP as fresh fears about the nation's debt problems continue to surface. That's equivalent to $485,000 annually to cover $10M of bonds for five years, based on prices from CDS data provider Markit. Friday, the cost was $462,000. The previous record close for CDS on Ireland was last Thursday at $477,000, said Markit, although the intraday record was $515,000 the same day. Early Monday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  cut the credit ratings of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anglo Irish Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's unguaranteed senior unsecured debt three notches to Baa3 from A3, reflecting the "greater marginal risk" without government support of the securities. Baa3 is the last rung on the ratings ladder before non-investment grade territory, or 'junk' debt. The bank's subordinated debt was also lowered six notches to Caa1 from Ba1. (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1834 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD will gain on back of Fed's possible QE but upside is limited, says Standard Chartered, which revised forecast for pair higher. "While monetary policy settings are currently working against the USD, fiscal dynamics and growth potential may be more positive influences in the months ahead. In addition, the euro area still faces a range of structural problems that diminishes its appeal as an investment alternative and store of value." The bank calls for EUR/USD to end Q3 at 1.37, compared to its previous forecast of 1.22; it will end the year at 1.40, compared to previous forecast of 1.17. EUR/USD will end 2011 at 1.32 compared to previous forecast of 1.30, the bank said. Pair trades at 1.3474 from 1.3493 late Fri, according to EBS via CQG.\par \par \uc2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1823 GMT [Dow Jones] Emerging market policymakers are ramping up talk of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ---with some already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 -to stem their appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , blaming short-term "hot" money, which leads to market instability. But \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says the "wall of money" that's driving up emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is by-and-large not "hot" at all. It is more fundamental, not short-term, and is tied to broad balance of payments. Goldman pays particular attention to Taiwan and South Korea. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1810 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Exelon Generation Company\uc2  has launched its sale of $900M in 10-and 31-year bonds at 150BP and 208BP over Tsys, respectively, inside price guidance of 155 and 212.5. The shorter-term tranche is $550M and the longer-term one $350M. Net proceeds from the offering will be used to fund a portion of \uc2 Exelon Corp\uc2  acquisition of John Deere Renewables. The securities are expected to be rated A3 by \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , BBB by S&P and BBB+ by Fitch. The issuer will have the right to redeem the 10-year notes at 101% of par value if the acquisition is not completed by March 31 next year. That feature does not exist on the 31-year tranche. Leading the sale are \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 , \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 . (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1802 GMT [Dow Jones] After a slow summer, muni market is seeing a turn in issuance, but demand is showing signs of wear, says Janney's Alan Schankel. Issuance alone this week around $7B, and Bond Buyer 30-day visible supply, at $13.1B, is highest it's been since last Nov, he says. But demand doesn't seem to be as strong: flows into mutual funds have been positive all year, save two April tax weeks, but the pace is slowing, he says. "As investors wrestle with extremely low rates, and more new issues to choose from, yields may see upward pressure," Schankel says.(kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1751 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the wall of money going into emerging markets now, Win Thin of BBH says more and more countries will start to consider and possibly enact programs to limit foreign short-term fixed income investments. Brazil's Finance Minister has already signaled as much, almost exactly one year after his government implemented the broader IOF tax on incoming \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions. The new approach will be more targeted, says Thin. "The fundamental drivers of these inflows (growth and interest rate differentials between developed world and EM) are likely to persist through 2011," he said. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1740 GMT [Dow Jones] The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to raise rates in 1Q 2011, says Nomura, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday left its key rate at 5.25%. Nomura says given that there remains no deal with the IMF following local elections and concerns about peripheral European bond markets continue, this is likely to limit movement of Hungarian yield spreads. Nomura therefore considers rate cuts unlikely at this stage. It notes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still skeptical about the government's ability to put forward a credible budget. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1733 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Eurodollar futures prices sharply higher, with some contracts reaching intraday highs as 2-yr Tsy note auction produces record low yields. Quarterly 2012 contracts recently up 4.5 to 9 BP. 2013 contracts recently 10 to 13.5 BP higher. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1722 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were lower in NY Mon from Tokyo after the dollar fell against the yen. One-month at-the-money USD/JPY options implied volatilities were at 10.50%/11.20%, from 10.70%/11.40% in Tokyo and from 10.50%/11.20% Fri in NY. One-month EUR/USD implied vols were at 11.45%/12.45%, from 11.40%/12.40% in Tokyo and from 11.40%/12.40% Fri in NY. One-month EUR/JPY implied vols were at 13.50%/15.50% from 13.55%/15.55% in Tokyo and from 13.50%/15.50% in NY Fri. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1714 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are boosted after 2-yr sale. Notes are sold at lowest yield on record, a sign of strong ongoing demand for Tsys as investors remain wary that economy can recover on its own, without more Fed help. 2-yr up 1/32 to 0.424%, 10-yr up 25/32 to 2.515%. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1712 GMT [Dow Jones] The Treasury auction this week kicks off with a bang. The $36 bln two-year sale garnered strong demand, as reflected in the 3.78 bid-to-cover, which is the second highest after 3.97 in Aug 2007. The indirect bid of 39%, a gauge of foreign demand, jumps from 29.2 last month. Some market participants point to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related buying from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  which just \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years earlier this month to rein in the yen's surge. Dollar proceeds from Japan are typically recycled into short-dated Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1703 GMT [Dow Jones] The indirect bid - a proxy for demand from foreign buyers including central banks - for the two-year note was 39%, compared to the average of 34.9% for the past four auctions. The direct bid, a category of bids from non-primary dealers, banks, money managers and depository institutions who have direct accounts to submit bids to the Treasury auctions, was 11%, compared to the average of 15.5% for the past four auctions. (min.zeng@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 1455ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r000bk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
