\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Exelon Launches $900M 2-Part Bond Issue\b0\par\par\uc2 1,086 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 20:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1810 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Exelon Generation Company\f28 \uc2 \f2  has launched its sale of $900M in 10-and 31-year bonds at 150BP and 208BP over Tsys, respectively, inside price guidance of 155 and 212.5. The shorter-term tranche is $550M and the longer-term one $350M. Net proceeds from the offering will be used to fund a portion of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Exelon Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  acquisition of John Deere Renewables. The securities are expected to be rated A3 by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , BBB by S&P and BBB+ by Fitch. The issuer will have the right to redeem the 10-year notes at 101% of par value if the acquisition is not completed by March 31 next year. That feature does not exist on the 31-year tranche. Leading the sale are \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1802 GMT [Dow Jones] After a slow summer, muni market is seeing a turn in issuance, but demand is showing signs of wear, says Janney's Alan Schankel. Issuance alone this week around $7B, and Bond Buyer 30-day visible supply, at $13.1B, is highest it's been since last Nov, he says. But demand doesn't seem to be as strong: flows into mutual funds have been positive all year, save two April tax weeks, but the pace is slowing, he says. "As investors wrestle with extremely low rates, and more new issues to choose from, yields may see upward pressure," Schankel says.(kelly.nolan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1751 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the wall of money going into emerging markets now, Win Thin of BBH says more and more countries will start to consider and possibly enact programs to limit foreign short-term fixed income investments. Brazil's Finance Minister has already signaled as much, almost exactly one year after his government implemented the broader IOF tax on incoming \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions. The new approach will be more targeted, says Thin. "The fundamental drivers of these inflows (growth and interest rate differentials between developed world and EM) are likely to persist through 2011," he said. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1740 GMT [Dow Jones] The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to raise rates in 1Q 2011, says Nomura, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday left its key rate at 5.25%. Nomura says given that there remains no deal with the IMF following local elections and concerns about peripheral European bond markets continue, this is likely to limit movement of Hungarian yield spreads. Nomura therefore considers rate cuts unlikely at this stage. It notes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still skeptical about the government's ability to put forward a credible budget. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1733 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Eurodollar futures prices sharply higher, with some contracts reaching intraday highs as 2-yr Tsy note auction produces record low yields. Quarterly 2012 contracts recently up 4.5 to 9 BP. 2013 contracts recently 10 to 13.5 BP higher. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1722 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were lower in NY Mon from Tokyo after the dollar fell against the yen. One-month at-the-money USD/JPY options implied volatilities were at 10.50%/11.20%, from 10.70%/11.40% in Tokyo and from 10.50%/11.20% Fri in NY. One-month EUR/USD implied vols were at 11.45%/12.45%, from 11.40%/12.40% in Tokyo and from 11.40%/12.40% Fri in NY. One-month EUR/JPY implied vols were at 13.50%/15.50% from 13.55%/15.55% in Tokyo and from 13.50%/15.50% in NY Fri. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1714 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are boosted after 2-yr sale. Notes are sold at lowest yield on record, a sign of strong ongoing demand for Tsys as investors remain wary that economy can recover on its own, without more Fed help. 2-yr up 1/32 to 0.424%, 10-yr up 25/32 to 2.515%. (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1712 GMT [Dow Jones] The Treasury auction this week kicks off with a bang. The $36 bln two-year sale garnered strong demand, as reflected in the 3.78 bid-to-cover, which is the second highest after 3.97 in Aug 2007. The indirect bid of 39%, a gauge of foreign demand, jumps from 29.2 last month. Some market participants point to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related buying from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  which just \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in six years earlier this month to rein in the yen's surge. Dollar proceeds from Japan are typically recycled into short-dated Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1703 GMT [Dow Jones] The indirect bid - a proxy for demand from foreign buyers including central banks - for the two-year note was 39%, compared to the average of 34.9% for the past four auctions. The direct bid, a category of bids from non-primary dealers, banks, money managers and depository institutions who have direct accounts to submit bids to the Treasury auctions, was 11%, compared to the average of 15.5% for the past four auctions. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1702 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasury sold $36 bln two-year notes at a record low yield of 0.441%. It is the fifth month in a row the Treasury sells 2-yr debt with a record low rate. The yield compared with 0.443% just before the auction. The bid-to-cover ratio is 3.78, compared with the average of 3.21 from the past four auctions. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1658 GMT [Dow Jones] Shortly before $36 billion auction of two-year Treasurys at 1 p.m. EDT, the when-issued two-year note yielded 0.443% at 12:58 p.m. EDT, compared to the benchmark issue which yielded 0.428%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1656 GMT [Dow Jones] Before 2-yr auction, 2-yr at 0.428%, 10-yr at 2.528%. Auction expected to go well, getting boost from Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 fx intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Dan Greenhaus\uc2  of \uc2 Miller Tabak + Co\uc2 . says that "along with the fairly high probability of further Fed asset purchases, Treasury auctions are likely to be bolstered for the foreseeable future."\par \par \uc2 (deborah.blumberg@dowjones.com) [ 09-27-10 1414ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r000be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
