\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Brazil Just The First To Signal Limit In Short-Term  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Flows\b0\par\par\uc2 944 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 19:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1751 GMT [Dow Jones] Given the wall of money going into emerging markets now, Win Thin of BBH says more and more countries will start to consider and possibly enact programs to limit foreign short-term fixed income investments. Brazil's Finance Minister has already signaled as much, almost exactly one year after his government implemented the broader IOF tax on incoming \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions. The new approach will be more targeted, says Thin. "The fundamental drivers of these inflows (growth and interest rate differentials between developed world and EM) are likely to persist through 2011," he said. (riva.froymovich@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1740 GMT [Dow Jones] The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to raise rates in 1Q 2011, says Nomura, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday left its key rate at 5.25%. Nomura says given that there remains no deal with the IMF following local elections and concerns about peripheral European bond markets continue, this is likely to limit movement of Hungarian yield spreads. Nomura therefore considers rate cuts unlikely at this stage. It notes the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still skeptical about the government's ability to put forward a credible budget. (veronika.gulyas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1733 GMT [Dow Jones] Longer-dated Eurodollar futures prices sharply higher, with some contracts reaching intr