\par\par \b \uc2 CHARTING MARKETS:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Bank Acts, Market  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Cox, CMT   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 419 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 18:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Evidently, pulses fluttered lately when the Bank of Japan heavily sold yen against the dollar and made good on this month's mutterings of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese officials.\par \par \uc2 Now \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is attractive to nimble and well-positioned traders who stand to profit from the generally wide short-term moves that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action generates.\par \par \uc2 In this case, however, my examination of the charts suggests that the BOJ's action to cheapen the yen will be futile. See chart at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4634"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4634}}}\par \par \uc2 For example, the wide range of Sept. 15 included a technical breakout above channel resistance at Y84.41. But as of this writing, as the chart shows, the dollar is trading at Y84.24 and so the Sept. 15 breaking is now likely to be nullified.\par \par \uc2 A zoom in on the chart shows that the range of Sept. 15 trading was between Y82.86 and Y85.79. The dollar is now just below resistance at the September 16 range midpoint, which is Y84.32. This is a technical caution signal to the dollar bulls. See chart at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4635"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4635}}}\par \par \uc2 Finally, this range midpoint technique of course can be applied to the annual chart to show the high probability of an imminent extension of the dollar's multi-year downtrend against the yen.\par \par \uc2 In 1995, the dollar fell to Y79.75, which is the current low for its downtrend since 1971, on this chart. See chart at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4637"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4637}}}\par \par \uc2 The dollar is now well below the midpoint of 1995 trading, which is Y92.27, and a revisit of the 1995 low is in my opinion is a matter of a relatively short time. The result may be eventual trading below Y60.\par \par \uc2 (Stephen Cox, a chartered market technician, is chief technician for Dow Jones Newswires. He can be reached at 212-416-2212 or by email at stephen.cox@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (Data by CQG)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 09-27-10 1201ET ]\par \par \uc2 1081 4247   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r000a6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
