\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Indonesia Shares +1.6%; Resistance Tipped At 3470\b0\par\par\uc2 1,552 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 05:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0307 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +1.6% at 3450.812 in moderate volume, off earlier high of 3461.035, boosted by rally on Wall Street Friday plus firmer IDR, traders say; tip resistance at 3470. "Swift profit taking by retail local players pulled down the main index from its high amid a generally bullish trend," says trader at First Asia Capital. Among gainers, chemical company \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tri Polyta\f28 \uc2 \f2  (TPIA.JK) +14% at IDR3,700, on news parent Barito (BRPT.JK), up 6.5% at IDR1,320, will merge \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tri Polyta\f28 \uc2 \f2  with unlisted unit chemical company PT Chandra Asri. Meanwhile, heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) up 2.3% at IDR9,100 after ADRs in NY rise 2.6%. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds may open flat to mildly positive on continuing positive sentiment after last week's foreign fund limit hike in debt, lower fiscal 2H borrowing, says trader with primary dealership. But notes "traders have now taken positions. So, I don't expect yields to drift down too much. 7.80% looks like a strong support for the 10-year paper (vs 7.87% last close)." Policy makers remain confident about inflation easing, which may also give traders hope of RBI pausing at ensuing policy meeting. Plan Panel chief Montek Singh Ahluwalia tells Dow Jones yesterday that he expects inflation for December to be about 6%, and easing down further to levels of 5.5% by March; also expects food inflation to ease to 5%-6% by November. Dealer tips 10-year paper in 7.85%-7.88% yield band for session. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely to open down as stronger EUR, expected higher opening for stocks may push pair down, says dealer with foreign bank; pair may open at 45.20 vs 45.26 in late Friday trade. "Friday saw a lot of selling by exporters but today the pair may consolidate in a range," says dealer. Tips pair in 45.05-45.35 range in session. Foreign funds were net buyers of provisional INR11.50 billion worth of Indian stocks on National Stock Exchange on Friday. USD/INR 1-month NDF at 45.41/45.51. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] STOCK CALL: Yuanta cuts Chicony's (2385.TW) target to NT$61.00 from NT$70.00 after lowering 2010 earnings forecast by 18% and 2011's by 24% to reflect weaker sales and margin outlooks. "Chicony has seen several order cuts for its notebook products (43% of 2010 sales) owing to weak demand since July, in-line with what we observed in the supply chain. This in turn has hampered the company's ability to raise keyboard and power supply prices in 3Q10. " Keeps Hold. Stock +1.9% at NT$68.70. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options rise to 10.70%/11.40% from 10.50%/11.20% in NY Friday, as investors who sold options contracts Friday to avoid decline in value over time now buy back contracts at beginning of week. Options dealer at major Japanese bank says vols unlikely to change in global day as long as spot stays around current levels. Adds spot may trade in narrow range with downside limited as players cautious about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities' potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while upside capped by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  exporter selling before quarter's end. Says a player buys 6-month USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 89.95 with implied vol of 11.65% for unknown amount. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,295.90/oz, down $1.10 since Friday's NY close but overall bull trend intact, says MKS Finance. "We expect prices to move higher when Chinese markets will reopen after a public holiday as the underlying bullish trend will most probably continue to be fueled by the high level of uncertainty regarding the U.S. and European economies." House adds, silver likely to continue to gain after hitting highest level since October 1980 on Friday. Tips technical target of 23.54/oz. Spot silver at $21.44/oz, down 2 cents. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR extends decline to reach fresh 13-year low of 3.0890 vs 3.0920 late Friday; dealer says given USD's recent weak spell, pair likely to maintain downward bias this week; tips pair to test 3.0750 support near-term; adds, market participants on lookout for any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action following suspected USD buying in recent sessions to slow MYR's rise. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW lower at 1,148.8, but off earlier 1,146.0 low, on solid corporate USD demand on dips; participants remain cautious over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities at levels below KRW1,150, traders say. "The 1,145 level seems to be about as low as the pair could get for today. But it will also be difficult to see the pair rebound back to levels above 1,152 either," says local bank trader. Traders note pair remains biased down vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul, as offshore players more interested in selling USD/KRW. Kospi last +0.6%. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Upcoming festival season in India, traditional peak of gold demand, not necessarily one-way bet for price rises, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Rennie. Jewellery traditionally acc