\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Rise; Players Buy Back Contracts\b0\par\par\uc2 1,578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 04:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0257 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options rise to 10.70%/11.40% from 10.50%/11.20% in NY Friday, as investors who sold options contracts Friday to avoid decline in value over time now buy back contracts at beginning of week. Options dealer at major Japanese bank says vols unlikely to change in global day as long as spot stays around current levels. Adds spot may trade in narrow range with downside limited as players cautious about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities' potential \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while upside capped by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exporter selling before quarter's end. Says a player buys 6-month USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 89.95 with implied vol of 11.65% for unknown amount. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,295.90/oz, down $1.10 since Friday's NY close but overall bull trend intact, says MKS Finance. "We expect prices to move higher when Chinese markets will reopen after a public holiday as the underlying bullish trend will most probably continue to be fueled by the high level of uncertainty regarding the U.S. and European economies." House adds, silver likely to continue to gain after hitting highest level since October 1980 on Friday. Tips technical target of 23.54/oz. Spot silver at $21.44/oz, down 2 cents. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0246 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR extends decline to reach fresh 13-year low of 3.0890 vs 3.0920 late Friday; dealer says given USD's recent weak spell, pair likely to maintain downward bias this week; tips pair to test 3.0750 support near-term; adds, market participants on lookout for any \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action following suspected USD buying in recent sessions to slow MYR's rise. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0245 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW lower at 1,148.8, but off earlier 1,146.0 low, on solid corporate USD demand on dips; participants remain cautious over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities at levels below KRW1,150, traders say. "The 1,145 level seems to be about as low as the pair could get for today. But it will also be difficult to see the pair rebound back to levels above 1,152 either," says local bank trader. Traders note pair remains biased down vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul, as offshore players more interested in selling USD/KRW. Kospi last +0.6%. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0242 GMT [Dow Jones] Upcoming festival season in India, traditional peak of gold demand, not necessarily one-way bet for price rises, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Rennie. Jewellery traditionally accounted for majority of gold demand but pipped by investors seeking asset diversification and safe havens over past year; investment demand still booming, but given current record prices continued jewellery-buying can't be taken for granted: "The thing that worries me a bit is that at the lofty prices we've reached physical demand might show some signs of slowing up. Gold in rupees is expensive." Most-traded October gold on MCX has closed above June's INR19,069/kg record close in 9 of last 10 sessions; last at INR19,135/kg, equivalent to $1,316/oz; spot gold last at $1,296.10/oz. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] Upcoming festival season in India, traditional peak of gold demand, not necessarily one-way bet for price rises, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Robert Rennie. Jewellery traditionally accounted for majority of gold demand but pipped by investors seeking asset diversification and safe havens over past year; investment demand still booming, but given current record prices continued jewellery-buying can't be taken for granted: "The thing that worries me a bit is that at the lofty prices we've reached physical demand might show some signs of slowing up. Gold in rupees is expensive." Most-traded October gold on MCX has closed above June's INR19,069/kg record close in 9 of last 10 sessions; last at INR19,135/kg, equivalent to $1,316/oz; spot gold last at $1,296.10/oz. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to hold 0.8% gain as it continues to react to strength in Wall Street on Friday night, moves in Australia today, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson; "We're really haven't gone up as much as some of the offshore markets have gone up but that just seems to be what happens with our markets these days; we don't go up as much or down as much." Says \uc2 Hallenstein Glasson\uc2  (HLG.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$4.20 after announcing its net profit after tax for the 12 months ended Aug. 1 was +53% at NZ$19.6 million; "It was a relatively good result... It is an encouraging for the company." Says this is helping other retailers; Kathmandu +2.3% at NZ$1.82, Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) +1.1% at NZ$1.93. NZX-50 last 3236.73. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.4% at 9606.25 midday on rebound in exporter shares. Gains mostly seen as seasonal with Monday being last day stockholders (of record) are entitled to current round of dividend payouts. Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets, says market sentiment also supported by hopes for government \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , additional BOJ monetary easing and supplementary budget (reportedly to be compiled soon). "We are seeing buy-backs in exporter shares because there is a sense of expectation for (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar falls below 84 yen," she says. Puts Nikkei range for afternoon at 9450-9650. 29/33 Topix subindexes higher; Kyocera (6971.TO) up 3.0% at Y8,190 and \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 3.1% at Y4,380. Consumer lenders down 2.2% as a group (Topix 'other financial business' subindex) on Nikkei report Takefuji (8564.TO) may file for bankruptcy protection; (Takefuji shares remain halted). Aiful (8515.TO) off 23% at Y89. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up amid strong demand at long end as investors prefer holding such bonds for coupon earnings during week-long National Day holiday starting Friday, says Shanghai-based trader with local brokerage. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.65 vs 126.60 last close, tipped to consolidate in narrow 126.50-126.80 band before holiday. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex +0.6% at 8215.86, breaking resistance at 8200. "Stocks are still riding momentum from (China-Taiwan Economic Cooperation Framework Agreement), so the market looks likely to remain above 8100, although I think questions remain about the outlook for technology stocks beyond the third quarter," says Yuanta Securities analyst Kerry Chen. Tips market in 8100-8240 range. Tech stocks up on hot commercial sales of iPhone 4 in local market; \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +1.8% at NT$30.95, \uc2 Chimei Innolux\uc2  (3481.TW) +3.3% at NT$40.80. IPO of chip designer Nuvoton (4919.TW) +19% at NT$59.5 today, Capital Securities analyst Diana Wu calls performance "very hot" thanks to rising tech sector. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT (Dow Jones)--\uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  gains 2.9% after announcing plans to invest over A$600 million in its Bunnings hardware store chain in New South Wales state. The stock leads gains among consumer and staples, with the broader S&P/ASX 200 up 1.4% at 4667.5 points in mid-morning trade. \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  said the money will be invested in 18 metropolitan, regional sites over the next three years. Other big movers outside financials in morning trade are BHP, up 1.6%, \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . (owner of Dow Jones Newswires), up 2.9%, and \uc2 Telstra\uc2 , up 0.8%. Traders see the index maintaining new technical levels, with James Foulsham, head of dealing at CMC Markets, eyeing 4800 points for the S&P/ASX 200. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.0% at 22,348.94, off fresh 8-month high 22,396.58, with next resistance expected at its year-to-date high of 22,671 (hit January 11). Core Pacific-Yamaichi notes in near term, stock market likely to strengthen further on more upward momentum in first-hand housing transaction price trend in HK. Tips HSI in 21,800-22,600 range this week, while H-share index in 11,900-12,600 range. H-share index +1.3% at 12,373.26. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) +1.4% at HK$81.75 as uncertainty cleared after power struggle for bank's top post ends with news current CFO Douglas Flint to succeed Stephen Green as chairman. HKEx (0388.HK) +2.8% at HK$152, with shares supported by news Brazil's Vale, world's largest iron-ore producer, says in regulatory filing it plans to list shares in HK in form of depositary receipts. Volume brisk at HK$7.87 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2257ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r00024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
