\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: NZX-50 +0.8%, Likely To Hold Gains - HHG\b0\par\par\uc2 1,587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 04:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to hold 0.8% gain as it continues to react to strength in Wall Street on Friday night, moves in Australia today, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson; "We're really haven't gone up as much as some of the offshore markets have gone up but that just seems to be what happens with our markets these days; we don't go up as much or down as much." Says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hallenstein Glasson\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HLG.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$4.20 after announcing its net profit after tax for the 12 months ended Aug. 1 was +53% at NZ$19.6 million; "It was a relatively good result... It is an encouraging for the company." Says this is helping other retailers; Kathmandu +2.3% at NZ$1.82, Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) +1.1% at NZ$1.93. NZX-50 last 3236.73. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 1.4% at 9606.25 midday on rebound in exporter shares. Gains mostly seen as seasonal with Monday being last day stockholders (of record) are entitled to current round of dividend payouts. Yumi Nishimura, deputy general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets, says market sentiment also supported by hopes for government \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , additional BOJ monetary easing and supplementary budget (reportedly to be compiled soon). "We are seeing buy-backs in exporter shares because there is a sense of expectation for (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar falls below 84 yen," she says. Puts Nikkei range for afternoon at 9450-9650. 29/33 Topix subindexes higher; Kyocera (6971.TO) up 3.0% at Y8,190 and \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) up 3.1% at Y4,380. Consumer lenders down 2.2% as a group (Topix 'other financial business' subindex) on Nikkei report Takefuji (8564.TO) may file for bankruptcy protection; (Takefuji shares remain halted). Aiful (8515.TO) off 23% at Y89. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up amid strong demand at long end as investors prefer holding such bonds for coupon earnings during week-long National Day holiday starting Friday, says Shanghai-based trader with local brokerage. Shanghai Stock Exchange government bond index at 126.65 vs 126.60 last close, tipped to consolidate in narrow 126.50-126.80 band before holiday. (ming.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiex +0.6% at 8215.86, breaking resistance at 8200. "Stocks are still riding momentum from (China-Taiwan Economic Cooperation Framework Agreement), so the market looks likely to remain above 8100, although I think questions remain about the outlook for technology stocks beyond the third quarter," says Yuanta Securities analyst Kerry Chen. Tips market in 8100-8240 range. Tech stocks up on hot commercial sales of iPhone 4 in local market; \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +1.8% at NT$30.95, \uc2 Chimei Innolux\uc2  (3481.TW) +3.3% at NT$40.80. IPO of chip designer Nuvoton (4919.TW) +19% at NT$59.5 today, Capital Securities analyst Diana Wu calls performance "very hot" thanks to rising tech sector. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT (Dow Jones)--\uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  gains 2.9% after announcing plans to invest over A$600 million in its Bunnings hardware store chain in New South Wales state. The stock leads gains among consumer and staples, with the broader S&P/ASX 200 up 1.4% at 4667.5 points in mid-morning trade. \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  said the money will be invested in 18 metropolitan, regional sites over the next three years. Other big movers outside financials in morning trade are BHP, up 1.6%, \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . (owner of Dow Jones Newswires), up 2.9%, and \uc2 Telstra\uc2 , up 0.8%. Traders see the index maintaining new technical levels, with James Foulsham, head of dealing at CMC Markets, eyeing 4800 points for the S&P/ASX 200. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +1.0% at 22,348.94, off fresh 8-month high 22,396.58, with next resistance expected at its year-to-date high of 22,671 (hit January 11). Core Pacific-Yamaichi notes in near term, stock market likely to strengthen further on more upward momentum in first-hand housing transaction price trend in HK. Tips HSI in 21,800-22,600 range this week, while H-share index in 11,900-12,600 range. H-share index +1.3% at 12,373.26. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) +1.4% at HK$81.75 as uncertainty cleared after power struggle for bank's top post ends with news current CFO Douglas Flint to succeed Stephen Green as chairman. HKEx (0388.HK) +2.8% at HK$152, with shares supported by news Brazil's Vale, world's largest iron-ore producer, says in regulatory filing it plans to list shares in HK in form of depositary receipts. Volume brisk at HK$7.87 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper price more than $1,000 below pre-financial crisis record high of $8,940 hit in July 2008 so plenty of potential upside, says metals trader based in Singapore. "There's metal coming into the warehouses and some going out and that's a healthy situation. Premiums are firm for all the metals reflecting the strength of the physical market," he says. LME 3-month copper at $7,920/ton, down $24 with 1,387 lots done. SHFE December copper at CNY60,600/ton, up CNY790 and at $220 discount to equivalent LME contract. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed in tight range, as falls in U.S. Treasurys Friday mostly offset by weak gains in Taiwan shares (Taiex last +0.6%), says local brokerage trader. Notes "the government bonds may be biased down ahead of the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy) meeting" on Thursday, on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may again hike policy rates; it unexpectedly raised key policy rates at 2Q policy meeting, first tightening since June 2008. 5-year yield last at 0.8866% vs 0.8885% Friday, tipped in 0.8800%-0.8950% band for day; 10-year yield last at 1.2128% vs 1.2091%, eyed in 1.2000%-1.2200% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.5% at 1855.79 amid continued foreign buying as KRW strengthens. "U.S. economic data has been meeting expectations in September, easing worries about a global economic double dip considerably," says \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  analyst Bae Sung-young. Expects market to remain on gradual upward trend, tips 1860 resistance for day. \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE) +5.8% at KRW75,300 as expectations for bidding war between \uc2 Hyundai Motor Group\uc2  and \uc2 Hyundai Group\uc2  for controlling stake in firm up for sale lifts shares. Hyundai Merchant Marine (011200.SE) +15% at KRW49,500 and Hyundai Elevator (017800.SE) +15% at KRW80,500 also on hopes they may be subject to M&A battle depending on who winds up with \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2 ; \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  has 8.3% stake in Hyundai Merchant Marine, while Hyundai Elevator owned 20.6% in Hyundai Merchant Marine as of end 2Q. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely higher, boosted by gains on Wall Street Friday plus firmer IDR, traders say. Tip resistance at 3450 after shares last end +1.8% at record high 3397.626. "It looks like the main index is set to close at another fresh record high," says trader at Lautandhana Securities, but cautions about possibility of swift profit taking. Heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) may gain after ADRs in NY up 2.6%, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may rise on hopes for strong 9-month earnings. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.6% at 452.31 with local investors coming back to buy after state-run \uc2 Vietnam Television\uc2  reported that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon issue regulation allowing banks to boost lending, cut down interest rates. "Sentiment is also stronger because players have faith that many companies will post 3Q earnings results soon," says Hanoi-based trader. Tips key index in 450-455 range. Property stocks among most active, led by Petroland (PTL.VH), \uc2 Quoc Cuong Gia Lai\uc2  (QCG.VH), Ocean Group (OGC.VH). (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on September 15 represents an "emergency," thinks BOJ has no choice but to go to aggressive monetary easing, expects strong stock rally for advanced economies. "Aggressive monetary easing is especially likely to rev up the U.S. economy, albeit with some lag. If the U.S. starts to recover and the dollar enters a clear uptrend vs the yen, Japanese stocks stand to gain the most among the markets of advanced economies," writes analyst in report. Believes catalyst for 2005-style stock market rally going forward would involve revisions to Bank of Japan Act (set employment maximization as policy target, introduce inflation targets), aimed for proposal in Diet by Your Party. "In that case, strong improvement in market environment is likely." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2231ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r0001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal27