 H-share index +1.3% at 12,373.26. \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) +1.4% at HK$81.75 as uncertainty cleared after power struggle for bank's top post ends with news current CFO Douglas Flint to succeed Stephen Green as chairman. HKEx (0388.HK) +2.8% at HK$152, with shares supported by news Brazil's Vale, world's largest iron-ore producer, says in regulatory filing it plans to list shares in HK in form of depositary receipts. Volume brisk at HK$7.87 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0226 GMT [Dow Jones] Copper price more than $1,000 below pre-financial crisis record high of $8,940 hit in July 2008 so plenty of potential upside, says metals trader based in Singapore. "There's metal coming into the warehouses and some going out and that's a healthy situation. Premiums are firm for all the metals reflecting the strength of the physical market," he says. LME 3-month copper at $7,920/ton, down $24 with 1,387 lots done. SHFE December copper at CNY60,600/ton, up CNY790 and at $220 discount to equivalent LME contract. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed in tight range, as falls in U.S. Treasurys Friday mostly offset by weak gains in Taiwan shares (Taiex last +0.6%), says local brokerage trader. Notes "the government bonds may be biased down ahead of the (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy) meeting" on Thursday, on expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may again hike policy rates; it unexpectedly raised key policy rates at 2Q policy meeting, first tightening since June 2008. 5-year yield last at 0.8866% vs 0.8885% Friday, tipped in 0.8800%-0.8950% band for day; 10-year yield last at 1.2128% vs 1.2091%, eyed in 1.2000%-1.2200% band for day. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.5% at 1855.79 amid continued foreign buying as KRW strengthens. "U.S. economic data has been meeting expectations in September, easing worries about a global economic double dip considerably," says \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  analyst Bae Sung-young. Expects market to remain on gradual upward trend, tips 1860 resistance for day. \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE) +5.8% at KRW75,300 as expectations for bidding war between \uc2 Hyundai Motor Group\uc2  and \uc2 Hyundai Group\uc2  for controlling stake in firm up for sale lifts shares. Hyundai Merchant Marine (011200.SE) +15% at KRW49,500 and Hyundai Elevator (017800.SE) +15% at KRW80,500 also on hopes they may be subject to M&A battle depending on who winds up with \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2 ; \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  has 8.3% stake in Hyundai Merchant Marine, while Hyundai Elevator owned 20.6% in Hyundai Merchant Marine as of end 2Q. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely higher, boosted by gains on Wall Street Friday plus firmer IDR, traders say. Tip resistance at 3450 after shares last end +1.8% at record high 3397.626. "It looks like the main index is set to close at another fresh record high," says trader at Lautandhana Securities, but cautions about possibility of swift profit taking. Heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) may gain after ADRs in NY up 2.6%, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) may rise on hopes for strong 9-month earnings. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.6% at 452.31 with local investors coming back to buy after state-run \uc2 Vietnam Television\uc2  reported that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon issue regulation allowing banks to boost lending, cut down interest rates. "Sentiment is also stronger because players have faith that many companies will post 3Q earnings results soon," says Hanoi-based trader. Tips key index in 450-455 range. Property stocks among most active, led by Petroland (PTL.VH), \uc2 Quoc Cuong Gia Lai\uc2  (QCG.VH), Ocean Group (OGC.VH). (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on September 15 represents an "emergency," thinks BOJ has no choice but to go to aggressive monetary easing, expects strong stock rally for advanced economies. "Aggressive monetary easing is especially likely to rev up the U.S. economy, albeit with some lag. If the U.S. starts to recover and the dollar enters a clear uptrend vs the yen, Japanese stocks stand to gain the most among the markets of advanced economies," writes analyst in report. Believes catalyst for 2005-style stock market rally going forward would involve revisions to Bank of Japan Act (set employment maximization as policy target, introduce inflation targets), aimed for proposal in Diet by Your Party. "In that case, strong improvement in market environment is likely." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls sharply to 31.440 vs 31.620 Friday, weighed by foreign fund inflows amid Taiex's 0.6% rise, also on weaker global USD, says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in 31.400-31.600 band for day. Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in session to slow pair's drop, but "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely to reverse the (downward) trend." Expects pair to fall to 31.200 support this week. (mason.wu@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2231ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r0001w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
