\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Stk Mkt Rally Possible If BOJ Eases-Daiwa\b0\par\par\uc2 1,371 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 04:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0208 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets on September 15 represents an "emergency," thinks BOJ has no choice but to go to aggressive monetary easing, expects strong stock rally for advanced economies. "Aggressive monetary easing is especially likely to rev up the U.S. economy, albeit with some lag. If the U.S. starts to recover and the dollar enters a clear uptrend vs the yen, Japanese stocks stand to gain the most among the markets of advanced economies," writes analyst in report. Believes catalyst for 2005-style stock market rally going forward would involve revisions to Bank of Japan Act (set employment maximization as policy target, introduce inflation targets), aimed for proposal in Diet by Your Party. "In that case, strong improvement in market environment is likely." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD falls sharply to 31.440 vs 31.620 Friday, weighed by foreign fund inflows amid Taiex's 0.6% rise, also on weaker global USD, says foreign bank trader. Tips pair in 31.400-31.600 band for day. Says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in session to slow pair's drop, but "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not likely to reverse the (downward) trend." Expects pair to fall to 31.200 support this week. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares advancing, with main index piercing 4100 level early on strong overseas cues; PSEi +0.9% to 4117.02 in moderate volume with resistance still at record high of 4125 hit Sep. 21. PCCI Securities Brokers tells clients "the bullish signals from other markets" are providing local market a boost; "End-of-quarter window-dressing activities could be an additional push factor as well." Market breadth positive as gainers swamp losers 71 to 17; leading gainers: major blue chips \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH), +1.0% to PHP411, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH), +1.8% to PHP6.18. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD may fall to 1.3400 as investors take profits after pair hit 5-month high at 1.3496 Friday, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, senior dealer at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 ; adds pair may fall to 1.3300 if it breaks 1.3400, but further falls unlikely due to recent broad USD weakness in expectation of further monetary easing by Fed. Pair drops to 1.3446 low earlier, last at 1.3458. EUR/JPY falls in tandem, to intraday low at 113.41, after hitting 7-week high at 113.77 Friday; cross last 113.50. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals all dropping, surprising given lack of news flow and positive cue from Comex, which had December futures above $8,000/oz; copper down 0.5% at $7,903/ton, aluminum off 1% at $2,295, zinc down 1.6% at $2,210. Sydney-based trader eyes Chinese traders returning from 3-day holiday and taking profits after complex's dollar-driven run-up since Tuesday; copper still up 3% from that day's $7,680 close. "There's not too much out there that's warranting bad news, the physical copper market's still pretty tight and there's big drawdowns in inventories," says Sydney-based trader. "Hopefully it's a bit of profit-taking and people will start buying it back up into the session, but it's been running pretty hot so it might run down a bit." (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD lower on weaker USD offshore, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7560-7.7610 band near term. "Both local and foreign banks are eager to sell U.S. dollar on offshore cues. But the trading volume remains thin." Pair last at 7.7583 vs 7.7608 Friday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0153 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY falls moderately but stays well above 6.70 key psychological level after PBOC set fixing at 6.7098, higher for first time after 9 consecutive session of falls, much higher than around 6.6900 some traders anticipated, 6.6997 fixing Tuesday. On OTC, USD/CNY at 6.7022 vs 6.7079 Tuesday close (China markets closed Wednesday-Friday for holiday). Significantly higher-than-expected fixing could mean PBOC intends to let USD/CNY consolidate around 6.70 after CNY's 1.3% rise vs USD since Sept. 8, traders say. "But we need to monitor 1-2 more days to see if that's indeed the signal," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. "U.S.-China relations are quite sensitive right now." Still, says CNY demand stronger so far morning due to EUR's sharp gains vs USD during past few days, USD/CNY may still test 6.7000; tipped in 6.6950-6.7050 for session. Offshore 1-year USD/CNY at 6.5550/6.5620 vs 6.5833/6.5873 late Tuesday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures +0.6% at 22,345, as cash market tipped to gain, tracking U.S. stocks. Contract ends Friday at sizable premium to cash of 92 points, signaling still positive bias in market's near-term direction, even with HSI already rising for 10th session out of 11. September open interest decreases 863 lots to 103,685. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares up early, led by metals firms following strength in U.S. stocks last week. Shanghai Composite Index +0.5% at 2605.53, tipped to test resistance at 2650. "China's market is up today mainly due to strength in overseas markets last week, but it's likely to consolidate later this week ahead of the holidays because investors are still cautious," says Tu Chunhui at China Development Bank Securities. China's 7-day National Day holiday begins Friday. Among actives, Chalco (601600.SH) +1.3% at CNY9.74, Jiangxi Copper (600362.SH) +1.7% at CNY29.99. Shenzhen index +0.4% at 1148.89. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares tipped to rise as continued fund inflows amid absence of fresh negative cues from overseas bourses to underpin. Fund inflows likely to drive big caps while retail investors may switch to small, medium sized stocks, says KT Zmico Securities' First VP Charoen Iamphattanatham. Adds 3Q earning forecasts expected to flood in but market largely priced in that 3Q may be weak on seasonality. Notes if benchmark moves decisively above 960, SET Index projected to hit 1,000 points near term. Support put at 940. SET Index ended +0.5% at fresh 14-year-closing high of 951.90 Friday. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0145 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR tad lower at 8,950 vs 8,955, with suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank Indonesia slowing pair's slide, dealers say. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to have bought around $50 million at 8,950 via state banks, 3 dealers say. "Besides (suspected) Bank Indonesia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar seems to be getting support around the current level, from demand from importers," dealer says; 8,940-8,960 range tipped. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.5% at 1458.35, boosted by gains in U.S. stocks, regional markets; but further upside limited by dearth of fresh leads, dealers say. "The KLCI will probably consolidate in the 1450 to 1465 range for now as players wait for clearer direction." Among gainers, Gamuda (5398.KU) +2.1% at MYR3.92 on optimism it may clinch government approval for major rail project, AMMB (1015.KU) +2.1% at MYR5.78, Genting (3182.KU) +1.1% at MYR9.86. Among decliners, Gopeng (2135.KU) down 3.0% at MYR1.29 after agreeing to sell its stake in cement company Perak-Hanjoong - a major profit contributor - to \uc2 YTL Cement\uc2  (8737.KU). (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2207ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r0001i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
