\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:HK CPI Tilted To Upside, May Push Ppty Prices - CS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 03:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0125 GMT [Dow Jones] HK's inflation risk "is tilted to the upside," may surprise market over next 18 months, warns \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 ; expects higher imported food prices from China (1% change in China food CPI estimated to contribute 0.25 ppt to HK's headline CPI inflation), among others, to push up CPI in months to come - raises end-2011 CPI inflation forecast to 5.3% on year vs 4.2% previously. Adds higher inflation expected to turn real interest rate even more negative in future, pushing HK's deposit base into asset markets, in particular property market. Without ability to alter HK's monetary policy, house believes government has to do a lot more administratively to get property market under control, but "there is no guarantee that these measures would be successful." (robert.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0123 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says coming weeks warrant attention on U.S. corporate profit warnings; notes firms typically publish profit warnings during several weeks around end and beginning of a quarter, while a series of earnings downgrades often trigger sell-offs in shares. "As such, our eyes are on announcements from U.S. companies over the coming weeks," writes analyst in report, alluding to possible subsequent fallout on Japan shares. Adds, however, weakness on profit warnings might provide good entry opportunity (for equities generally), given stocks tend to bottom during earnings season as negatives price in. Topix up 0.7% at 843.84, up 0.3% for quarter ending September, Nikkei up 1.2% at 9585.28, up 2.2% for July-September.(bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI set to rise after Wall Street rally Friday, with index expected to test April high of 22,388 near-term, if not today, says Mark To at Wing Fung Financial; index closed +0.3% at 22,119.43 Friday. Expects volume to increase in run-up to end of 3Q due to window-dressing activities, continued liquidity inflows. Tips index may rise further in October to test above 23,000. Expects \uc2 HSBC\uc2  (0005.HK) to rise as uncertainty cleared after power struggle for bank's top post ends with current CFO Douglas Flint to succeed Stephen Green as chairman. Tips stock may test HK$82.50 within days after closing Friday down 0.6% at HK$80.60. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, with sentiment likely buoyed by strong overseas cues; pair may initially pierce 4100 barrier after ending 0.3% higher to 4078.87 Friday, next resistance likely at 4125, index's record high. Support pegged at 4050. "The Dow closed strongly Friday and gold prices are at record highs," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. Likely to again lead market rise are mining stocks, particularly \uc2 Philex Mining\uc2  (PX.PH), country's most profitable gold, copper producer. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] JGB yields should keep falling for now as additional JGB issuance for this fiscal year unlikely at least at this point, also BOJ tankan survey Wednesday may suggest Japan's deflationary pressure strengthening again due to rising inventories, says RBS Securities chief bond strategist Akito Fukunaga. Adds, investors should also watch if government increases pressure on BOJ to ease policy further; 10-year yield not traded yet, closed at 0.995% Friday; December futures last down 0.04 at 142.84.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 9-session high $76.85/bbl this morning, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; meanwhile, 5-day moving average staging bullish crossover against 15-day moving average. Resistance at $77.47 (200-day moving average); breach would expose upside to $78.04 (September 13 reaction high), then $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 August 4-August 25 decline) and psychological $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). Support at $75.36 (hourly chart), then at $74.66 (Friday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to $73.58 (Thursday's low), then $72.75 (September 17 reaction low), $72.63 (September 7 low) and $71.53 (August 31 reaction low). November crude last up 31 cents at $76.80/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.68 vs 30.70-30.72 late Friday in thin trade, tracking broader USD fall vs Asians. Pair biased downward on continued risk appetite, players seeking higher yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while more fund inflows expected as Thai stock market tipped to rise, says local dealer. "Look at the yen (which is still stronger against the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )...hopefully, the baht won't slip as quickly as previously"; expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be in market only to curb volatility. Pair tipped 30.60-30.70 band. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.3% at 4660.5, with financials, materials and consumer staples stocks leading gains as the market reacts to a strong close on Wall Street that saw the DJIA cap a fourth straight week of gains. In financials, bellwethers ANZ (ANZ.AU) up 2.0%, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) up 1.7%, Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU) up 1.6% and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 1.5%. James Foulsham, head of dealing at CMC Markets, says the ASX is being driven by "the lead we got from the U.S", adds it's a "positive sign the market can hold these levels and maybe take the next step." He says a target of 4800 over the next month is possible for the index given the receding fear of a double dip recession in the U.S. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to rise after ending down 0.4% at 8166.62 on Friday. With gains in U.S. stocks on Friday, "look for the Taiex to consolidate above critical resistance at 8200," says KGI Securities' Randy Chang. Tips shares in 8200-8250 range. Adds techs likely strong, especially touch panels, on hot commercial sales of iPhone 4 in local market. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall to 1,146.9 from 1,147.9 earlier, vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul as offshore players sell USD actively amid broadly weaker dollar of late. But local traders peg support at 1,145 amid high chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities near that level to stem KRW gains. "The pair will likely continue trading in the 1,140s area for the rest of the day," says local bank trader. Adds, possible USD buying by companies relating to recent M&A deals may provide support, "but they aren't likely to provide enough momentum to reverse the dominating short-dollar bias in the market." Kospi +0.5%, also weighing on USD/KRW. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower, taking cue from weak sentiment for USD; pair may again slide below 44 after settling late Friday at 44.09, with support at 43.88, resistance at 44.20. "Aside from strong funds inflow into the country, globally investor sentiment remains weak for the dollar because of the potential for QE (quantitative easing) from the Fed," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Foreign Minister Maehara's interview with WSJ Friday suggests Tokyo comfortable with international reaction so far to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to stay in touch with U.S. and European countries on potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market; "Going forward, there may be a possibility for the Japanese government to show its very determined intent." Says JPY situation not similar to China, where U.S. pressing for CNY revaluation, since "the yen has strengthened, more than indicated by the actual strength of the Japanese economy." (willam.sposato@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2125ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
