\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Philippine Shares Tipped To Rise; 4100 Resistance\b0\par\par\uc2 1,404 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 03:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0122 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares tipped higher, with sentiment likely buoyed by strong overseas cues; pair may initially pierce 4100 barrier after ending 0.3% higher to 4078.87 Friday, next resistance likely at 4125, index's record high. Support pegged at 4050. "The Dow closed strongly Friday and gold prices are at record highs," says Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities. Likely to again lead market rise are mining stocks, particularly \f28 \uc2 \f2 Philex Mining\f28 \uc2 \f2  (PX.PH), country's most profitable gold, copper producer. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] JGB yields should keep falling for now as additional JGB issuance for this fiscal year unlikely at least at this point, also BOJ tankan survey Wednesday may suggest Japan's deflationary pressure strengthening again due to rising inventories, says RBS Securities chief bond strategist Akito Fukunaga. Adds, investors should also watch if government increases pressure on BOJ to ease policy further; 10-year yield not traded yet, closed at 0.995% Friday; December futures last down 0.04 at 142.84.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0110 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to consolidate with risks skewed to upside after hitting 9-session high $76.85/bbl this morning, Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart positive-biased as MACD & stochastics in bullish mode; meanwhile, 5-day moving average staging bullish crossover against 15-day moving average. Resistance at $77.47 (200-day moving average); breach would expose upside to $78.04 (September 13 reaction high), then $78.30 (61.8% Fibonacci correction of $82.97-$70.76 August 4-August 25 decline) and psychological $80.00 (just beneath previous base set Aug. 6). Support at $75.36 (hourly chart), then at $74.66 (Friday's low); breach would temper near-term positive outlook, exposing downside to $73.58 (Thursday's low), then $72.75 (September 17 reaction low), $72.63 (September 7 low) and $71.53 (August 31 reaction low). November crude last up 31 cents at $76.80/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.68 vs 30.70-30.72 late Friday in thin trade, tracking broader USD fall vs Asians. Pair biased downward on continued risk appetite, players seeking higher yield \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while more fund inflows expected as Thai stock market tipped to rise, says local dealer. "Look at the yen (which is still stronger against the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )...hopefully, the baht won't slip as quickly as previously"; expects \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be in market only to curb volatility. Pair tipped 30.60-30.70 band. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.3% at 4660.5, with financials, materials and consumer staples stocks leading gains as the market reacts to a strong close on Wall Street that saw the DJIA cap a fourth straight week of gains. In financials, bellwethers ANZ (ANZ.AU) up 2.0%, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) up 1.7%, Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU) up 1.6% and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 1.5%. James Foulsham, head of dealing at CMC Markets, says the ASX is being driven by "the lead we got from the U.S", adds it's a "positive sign the market can hold these levels and maybe take the next step." He says a target of 4800 over the next month is possible for the index given the receding fear of a double dip recession in the U.S. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to rise after ending down 0.4% at 8166.62 on Friday. With gains in U.S. stocks on Friday, "look for the Taiex to consolidate above critical resistance at 8200," says KGI Securities' Randy Chang. Tips shares in 8200-8250 range. Adds techs likely strong, especially touch panels, on hot commercial sales of iPhone 4 in local market. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall to 1,146.9 from 1,147.9 earlier, vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul as offshore players sell USD actively amid broadly weaker dollar of late. But local traders peg support at 1,145 amid high chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities near that level to stem KRW gains. "The pair will likely continue trading in the 1,140s area for the rest of the day," says local bank trader. Adds, possible USD buying by companies relating to recent M&A deals may provide support, "but they aren't likely to provide enough momentum to reverse the dominating short-dollar bias in the market." Kospi +0.5%, also weighing on USD/KRW. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower, taking cue from weak sentiment for USD; pair may again slide below 44 after settling late Friday at 44.09, with support at 43.88, resistance at 44.20. "Aside from strong funds inflow into the country, globally investor sentiment remains weak for the dollar because of the potential for QE (quantitative easing) from the Fed," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Foreign Minister Maehara's interview with WSJ Friday suggests Tokyo comfortable with international reaction so far to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to stay in touch with U.S. and European countries on potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market; "Going forward, there may be a possibility for the Japanese government to show its very determined intent." Says JPY situation not similar to China, where U.S. pressing for CNY revaluation, since "the yen has strengthened, more than indicated by the actual strength of the Japanese economy." (willam.sposato@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to fall to fresh low under current system after long holiday (China markets were closed Wednesday-Friday) on euro's strength vs USD during period; USD/CNY central parity expected at new record low around 6.6900 vs previous low of 6.6997 Tuesday; USD/CNY tipped in 6.6900-6.7050 vs 6.7079 Tuesday close; "given the pattern before the holiday, the dollar-yuan will fall further today," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. "But I think the pair should consolidate around the key level of 6.70 after its recent sharp fall." Adds if USD/CNY breaks resolutely below 6.70, it may trigger wave of exporter selling. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's exports, main driver of economic recovery, slowing down and "the idea of a V-shaped recovery based solely on the export sector is no longer a credible scenario," says Japan Research Institute chief economist Hidehiko Fujii. Adds, exports slowdown in part due to recent JPY strength, which reduces exports in JPY terms. "It is increasingly uncertain what will lead Japan's economic recovery...the outlook for the Japanese economy is severe," he says. Japan exports +15.8% on year in August to Y5.224 trillion; fall short of +17.9% forecast in Dow Jones poll of economists. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.5% at 1856.17, led by techs after recent falls, amid foreign buying; "foreign investors seem to have an interest in non-dollar assets due to the depreciation of the dollar and a stronger won is benefiting big importers and companies with heavy dollar debt," says Kim Jung-hoon at \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 . \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.4% at KRW765,000, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) +3.1% at KRW21,750. Foreigners net buyers of KRW33 billion of stocks, while domestic institutions net sellers of KRW6.2 billion, retail investors net sellers of KRW15.9 billion. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.6% at KRW159,000, weighed by strong won, news over weekend that it will recall some 140,000 Sonata sedans in U.S. market; other auto plays in negative territory too, with \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) down 0.8% at KRW36,500, \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) down 1.6% at KRW9,350. Kospi resistance tipped at 1860. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2122ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
