 on euro's strength vs USD during period; USD/CNY central parity expected at new record low around 6.6900 vs previous low of 6.6997 Tuesday; USD/CNY tipped in 6.6900-6.7050 vs 6.7079 Tuesday close; "given the pattern before the holiday, the dollar-yuan will fall further today," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. "But I think the pair should consolidate around the key level of 6.70 after its recent sharp fall." Adds if USD/CNY breaks resolutely below 6.70, it may trigger wave of exporter selling. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's exports, main driver of economic recovery, slowing down and "the idea of a V-shaped recovery based solely on the export sector is no longer a credible scenario," says Japan Research Institute chief economist Hidehiko Fujii. Adds, exports slowdown in part due to recent JPY strength, which reduces exports in JPY terms. "It is increasingly uncertain what will lead Japan's economic recovery...the outlook for the Japanese economy is severe," he says. Japan exports +15.8% on year in August to Y5.224 trillion; fall short of +17.9% forecast in Dow Jones poll of economists. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.5% at 1856.17, led by techs after recent falls, amid foreign buying; "foreign investors seem to have an interest in non-dollar assets due to the depreciation of the dollar and a stronger won is benefiting big importers and companies with heavy dollar debt," says Kim Jung-hoon at \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 . \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.4% at KRW765,000, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) +3.1% at KRW21,750. Foreigners net buyers of KRW33 billion of stocks, while domestic institutions net sellers of KRW6.2 billion, retail investors net sellers of KRW15.9 billion. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.6% at KRW159,000, weighed by strong won, news over weekend that it will recall some 140,000 Sonata sedans in U.S. market; other