\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/THB Lower; Biased Down On Risk Appetite\b0\par\par\uc2 1,446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 03:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.68 vs 30.70-30.72 late Friday in thin trade, tracking broader USD fall vs Asians. Pair biased downward on continued risk appetite, players seeking higher yield \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  while more fund inflows expected as Thai stock market tipped to rise, says local dealer. "Look at the yen (which is still stronger against the dollar despite \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 )...hopefully, the baht won't slip as quickly as previously"; expects \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be in market only to curb volatility. Pair tipped 30.60-30.70 band. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0108 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 1.3% at 4660.5, with financials, materials and consumer staples stocks leading gains as the market reacts to a strong close on Wall Street that saw the DJIA cap a fourth straight week of gains. In financials, bellwethers ANZ (ANZ.AU) up 2.0%, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  (NAB.AU) up 1.7%, Commonwealth Bank (CBA.AU) up 1.6% and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 1.5%. James Foulsham, head of dealing at CMC Markets, says the ASX is being driven by "the lead we got from the U.S", adds it's a "positive sign the market can hold these levels and maybe take the next step." He says a target of 4800 over the next month is possible for the index given the receding fear of a double dip recession in the U.S. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to rise after ending down 0.4% at 8166.62 on Friday. With gains in U.S. stocks on Friday, "look for the Taiex to consolidate above critical resistance at 8200," says KGI Securities' Randy Chang. Tips shares in 8200-8250 range. Adds techs likely strong, especially touch panels, on hot commercial sales of iPhone 4 in local market. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall to 1,146.9 from 1,147.9 earlier, vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul as offshore players sell USD actively amid broadly weaker dollar of late. But local traders peg support at 1,145 amid high chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities near that level to stem KRW gains. "The pair will likely continue trading in the 1,140s area for the rest of the day," says local bank trader. Adds, possible USD buying by companies relating to recent M&A deals may provide support, "but they aren't likely to provide enough momentum to reverse the dominating short-dollar bias in the market." Kospi +0.5%, also weighing on USD/KRW. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower, taking cue from weak sentiment for USD; pair may again slide below 44 after settling late Friday at 44.09, with support at 43.88, resistance at 44.20. "Aside from strong funds inflow into the country, globally investor sentiment remains weak for the dollar because of the potential for QE (quantitative easing) from the Fed," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Foreign Minister Maehara's interview with WSJ Friday suggests Tokyo comfortable with international reaction so far to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to stay in touch with U.S. and European countries on potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight2