\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan Shares Tipped To Breach 8200 Resistance\b0\par\par\uc2 1,397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 02:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to rise after ending down 0.4% at 8166.62 on Friday. With gains in U.S. stocks on Friday, "look for the Taiex to consolidate above critical resistance at 8200," says KGI Securities' Randy Chang. Tips shares in 8200-8250 range. Adds techs likely strong, especially touch panels, on hot commercial sales of iPhone 4 in local market. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW extends fall to 1,146.9 from 1,147.9 earlier, vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul as offshore players sell USD actively amid broadly weaker dollar of late. But local traders peg support at 1,145 amid high chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities near that level to stem KRW gains. "The pair will likely continue trading in the 1,140s area for the rest of the day," says local bank trader. Adds, possible USD buying by companies relating to recent M&A deals may provide support, "but they aren't likely to provide enough momentum to reverse the dominating short-dollar bias in the market." Kospi +0.5%, also weighing on USD/KRW. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0054 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower, taking cue from weak sentiment for USD; pair may again slide below 44 after settling late Friday at 44.09, with support at 43.88, resistance at 44.20. "Aside from strong funds inflow into the country, globally investor sentiment remains weak for the dollar because of the potential for QE (quantitative easing) from the Fed," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Foreign Minister Maehara's interview with WSJ Friday suggests Tokyo comfortable with international reaction so far to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to stay in touch with U.S. and European countries on potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market; "Going forward, there may be a possibility for the Japanese government to show its very determined intent." Says JPY situation not similar to China, where U.S. pressing for CNY revaluation, since "the yen has strengthened, more than indicated by the actual strength of the Japanese economy." (willam.sposato@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0050 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to fall to fresh low under current system after long holiday (China markets were closed Wednesday-Friday) on euro's strength vs USD during period; USD/CNY central parity expected at new record low around 6.6900 vs previous low of 6.6997 Tuesday; USD/CNY tipped in 6.6900-6.7050 vs 6.7079 Tuesday close; "given the pattern before the holiday, the dollar-yuan will fall further today," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. "But I think the pair should consolidate around the key level of 6.70 after its recent sharp fall." Adds if USD/CNY breaks resolutely below 6.70, it may trigger wave of exporter selling. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's exports, main driver of economic recovery, slowing down and "the idea of a V-shaped recovery based solely on the export sector is no longer a credible scenario," says Japan Research Institute chief economist Hidehiko Fujii. Adds, exports slowdown in part due to recent JPY strength, which reduces exports in JPY terms. "It is increasingly uncertain what will lead Japan's economic recovery...the outlook for the Japanese economy is severe," he says. Japan exports +15.8% on year in August to Y5.224 trillion; fall short of +17.9% forecast in Dow Jones poll of economists. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.5% at 1856.17, led by techs after recent falls, amid foreign buying; "foreign investors seem to have an interest in non-dollar assets due to the depreciation of the dollar and a stronger won is benefiting big importers and companies with heavy dollar debt," says Kim Jung-hoon at \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 . \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.4% at KRW765,000, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) +3.1% at KRW21,750. Foreigners net buyers of KRW33 billion of stocks, while domestic institutions net sellers of KRW6.2 billion, retail investors net sellers of KRW15.9 billion. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.6% at KRW159,000, weighed by strong won, news over weekend that it will recall some 140,000 Sonata sedans in U.S. market; other auto plays in negative territory too, with \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) down 0.8% at KRW36,500, \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) down 1.6% at KRW9,350. Kospi resistance tipped at 1860. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may rise, tracing strong rally in U.S. stocks last week while China on break during Mid-Autumn Festival holiday. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to test resistance at 2650, after ending up 0.1% at 2591.55 last Tuesday. "Trading volume will likely be thin again this week ahead of the National Day holiday, but the Shanghai index will see solid support from international markets," says Amy Lin at Capital Securities. China will go on 7-day break starting Friday. DJIA +2.4% last week for 4th straight positive week on signs of stabilization in durable goods, encouraging U.S. housing data. Shenzhen index last ends flat at 1144.55. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.9% at 9556.42 following Friday's U.S. stock rally and hopes BOJ will carry out additional easing measures. "There is a possibility that the BOJ will make a move (for monetary easing) during next month's policy meeting," which would (likely) temporarily weaken the yen, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. Notes government may also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week before companies close books at month-end. Adds if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumors spread today, speculation could push Nikkei to 9700 level. 26/33 Topix subindexes higher, with exporters rising; Canon (7751.TO) up 1.4% at Y3,850 and \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) up 1.3% at Y2,971. Topix "other financial business" subindex (home to consumer lenders) down 3.4% on Nikkei report Takefuji (8564.TO) may file for bankruptcy protection. TSE has halted Takefuji shares, but several sector peers off sharply; Aiful (8515.TO) down 17% at Y95, Acom (8572.TO) off 13% at Y1,296. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, buoyed by rally in U.S. stocks Friday. KLCI lower for a 3rd straight session Friday, down 0.5% at 1451.19 Friday. "Following the market's correction late last week, investors may return today to do some bargain hunting as the long-term outlook remains bullish," dealer says. TA Research says for this week, KLCI likely to face resistance at 1479, with support at 1424. Techs such as chipmaker Unisem (5005.KU) may rise, tracking gains in U.S. counterparts. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW breaches 1,150 support at market open, slips to 1,147.9 vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul, tracking broad USD weakness late last week, traders say. Local bank trader pegs next support at 1,145. "With the dollar's fall in the global market picking up speed, while domestic fundamentals remain favorable including the strong current account surplus trend and rising \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the KRW1,150 level may seem too high," says another local bank trader. Players likely to remain increasingly wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities as pair currently trading around lowest levels in more than 4 months, traders note. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR tad lower at 3.0900 vs 3.0920 late Friday on broad USD weakness; dealers tip pair to extend declines to reach fresh 13-year low. "Globally, sentiment towards the dollar is still rather weak. The immediate 3.0900 support level will probably be broken," dealer says; tips next support at 3.0750, but adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suspected of buying USD in recent sessions, may emerge again to slow MYR's rise. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2056ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
