re the holiday, the dollar-yuan will fall further today," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. "But I think the pair should consolidate around the key level of 6.70 after its recent sharp fall." Adds if USD/CNY breaks resolutely below 6.70, it may trigger wave of exporter selling. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0049 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's exports, main driver of economic recovery, slowing down and "the idea of a V-shaped recovery based solely on the export sector is no longer a credible scenario," says Japan Research Institute chief economist Hidehiko Fujii. Adds, exports slowdown in part due to recent JPY strength, which reduces exports in JPY terms. "It is increasingly uncertain what will lead Japan's economic recovery...the outlook for the Japanese economy is severe," he says. Japan exports +15.8% on year in August to Y5.224 trillion; fall short of +17.9% forecast in Dow Jones poll of economists. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi last +0.5% at 1856.17, led by techs after recent falls, amid foreign buying; "foreign investors seem to have an interest in non-dollar assets due to the depreciation of the dollar and a stronger won is benefiting big importers and companies with heavy dollar debt," says Kim Jung-hoon at \uc2 Korea Investment & Securities\uc2 . \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) +0.4% at KRW765,000, \uc2 Hynix Semiconductor\uc2  (000660.SE) +3.1% at KRW21,750. Foreigners net buyers of KRW33 billion of stocks, while domestic institutions net sellers of KRW6.2 billion, retail investors net sellers of KRW15.9 billion. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) down 1.6% at KRW159,000, weighed by strong won, news over weekend that it will recall some 140,000 Sonata sedans in U.S. market; other auto plays in negative territory too, with \uc2 Kia Motors\uc2  (000270.SE) down 0.8% at KRW36,500, \uc2 Ssangyong Motor\uc2  (003620.SE) down 1.6% at KRW9,350. Kospi resistance tipped at 1860. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0045 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares may rise, tracing strong rally in U.S. stocks last week while China on break during Mid-Autumn Festival holiday. Shanghai Composite Index tipped to test resistance at 2650, after ending up 0.1% at 2591.55 last Tuesday. "Trading volume will likely be thin again this week ahead of the National Day holiday, but the Shanghai index will see solid support from international markets," says Amy Lin at Capital Securities. China will go on 7-day break starting Friday. DJIA +2.4% last week for 4th straight positive week on signs of stabilization in durable goods, encouraging U.S. housing data. Shenzhen index last ends flat at 1144.55. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 0.9% at 9556.42 following Friday's U.S. stock rally and hopes BOJ will carry out additional easing measures. "There is a possibility that the BOJ will make a move (for monetary easing) during next month's policy meeting," which would (likely) temporarily weaken the yen, says \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. Notes government may also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week before companies close books at month-end. Adds if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rumors spread today, speculation could push Nikkei to 9700 level. 26/33 Topix subindexes higher, with exporters rising; Canon (7751.TO) up 1.4% at Y3,850 and \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  (7267.TO) up 1.3% at Y2,971. Topix "other financial business" subindex (home to consumer lenders) down 3.4% on Nikkei report Takefuji (8564.TO) may file for bankruptcy protection. TSE has halted Takefuji shares, but several sector peers off sharply; Aiful (8515.TO) down 17% at Y95, Acom (8572.TO) off 13% at Y1,296. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise, buoyed by rally in U.S. stocks Friday. KLCI lower for a 3rd straight session Friday, down 0.5% at 1451.19 Friday. "Following the market's correction late last week, investors may return today to do some bargain hunting as the long-term outlook remains bullish," dealer says. TA Research says for this week, KLCI likely to face resistance at 1479, with support at 1424. Techs such as chipmaker Unisem (5005.KU) may rise, tracking gains in U.S. counterparts. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW breaches 1,150 support at market open, slips to 1,147.9 vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul, tracking broad USD weakness late last week, traders say. Local bank trader pegs next support at 1,145. "With the dollar's fall in the global market picking up speed, while domestic fundamentals remain favorable including the strong current account surplus trend and rising \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the KRW1,150 level may seem too high," says another local bank trader. Players likely to remain increasingly wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities as pair currently trading around lowest levels in more than 4 months, traders note. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR tad lower at 3.0900 vs 3.0920 late Friday on broad USD weakness; dealers tip pair to extend declines to reach fresh 13-year low. "Globally, sentiment towards the dollar is still rather weak. The immediate 3.0900 support level will probably be broken," dealer says; tips next support at 3.0750, but adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suspected of buying USD in recent sessions, may emerge again to slow MYR's rise. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0017 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares expected to start final week of 3Q10 on firm note following Wall Street's rally Friday. If year-to-date high of 3116 gives way, next resistance for STI expected at 3146 (higher end of breakdown gap formed in June 2008); index closed +0.3% at 3092.68 Friday, +4.8% so far this month. "It looks like the market still has room to head higher. The bias for the rest of the week could remain positive as shares could be propped up by end-of-quarter window dressing," says trader at local brokerage. \uc2 China XLX Fertiliser\uc2  (B9R.SG) may be in focus with company unveiling CNY3 billion investment in 4th production plant; shares down 0.9% at S$0.58 Friday. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs higher but sharp gains unlikely as benchmark 10-year yield trades below psychological level of 1.00%, luring some profit-taking orders from speculators, but trend remains positive for JGBs due to expectations for BOJ to ease policy further to cope with slowing economic growth, strong JPY, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. Says BOJ's tankan survey, industrial output data due this week may hint Japan economy will fall into lull from October, tailwind for JGBs, he says. Tips December futures to trade in 142.60-143.10 vs last 142.91, up 0.03 while 10-year yield may trade in 0.970%-1.030% vs last 0.995%. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2054ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
