cking gains in U.S. counterparts. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0025 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW breaches 1,150 support at market open, slips to 1,147.9 vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul, tracking broad USD weakness late last week, traders say. Local bank trader pegs next support at 1,145. "With the dollar's fall in the global market picking up speed, while domestic fundamentals remain favorable including the strong current account surplus trend and rising \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the KRW1,150 level may seem too high," says another local bank trader. Players likely to remain increasingly wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by authorities as pair currently trading around lowest levels in more than 4 months, traders note. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0024 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR tad lower at 3.0900 vs 3.0920 late Friday on broad USD weakness; dealers tip pair to extend declines to reach fresh 13-year low. "Globally, sentiment towards the dollar is still rather weak. The immediate 3.0900 support level will probably be broken," dealer says; tips next support at 3.0750, but adds \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suspected of buying USD in recent sessions, may emerge again to slow MYR's rise. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0017 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares expected to start final week of 3Q10 on firm note following Wall Street's rally Friday. If year-to-date high of 3116 gives way, next resistance for STI expected at 3146 (higher end of breakdown gap formed in June 2008); index closed +0.3% at 3092.68 Friday, +4.8% so far this month. "It looks like the market still has room to head higher. The bias for the rest of the week could remain positive as shares could be propped up by end-of-quarter window dressing," says trader at local brokerage. \uc2 China XLX Fertiliser\uc2  (B9R.SG) may be in focus with company unveiling CNY3 billion investment in 4th production plant; shares down 0.9% at S$0.58 Friday. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs higher but sharp gains unlikely as benchmark 10-year yield trades below psychological level of 1.00%, luring some profit-taking orders from speculators, but trend remains positive for JGBs due to expectations for BOJ to ease policy further to cope with slowing economic growth, strong JPY, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. Says BOJ's tankan survey, industrial output data due this week may hint Japan economy will fall into lull from October, tailwind for JGBs, he says. Tips December futures to trade in 142.60-143.10 vs last 142.91, up 0.03 while 10-year yield may trade in 0.970%-1.030% vs last 0.995%. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0009 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely higher, but rangebound as investors bide time until auction of MYR3.0 billion 3-year Islamic bonds due Thursday. Dealer says "the ringgit appreciation theme will continue to be supportive for bonds, though upside will be rather limited in the near term due to the lack of leads." Market participants also cautious after previous auction in August (MYR3.0 billion 5-year MGS) drew slightly disappointing investor demand. On Friday, yields on benchmark MGSs end mixed, with May 2013 flat at 3.16%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.28%, September 2017 down 1 bp at 3.53%, November 2019 down 2 bps at 3.61%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 2050ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100927e69r0000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
