\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: BOJ Governor Offers Backing For  \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 12:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comments from Shirakawa, vice finance minister on buying bonds, background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Tomoyuki Tachikawa \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 OSAKA, Japan -(Dow Jones)- Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa pledged Monday to take "appropriate policy steps" if the strong yen seriously hits Japan's economy, his strongest statement yet on the BOJ's potential willingness to help the government's drive to push the yen lower.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to a group of business leaders in the western city of Osaka, he said the strong yen can "significantly affect business sentiment," adding that "the BOJ will take policy steps in a timely and appropriate manner if (the yen's rise) becomes a downside risk to the economy."\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa's comments had been closely watched to see if he would offer any clues to further easing steps from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The BOJ policy board is to meet Oct. 4-5. Any new steps would likely come from that session.\par \par \uc2 At a press conference later in the day, Shirakawa vowed to look into the negative impact of the yen's climb on Japan's economy at the policy board meeting.\par \par \uc2 "The appreciation of the yen has an adverse effect on the economy through business sentiment. At the same time, it is important to assess how the global economy--which is the cause of the yen's rise--will develop down the road," he said.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has come under increasing political pressure to play a larger role in revitalizing the economy, such as by setting a direct inflation target or undertaking large-scale buying of government debt.\par \par \uc2 Vice Finance Minister Mitsuru Sakurai raised the issue of bond buying at a news conference Monday. The BOJ "has made significant efforts, but there are various steps (it could still take), such as buying more long-term government bonds," he said.\par \par \uc2 In response, Shirakawa and other BOJ board members have offered some additional steps aimed at increasing the supply of money to the economy but have continued to oppose more radical proposals.\par \par \uc2 In his speech Monday, Shirakawa offered few new hints on further policy steps but did repeat the BOJ's view that "Japan's economy shows further signs of a moderate recovery," wording that would appear to lessen the chance of stronger action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Later, at his press conference, Shirakawa kept mum about whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would augment its Japanese government bond purchases, saying only that he is aware of the "merits" of JGB buying.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ is making use of the JGB purchases as a tool to provide adequate funds to the market," Shirakawa said, but warned that "long-term yields could rise if the JGB buying is regarded as a sign that the BOJ is trying to finance fiscal spending."\par \par \uc2 The BOJ purchases Y1.8 trillion of JGBs each month.\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa's frequent references to Japan's exchange rate dilemma took some BOJ watchers by surprise. The government has identified the strong yen as a major hindrance to the export-dependent corporate sector. On Sept. 15 it began what it has said will be an ongoing campaign to push the yen lower through direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Noting this, Shirakawa said the BOJ "has great interest in and will pay attention to developments in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and their impact."\par \par \uc2 -By Tomoyuki Tachikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2787; tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com [ 09-27-10 0648ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020100927e69r0002m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
