\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Close Lower After Late-Day Selloff\b0\par\par\uc2 2,590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2010\par 00:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10812.30  -47.91  -0.44% \par Nasdaq     2369.77  -11.45  -0.48% \par S&P 500    1142.22   -6.44  -0.56% \par FTSE 100   5573.42  -25.06  -0.45% \par Xetra DAX  6278.89  -19.41  -0.31% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 29/32  +1/32 0.428% +1.6BP \par 5Y Tsy   99 27/32 +10/32 1.283% +6.6BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 29/32 +24/32 2.521% +8.6BP \par  \par USD/JPY       84.32-34      0.00% \par Range         84.39-84.12 \par EUR/USD       1.3443-45    -0.30% \par Range         1.3506-1.3426 \par AUD/USD       0.9604-09    -0.12% \par Range         0.9644-0.9575 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.55-10.85% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    11.70-12.00% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    13.35-13.65% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,298.60 +50 cents \par Comex Gold Range    $1,295.80-$1,301.30 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $76.52   +$0.03 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks slipped Monday as concerns over the health of financials prompted investors to take a step back after four consecutive weeks of gains.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 47.91 points, or 0.44%, at 10812.3. On Friday, the Dow rallied 1.9% to its highest close since May 12. The Dow is up 8.5% this month, on pace for its best September performance since 1939. But some investors have become wary of the market's rapid ascent.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think the catalyst exists for any kind of meaningful move higher," said Peter Cecchini, chief strategist at BGC Financial. "I don't think the economic data has been as good as it's been portrayed."\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite closed down 11.45, or 0.48% to 2369.77, while the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-stock index shed 6.44, or 0.56% to 1142.22, after failing to climb above 1149, a key technical level for the measure.\par \par \uc2 Financial stocks weakened Monday after Moody's Investors Service downgraded the debt of \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2  by three notches because the bank is expected to require further government support. Investors are also still digesting the impact of new global banking regulations agreed upon in Basel, Switzerland, earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 "The required capital ratios are going up and that's going to have an impact," said Hans Olsen, chief investment officer at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  Private Wealth Management. "When you combine that with a macro environment that in and of itself has a large question mark above it, one has reason to be skittish around financial stocks at this point."\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  fell 2.7%, while Travelers dropped 1.8% and \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  shed 1.7%. M&T Bank slid 7% after its talks with Spain's Banco Santander stalled in recent days, making a deal in which M&T would buy Sovereign Bank, owned by Santander, seem less likely, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, telecommunications companies strengthened. Among the Dow's strongest stocks on Monday, \uc2 AT&T\uc2  rose 1% and \uc2 Verizon Communications\uc2  gained 0.7% amid a broader rise in telecommunications stocks. Shares of telecom companies, which often issue dividends, have surged recently as investors still nervous about the stock market have sought safety. \uc2 Sprint Nextel\uc2  rose 3.4%, while \uc2 MetroPCS Communications\uc2  gained 2.9%.\par \par \uc2 China's shares rose to nearly a two-week high Monday, following a three-day break for a national holiday last week, as metals companies caught up with the recent rally on the London Metal Exchange.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures were mostly flat Monday, pausing after Friday's sharp rally as traders wait for additional signals on the pace of the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely trade in a 2600-2650 range this week, ahead of the seven-day National Day holiday that begins Friday.\par \par \uc2 "Trading volume will likely be thin again this week ahead of the National Day holiday, but the Shanghai index will see solid support from international markets," said Amy Lin, an analyst at Capital Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Monday for the first time in four days as exporter shares temporarily brushed aside yen worries following Friday's sharp rally in U.S. equity markets, while Japanese consumer lenders plummeted on fears over a report that said bellwether Takefuji may file for bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 "There is a possibility that the BOJ will make a move (for monetary easing) during next month's policy meeting," which could temporarily weaken the yen, said \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2  strategist Hideyuki Ishiguro. He added another yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could push the Nikkei to the 9700 level.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures eked out marginal gains to settle at a new record in U.S. trading, while silver hit a fresh 30-year high Monday as the popularity of precious metals continued on the back of expectations of further U.S. stimulus money and worries about European sovereign finances.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange were broadly lower Monday, weighed down by lackluster metals trading in China and lower European equity markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar mustered a modest late rally against the euro Monday after it emerged that Federal Reserve officials could be considering a boost to the sagging economy by using a more open-ended, smaller-scale program than some investors had originally thought.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar had been on a weakening path against the euro and its other competitors--sinking earlier to its lowest level since April against the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --with investors betting the Fed would soon implement a fresh round of asset purchases, known as quantitative easing, to kickstart slowing growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Wall Street Journal reported late Monday that Federal Reserve officials are considering new tactics if they resume purchases of long-term U.S. Treasury securities; rather than announcing massive bond purchases with a finite end, as they did in 2009 to shock the U.S. financial system back to life, Fed officials are weighing other options, the Journal reported.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The whole selloff in the dollar has been based on expectation the Fed is going to do some QE," said Brian Dolan, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .com in Bedminster, N.J. "The less likely they are to do that, the less negative it is for the dollar."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday in New York, the euro was at $1.3444 from $1.3493 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. It had ticked as high as $1.3507. The dollar was at Y84.25 from Y84.28, while the euro was at Y113.25 from Y113.70. The U.K. pound was at $1.5826 from $1.5833. The dollar was at CHF0.9857 from CHF0.9848.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of others, was at 79.502 from 79.280. The index earlier fell to its lowest level since February.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's very far from a done deal that they're going to be forced to do" another round of quantitative easing," Dolan said. "This is one of the last bullets in their ammo belt, and they're going to be very reluctant to use it," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though it recouped its losses against the euro, the dollar fell Monday to its lowest level against the yen since the Japanese government's massive mid-September \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem yen strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors went on high alert for signs of the Japanese authorities in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after the dollar dipped to Y84.11, its bottom point since \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Y2 trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Sept. 15. However, the dollar stuck close to its intraday lows for the remainder of the session and there were no indications of any sort of official Japanese action, said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar has been trapped in a tight range against the yen since, with investors cautious about being on the wrong side of another possible round of government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governor Masaaki Shirakawa pledged Monday to take "appropriate policy steps" if the yen's rise puts downward pressure on the country's economy, emphasizing that the BOJ will watch developments in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets with "great interest."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the euro earlier rose above $1.35 as investors shook off fresh concerns over the health of European banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, China's yuan rose for the 10th consecutive session against the U.S. dollar late Monday to another intraday high under the current system.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's gain came after China Premier Wen Jiabao ruled out any large-scale appreciation of the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , following a meeting with U.S. President Barack Obama during Wen's trip to New York last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. sold $36 billion in two-year notes at a record-low yield of 0.441% Monday, and Treasurys rallied as investors continued to fret about the health of the global economy and stocked up on low-risk assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The successful auction served as evidence of the continuing solid demand for low-risk U.S. government debt, as investors, still concerned about the recovery in the U.S. and abroad, continue to pour money into Treasurys. Demand for Treasurys intensified last week after the Federal Reserve highlighted the risk of deflation and signaled it is ready to provide more help to the economy if needed. Many investors are even more persuaded the Fed will launch another large-scale bond-buying program this year, purchasing Treasurys, which has left the government bond market well bid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Wall Street Journal article Monday offered details on possible Treasury purchases, noting that Fed officials are considering new tactics if they resume purchases, namely a more open-ended, smaller-scale program they could adjust as needed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Monday afternoon trading in New York, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was up 24/32 to yield 2.521% and the 30-year bond was up 1 21/32 to yield 3.702%. Bond prices move inversely to yields. The two-year note was up 1/32 to yield 0.428%. [ 27-09-10 2208GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two-year notes were sold well below the 0.498% from the August auction, and the sale marked the fifth consecutive month that new two-years have been offered at a record-low yield. That is good news for the U.S. government as it continues to borrow at historically low interest rates to fund a budget deficit that is more than $1 trillion. The Treasury is selling $100 billion in new notes this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The total amount of bids for the two-year note auction was 3.78 times the amount on offer, the second highest bid-to-cover record, far surpassing the 3.12 at the last two-year auction and the average of 3.21 for the prior four auctions. An August 2007 two-year sale drew a 3.97 bid to cover.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The indirect bid, a proxy of buying by foreign investors including central banks, was 39%, compared with 29.2% at the last sale and the average of 34.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower Monday amid renewed concerns over inflation after China Business News reported Saturday, citing Wang Jian, deputy chief of an economic research unit under the NDRC, that China's CPI growth will likely rise around 5% on the year in October, driven by higher food prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wang also said it will be difficult for China to meet the 2010 CPI target of 3%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If Wang's CPI prediction comes true, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will very likely hike the interest rates to curb inflation," a Beijing-based analyst with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is likely to consolidate around 126.5 in the near term.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond futures ended slightly lower Monday as gains in Tokyo share prices caused some players to move out of safe-haven assets, but investor sentiment was still broadly cautious ahead of the release of key Bank of Japan business sentiment data later this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There will likely be more direction (in the bond market) once investors see the business sentiment survey and any hints on monetary policy for the second half of the fiscal year in the (BOJ) tankan" due Wednesday, said Makoto Yamashita, a strategist at Deutsche Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JGB market participants will also keep a close eye on the survey's data on corporate capital expenditure and profit outlooks for any hints on the possibility of further monetary easing by the BOJ, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even if the tankan comes in ahead of forecasts, JGBs are expected to stay supported in the near term by deep-rooted expectations for additional monetary easing in both Japan and the U.S.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday, the market will focus on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's sale of Y2.6 trillion worth of two-year government bonds. Analysts expect the new two-year bonds to receive solid demand as a safe place to park surplus cash.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Fed Weighs Different Approach To Bond Purchases \par GMAC Mortgage Probed Over Foreclosure Documents \par Southwest Acquire \f28 \uc2 \f2 Airtran\f28 \uc2 \f2  In $1.4B Deal \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Unilever\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Buy \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alberto Culver\f28 \uc2 \f2  For $3.7B \par IMF Says UK's Credible Fiscal Plan Is Essential \par Harrisburg May Miss Payroll Over Political Squabble \par US Appeals Court Appears Divided In Stem Cell Case \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Gives AIB Three-notch Downgrade \par BP Sees 'Phased Restart' Of Offshore Drilling \par =GE Inks Deal To Extend Reach In China's Wind Power Market \par =WSJ: N Korea's Kim Jong Il Makes His Third Son A Four-Star General \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2100  S Korea      Sep Consumer Sentiment Index \par 0100  Philippines  Jul External Trade Performance \par 0300  S Korea      Aug New deposit, loan rates \par 0600  Japan        Jul Revised Machine Tool Orders \par 0610  Germany      Oct GfK consumer climate survey \par 0645  France       Aug Household consumption expenditure in manufactured goods \par 0645  France       Aug Housing starts \par 0830  UK           2Q  Balance of Payments \par 0830  UK           2Q  Quarterly national accounts (GDP) \p