\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Kospi Likely To Open Higher On US Cues; 1860 Cap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 01:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2348 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi likely to open higher, taking cue from Wall Street gains Friday; "investors seem to have regained confidence in economic fundamentals and they will watch to see if foreigners will continue to buy domestic stocks," says Lee Kyung-soo at Taurus Investment & Securities. Notes foreigners bought KRW3.5 trillion worth of stocks in September alone. Today, if technology shares try "meaningful" rebound after recent falls, Kospi may rise sharply, he says. News over weekend that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hyundai Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2  (005380.SE) will recall some 140,000 Sonata sedans in U.S. market due to steering problems may weigh on shares in early session, though stock may recover later as recall voluntary, fix for problems expected to be affordable. Tips Kospi in 1840-1860 band today; index closed +0.8% at 1846.60 Friday. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] Comex December copper now trading $3.6380/lb, $8,021.79/ton, suggesting that LME 3-month could breach $8,000/ton level for first time since April after $7,990/ton intraday high Friday. EUR/USD barely changed since Friday, down 0.0011 to 1.3474 from 1.3485, but little rise necessary to flutter metal above crucial psychological level; Japan trade statistics due out shortly to be first crucial indicator for USD, though recent \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by BoJ may mute impact. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] Volume in Australia's debt capital markets year to date stands at US$92.7 billion in 391 deals, down 17% on-year but still second highest year to date on record, according to Dealogic data. Most notable is jump in Kangaroo bond issuance, stands at US$27.7 billion year to date or triple amount raised in 2009. Healthy volumes look strong on a headline basis, but still dominated by bank issuers and supranational Kangaroo issuers on back of attractive basis swap, suggesting investors still conservative. \uc2 Westpac\uc2  top of the bookrunners table, holds 11.5% market share, followed by \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  and \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW may fall vs 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul as sentiment for greenback continues to remain bearish in general amid concerns Fed could initiate another round of asset purchases, analysts say. "Dollar/won will likely be under pressure as the dollar's fall in the broader market accelerates with the dollar index's slip below the 80 mark," says Samsung Futures analyst Jeon Seung-ji. But expects continued smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities to stem any sharp KRW gains, potential USD demand from state-run companies to cushion pair's fall. Pegs support at 1,147. Local traders said last week BOK suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  towards end of domestic trade Friday to buy USD at levels below KRW1,155. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY ROUNDUP: Precious metals held close to their end-week peaks at the start of spot market trading today, with gold up 20 cents against its late New York price at $1,297.20/oz, silver off 2 cents at $21.44/oz, platinum up $2 at $1,641/oz, and palladium up $3 at $560/oz. Gold and silver hit fresh all-time and 30-year records respectively Friday as U.S. dollar weakness and attraction of safe-haven assets burnished luster of precious metals. All base and precious metals except nickel and tin rose on the week; firmer equities and the weaker U.S. dollar Friday helped push up base metals on the day trade, with LME 3-month copper hitting an intraday $7,990/ton, highest since mid-April peak. Copper closed at $7,944/ton, up $64 from Thursday's PM kerb, aluminum gained $25.50 to $2,317/ton, zinc ebbed $3 to $2,247/ton, lead rose $21.50 to $2,296/ton, nickel climbed $195 to $22,895/ton, tin lost $95 to $23,600/ton. Nymex October WTI was up 29 cents at $76.79 while EUR/USD fell 0.12 cents to 1.3475. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 3.5 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket buying following 1 session of net selling may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 15.0 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 11.5 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury yield may rise this week due to strong U.S. stocks recently, and not-so-negative economic reports such as durable goods orders that suggested signs of stabilization ahead, says senior dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. Adds investors will watch U.S. share prices to gauge outlook for Treasurys. Yield last at 2.612%; dealer tips trade in 2.600%-2.650% range.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 83.80 due to broad USD weakness as U.S. economic indicators Friday, particularly housing data, were worse than expected and growing view that U.S. Treasury yields unlikely to rise further for time being, says Yuichiro Harada, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Says fall below 83.80 unlikely as players still cautious about potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. Says market players watching remarks from BOJ Governor Shirakawa for 