hitting all-time low of 1.3209 in New York trade Friday vs 1.3257 late Asia trade Friday in line with broad-based USD weakness vs majors. Friday's gains at Wall Street, EUR gains following stronger-than-expected German economic data indicate appetite for riskier assets picking up. Market likely watching if MAS will support or lift USD/SGD; a source familiar with MAS's thinking told Dow Jones last week that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tolerating a weaker U.S. dollar as gains by EUR, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  limiting broad impact of SGD's rise. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate psychological support at 1.3200, next 1.3170. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2254 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 1.2% at 4677 after stocks in the U.S. closed up Friday. DJIA closed up 1.86% at 10860.26, S&P 500 up 2.12% at 1148.67, Nasdaq up 2.3% at 2381.22. The Dow rose 2.38% during the week, extending its winning streak to a fourth week. The measure hasn't had such a long weekly positive run since an eight-week advance that ended in April. Key indicators for the health of the U.S. economy to watch this week include data on home prices in 20 major cities due Tuesday; latest government numbers for 2Q gross domestic product Thursday and auto sales figures Friday. Michael McCarthy, head of dealing at Cityindex, says "talk is that we've breached important technical levels not just locally by internationally," adding that continued U.S. economic recovery will help drive trading sentiment on the ASX. Gold futures broke above $1,300 an ounce for the first time Friday in U.S. trading, but pared gains as some traders took profits from the metal's record-setting rise. Front-month Nymex crude oil traded on Globex was up $0.23 cents at $76.72 in early Asian trade. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 1945ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00