\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Commodity Summary: USD Raises Base, Precious Metals\b0\par\par\uc2 1,786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2010\par 01:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY ROUNDUP: Precious metals held close to their end-week peaks at the start of spot market trading today, with gold up 20 cents against its late New York price at $1,297.20/oz, silver off 2 cents at $21.44/oz, platinum up $2 at $1,641/oz, and palladium up $3 at $560/oz. Gold and silver hit fresh all-time and 30-year records respectively Friday as U.S. dollar weakness and attraction of safe-haven assets burnished luster of precious metals. All base and precious metals except nickel and tin rose on the week; firmer equities and the weaker U.S. dollar Friday helped push up base metals on the day trade, with LME 3-month copper hitting an intraday $7,990/ton, highest since mid-April peak. Copper closed at $7,944/ton, up $64 from Thursday's PM kerb, aluminum gained $25.50 to $2,317/ton, zinc ebbed $3 to $2,247/ton, lead rose $21.50 to $2,296/ton, nickel climbed $195 to $22,895/ton, tin lost $95 to $23,600/ton. Nymex October WTI was up 29 cents at $76.79 while EUR/USD fell 0.12 cents to 1.3475. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 3.5 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket buying following 1 session of net selling may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 15.0 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 11.5 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury yield may rise this week due to strong U.S. stocks recently, and not-so-negative economic reports such as durable goods orders that suggested signs of stabilization ahead, says senior dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. Adds investors will watch U.S. share prices to gauge outlook for Treasurys. Yield last at 2.612%; dealer tips trade in 2.600%-2.650% range.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 83.80 due to broad USD weakness as U.S. economic indicators Friday, particularly housing data, were worse than expected and growing view that U.S. Treasury yields unlikely to rise further for time being, says Yuichiro Harada, senior dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Says fall below 83.80 unlikely as players still cautious about potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. Says market players watching remarks from BOJ Governor Shirakawa for hints at any further easing measures at his speech, press conference due 0530 GMT, 0745 GMT respectively. USD/JPY last 84.33. Due to broad USD weakness, EUR/USD biased up, may trade in 1.3430-1.3530 range in global day vs 1.3486 last. EUR/JPY may trade in 113.00-114.50 band vs 113.66 last.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield last at 0.995%, below 1.00% where investors usually lock in profits but this time that's unlikely and yields may keep falling, says Mizuho Securities chief market analyst Tetsuya Miura. Tips 0.970%-1.045% range. Says JGBs should get support from pessimistic views over Japan's economic outlook as BOJ tankan survey due this week likely show firms' sentiment deteriorating due to slowing U.S. economy, persisting strong JPY, he says. "You may notice there are not many factors that would prompt investors to sell JGBs for now," he says. Adds December futures may trade in 142.25-143.14 range vs last 142.88.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2312 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 +0.6% at 3231.86 as market follows strong offshore leads, tone expected to continue, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker James Porteous; "I would think we would continue on (higher), we haven't been as vigorous as the Dow and I would imagine that Australia would be pretty strong today as well." Adds Hallestein Glasson (HLG.NZ) +1.9% at NZ$4.50 after it announced its full year 2010 net profit +53% at NZ$19.6 million; "They have traditionally been regarded as solid operator, one of the best operator in that space," this confirms that, says Porteous. Retailers Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) +2.3% at NZ$1.82, Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ) +1.1% at NZ$1.93, Warehouse (WHS.NZ) +1.3% at NZ$3.80 after HLG said sales for first 7 weeks of FY11 +5%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2307 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to rise following Friday's US stock rally on signs of stabilization in durable goods orders. But gains may be capped on concerns about firmer yen (USD/JPY now 84.31) and lingering Japan-China tension despite release of detained captain over shipping incident in disputed waters. "It's going to be difficult for investors to move aggressively before the end of the month," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities, noting Monday is last day stockholders (of record) are entitled to current round of dividend payouts. Puts Nikkei range for today at 9450-9600 vs 9471.67 (down 1.0%) Friday close. Topix "other financial business" subindex (home to consumer lenders) expected to fall on Nikkei report Takefuji (8564.TO) will file for protection from creditors (shares off 6.6% Friday). Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9500 vs Osaka close at 9400. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2305 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD now at 1.3211 after hitting all-time low of 1.3209 in New York trade Friday vs 1.3257 late Asia trade Friday in line with broad-based USD weakness vs majors. Friday's gains at Wall Street, EUR gains following stronger-than-expected German economic data indicate appetite for riskier assets picking up. Market likely watching if MAS will support or lift USD/SGD; a source familiar with MAS's thinking told Dow Jones last week that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is tolerating a weaker U.S. dollar as gains by EUR, other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  limiting broad impact of SGD's rise. Dow Jones technical analysis shows immediate psychological support at 1.3200, next 1.3170. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2254 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bullish, with overnight SPI 200 futures up 1.2% at 4677 after stocks in the U.S. closed up Friday. DJIA closed up 1.86% at 10860.26, S&P 500 up 2.12% at 1148.67, Nasdaq up 2.3% at 2381.22. The Dow rose 2.38% during the week, extending its winning streak to a fourth week. The measure hasn't had such a long weekly positive run since an eight-week advance that ended in April. Key indicators for the health of the U.S. economy to watch this week include data on home prices in 20 major cities due Tuesday; latest government numbers for 2Q gross domestic product Thursday and auto sales figures Friday. Michael McCarthy, head of dealing at Cityindex, says "talk is that we've breached important technical levels not just locally by internationally," adding that continued U.S. economic recovery will help drive trading sentiment on the ASX. Gold futures broke above $1,300 an ounce for the first time Friday in U.S. trading, but pared gains as some traders took profits from the metal's record-setting rise. Front-month Nymex crude oil traded on Globex was up $0.23 cents at $76.72 in early Asian trade. (andrew.critchlow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2250 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely lifted by solid gains on Wall Street Friday as signs of stabilization in durable goods, housing boosted investors' appetite for risk. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, traders will remain on the lookout for any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese authorities to weaken JPY; EUR could be biased higher if regional equities track U.S. stocks higher. EUR/USD at 1.3485 vs 1.3493 late NY Friday, EUR/JPY at 133.66 vs 113.70, USD/JPY at 84.29 vs 84.28. On data slate: Japan provisional trade, corporate service price index at 2350 GMT, Taiwan business indicators at 0800 GMT, HK trade at 0830 GMT, BOJ governor Shirakawa speaks. Later there's U.K. Hometrack house prices survey, Chicago Fed midwest manufacturing index, Texas manufacturing outlook survey. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] The case that says there is a material benefit to an October RBA hike just hasn't been made, says Adam Carr, senior economist at ICAP. If growth is as strong as it looks then it makes no difference to that outlook whether hike comes in October or November. In contrast, the potential costs are very real if pessimists are correct. It would harm the RBA's credibility to reverse course again, it creates unnecessary policy volatility. So it really is naive to argue that the RBA could simply flick the switch back if things soften, as if nothing had happened. In sum, RBA will have a better idea of things by November with 3Q CPI out before then, so it would pay to wait. October 30-day interbank futures now 95.37, down 0.005. (james.glynn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2238 GMT [Dow Jones] Trade the front-month Nymex oil futures contract between $72.75 and $77.00 into next month, as building strong case for price move of much more than $3-$4 in either direction from current levels remains difficult, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. China, India continuing as engines of global oil demand growth, but 5-6 million bbl/day excess Opec production capacity is cushion against any unforeseen oil demand increases. Oil market taking cues more from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets than swings in equities. "A weakening U.S. dollar that is likely to emanate from the Fed's expected monetary easing will keep open a good chance of an oil price spike, especially if gold prices continue to push higher," Ritterbusch says. Current EUR values appear poised to post strongest levels since last April, which may lead front-month crude futures to make run at $77/bbl mark. Nymex crude for November delivery now up 3 cents at 76.52/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com) [ 09-26-10 1934ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100926e69q0001f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
