\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/MYR Higher; 3.0900-3.1050 Band Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 1,604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 02:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0020 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher at 3.0980 vs 3.0930 late yesterday; pair higher on MYR profit-taking following MYR's rise to fresh 13-year high yesterday; dealers say with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suspected of having bought USD recently to slow MYR's appreciation, participants wary of holding positions into weekend. "Market activity is expected to be rather quiet today in the absence of leads," dealer says; tips pair to remain in 3.0900-3.1050 band. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures rising, may extend gains to 143.00 following upturn in U.S. Treasurys overnight, says trader at Tokyo securities house. Adds if Tokyo shares continue falling (Nikkei now down 1.4%), demand for safe-haven assets will likely grow, pushing long-term JGB prices further up. Focus now on whether Japan's government will step into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again to sell JPY, which could help stocks rally. JGB futures now at 142.90, +0.25 from Wednesday close. Key 10-year JGB yield at 1.005%, down 2 bps. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0003 GMT [Dow Jones] Resources boom helping Australian government's budgetary position and should continue to improve rapidly from here, given stimulus spending peak has well passed, says RBS chief economist Kieran Davies. Says government will need to find some savings for concessions made to non-party lawmakers. "The dominant force is going to be the resources boom." Adds with GDP growth and robust jobs market, surplus target of 2012-2013 achievable. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Markets unlikely to pay much heed to backward-looking release of Australian government's 2009-10 budget figures, which show government recorded a narrower-than-expected A$54.8 billion deficit last fiscal year, vs A$57.1 billion forecast in May budget. More important will be any outlook comments as Treasurer Swan holds press conference to discuss data at 0000 GMT. Although Australia's finances are in better shape vs many developed-country peers, enormous policy challenge remains as resource-rich A$1.3 trillion economy grapples with fresh capacity pressures amid a resurgent resources boom. With markets pricing in 65% chance of October RBA hike after RBA Governor Stevens declared the resources boom would be biggest since 19th century, government may need to share more of the heavy lifting to keep inflation in check--which will be tough in the current fragile political environment. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound, with short-term paper expected to maintain slight upward bias on MYR appreciation; dealer says market lacking drivers, with August CPI data, announcement of size of MYR3.0 billion 3-year Islamic bond auction this week well within expectations. "The ringgit appreciation story will continue to be the main talking point for now until the outcome of the (Islamic bond) auction is out (on September 29)." Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end mostly lower, with May 2013 down 1 bp at 3.16%, August 2015 down 2 bps at 3.28%, September 2017 down 1 bp at 3.54%, November 2019 unchanged at 3.63%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely down at least 1.0% today after decisive falls on Wall Street - S&P 500, DJIA fell below former major resistance levels near 1130 and 10720, respectively. That should equate to S&P/ASX 200 falling below former major resistance i