\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Lead JGB Futures May Extend Gains To 143 -Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 02:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0013 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead December JGB futures rising, may extend gains to 143.00 following upturn in U.S. Treasurys overnight, says trader at Tokyo securities house. Adds if Tokyo shares continue falling (Nikkei now down 1.4%), demand for safe-haven assets will likely grow, pushing long-term JGB prices further up. Focus now on whether Japan's government will step into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market again to sell JPY, which could help stocks rally. JGB futures now at 142.90, +0.25 from Wednesday close. Key 10-year JGB yield at 1.005%, down 2 bps. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0003 GMT [Dow Jones] Resources boom helping Australian government's budgetary position and should continue to improve rapidly from here, given stimulus spending peak has well passed, says RBS chief economist Kieran Davies. Says government will need to find some savings for concessions made to non-party lawmakers. "The dominant force is going to be the resources boom." Adds with GDP growth and robust jobs market, surplus target of 2012-2013 achievable. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0002 GMT [Dow Jones] Markets unlikely to pay much heed to backward-looking release of Australian government's 2009-10 budget figures, which show government recorded a narrower-than-expected A$54.8 billion deficit last fiscal year, vs A$57.1 billion forecast in May budget. More important will be any outlook comments as Treasurer Swan holds press conference to discuss data at 0000 GMT. Although Australia's finances are in better shape vs many developed-country peers, enormous policy challenge remains as resource-rich A$1.3 trillion economy grapples with fresh capacity pressures amid a resurgent resources boom. With markets pricing in 65% chance of October RBA hike after RBA Governor Stevens declared the resources boom would be biggest since 19th century, government may need to share more of the heavy lifting to keep inflation in check--which will be tough in the current fragile political environment. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2357 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysia government bonds likely rangebound, with short-term paper expected to maintain slight upward bias on MYR appreciation; dealer says market lacking drivers, with August CPI data, announcement of size of MYR3.0 billion 3-year Islamic bond auction this week well within expectations. "The ringgit appreciation story will continue to be the main talking point for now until the outcome of the (Islamic bond) auction is out (on September 29)." Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS end mostly lower, with May 2013 down 1 bp at 3.16%, August 2015 down 2 bps at 3.28%, September 2017 down 1 bp at 3.54%, November 2019 unchanged at 3.63%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely down at least 1.0% today after decisive falls on Wall Street - S&P 500, DJIA fell below former major resistance levels near 1130 and 10720, respectively. That should equate to S&P/ASX 200 falling below former major resistance in 4600-4620 area. S&P/ASX 200 lost momentum recently and a sharp fall today will cement sell signals on technical indicators. "Last ditch" support lies at the former "neckline" resistance from "inverse head & shoulders pattern", near 4580, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. But a break of recent range support at 4603.6 will trigger a bearish triangle pattern, targeting 4533, which is also the Aug. 9 trough and 38.2% retracement of Aug. 25-Sept. 15 rise. When an inverse head & shoulders bottom fails, the market falls substantially. Also, since May, the index has fallen 285-475 points each time it's turned down from 4600-4650. Thus, there should be a lot of focus on the reaction to 4580 today. Wall Street took a "glass half empty" view last night. In reality, most of the U.S. economic data last night beat expectations and if the jobless situation is deteriorating, the Fed is more likely to launch QE 2. It seems that traders bought the break of resistance levels, yet investors are staying out of selling. Bears are focussing on potential U.S. mutual fund selling caused by redemptions and tax loss selling before year end. U.S. durable goods data later Friday. Index last 4633.6. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] Renewed concerns over health of global economy may weigh on Singapore shares in early trade after pushing U.S. stocks lower overnight. With local corporate newsflow thin, participants expected to take cue from other Asian bourses as session progresses. Markets in Hong Kong, Japan, South Korea reopen after recent public holidays. Support for STI, down 0.4% at 3083.13 yesterday, at 3043 August peak, with year-to-date high of 3116 expected to cap any upside. "A pullback would be healthy after the STI's 4.5% rise this month. The third-quarter earnings season will begin next month, so there is a risk of the market running ahead of expectations if it keeps going up," says trader at local brokerage. Olam (O32.SG) may be in focus as commodity trader reveals it's in talks with unlisted rival Louis Dreyfus on collaboration which could result in merger; shares +0.7% at S$2.95 yesterday. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2354 GMT [Dow Jones] Uptick in risk aversion hurting AUD/USD but pair still a buy in dips, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2  strategist Jonathan Cavenagh. Notes sovereign debt issues still bubbling away in Europe. "You are starting to see creeping risk aversion in credit markets and that's starting to affect equity markets." AUD/USD last 0.9477 from 0.9490. Adds market ignored government's final budget outcome for 2009/10 showing slightly better fiscal position. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2347 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares down 0.2% to 3221.221, drifting in tepid trading; "There is not really too much that is newsworthy out there. It is a very, very quiet start to the day. The market is just a bit weaker following through from the weaker close in international markets overnight," says First NZ Capital senior broker James Snell. Says that given Australia's corporate diary looks fairly light for day, market may remain around these levels for most of session. Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) down 0.6% at NZ$8.57 on profit taking after recent strong run. Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) +0.6% at NZ$1.78 after FY result in line with company guidance given last month. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may consolidate due to lack of major cues amid expectations for reduced trading volume after long Chuseok holidays lasting from Tuesday to Thursday. "The foreign stock exchanges showed no major moves during the holidays, so we may see the index hover around Monday's close (of 1832.63)," says Taurus Investment & Securities analyst Oh Tae-dong. Tips 1825-1838 range for day, notes market may hold off on taking major moves ahead of global economic data next week as month of September comes to a close. \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE) may rise on M&A hopes after creditors officially launch sale of their 34.88% stake in company, while \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) may rise after opening $500 million manufacturing plant in Russia. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may again test post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  low of 84.26 hit Thursday in NY trading in absence of any sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and if Nikkei drops after declines in U.S. shares overnight, says Naoto Taikoji, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be unwilling to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just as PM Kan meeting with U.S. President Obama in NY. Pair last 84.53. EUR/JPY biased down on broad JPY strength, may trade in 112.20-113.00 band vs last 112.55. Adds any further falls may be limited as market has already priced in EUR weakness due to new doubts on health of European economy. Says EUR/USD may trade in 1.3300-1.3360 band vs last 1.3318. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to fall, possibly targeting a breach of key 1,160 support vs 1,161.3 late Monday in Seoul, playing catch-up on broad USD weakness seen earlier this week during Chuseok holidays, traders say. Domestic markets reopen today after being shut for 3 days until yesterday. "Hints of possible quantitative easing measures from the Fed pushed the euro above the $1.3400 mark (earlier this week)," and offshore players likely to continue making bullish bets for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says local bank trader. Adds, amount of corporate USD demand plus possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at levels below 1,160 will likely be key for session. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 9 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 800,000 Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling in 9 sessions may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 13.9 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 13.1 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 09-23-10 2013ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100924e69o00008\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
