\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Yen rebounds as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk unconfirmed\b0\par\par\uc2 535 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 04:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Dollar back below 85 yen as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk not confirmed\par \par \uc2 * Buying of exporters helps Nikkei cut losses\par \par \uc2 * Broad market weakens after U.S. jobless numbers jump\par \par \uc2 By Elaine Lies and Kevin Plumberg\par \par \uc2 TOKYO/HONG KONG, Sept 24 (Reuters) - The yen regained some of the losses it suffered on Friday with no confirmation of rumours \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the second time this month.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk had helped Japanese equities cut their losses on strength in exporter stocks, but the benchmark index drifted lower later in the day.\par \par \uc2 European shares opened lower after latest U.S. jobless claims figures added to persistent concerns that the global economic recovery was still fragile.\par \par \uc2 Britain's FTSE 100 was down 0.3 percent, Germany's DAX fell 0.2 percent, and France's CAC-40 eased as much as 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI index for Asia ex-Japan stocks was flat following the U.S. jobless data, although the benchmark is up one percent on the week. It is just off a five month peak struck earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Initial U.S. claims for state unemployment benefits increased 12,000 to 465,000 last week, the Labor Department said on Thursday, breaking two straight weeks of declines. Financial markets had forecast claims steady at 450,000.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei share average finished down 1 percent after initially climbing on the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  talk. Rising diplomatic tensions between \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China also weighed on sentiment.\par \par \uc2 "The Nikkei was only briefly helped by the talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially since it's hard to tell if any such move actually took place," said Koichi Ogawa, chief portfolio manager at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of risk factors that have suddenly emerged, such as the situation with China, and this is making it very hard for the Nikkei to rise."\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose as high as 85.40 yen from about 84.55 yen in a matter of minutes, and several traders said it looked like the Bank of Japan, which acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had been selling yen. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted back towards 84.70 yen on the lack of confirmation on government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Given that this would be the second time (for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and not as much of a surprise, I think the impact would be pretty limited at best," said Masayoshi Okamoto, head of dealing with \uc2 Jujiya Securities\uc2  in Tokyo. "Even now, it seems tough for the dollar to hang on to the 85 yen level, and this will make it hard for the Nikkei to rise substantially in turn."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sept. 15 minutes after the dollar hit a 15-year low of 82.87 yen, selling an estimated 2 trillion yen ($23.70 billion), its largest single-day yen selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Also pressuring the dollar were shrinking yield gaps between the dollar and the yen.\par \par \uc2 Oil fell below $75 as investor unease about the global economic recovery spread across markets after lacklustre U.S. employment and housing data. ($1=84.37 Yen)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL/ (WRAPUP 2)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|R