\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI Flat; Reduced Appetite Not Bad Thing - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 08:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0656 GMT [Dow Jones] Demand for Singapore blue chips remains soft, with interest still centered on penny stocks. STI flat at 3084.20, with support at 3043 August high, resistance at year-to-date 3116 peak. In contrast, FTSE ST Catalist Index +3.4%. "Investors' appetite is smaller after a decent run in past weeks. A break now and in the coming days is not necessarily a bad thing going into the earnings season which starts in the middle of October," says dealer at local brokerage. Overall volume moderate at under 1.7 billion shares. Key performers among small caps include China New Town Development (D4N.SG), +6.7% at S$0.16, Bio-Treat (B22.SG), +5.6% at S$0.095, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Adventus Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  (5EF.SG), +8.3% at S$0.065. Among STI stocks, Olam (O32.SG) +7.5% at S$3.17, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sembcorp Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  (U96.SG) +1.2% at S$4.36, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Fraser & Neave\f28 \uc2 \f2  (F99.SG) +1.3% at S$6.20. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] Front-end India overnight indexed swaps flat-to-mildly higher on tight cash conditions while back-end swaps (1-year to 5-years) slip, mirroring lower bond yields, says foreign bank trader. 1-year OIS rate flat vs Thursday close at 6.43%/6.46%, 5-year OIS at 7.01%/7.04% vs 7.07%/7.10% last close. Trader says, swaps saw receiving interest early session on upbeat sentiment after government late Thursday announced INR100 billion cut in fiscal 2H borrowing plan, while also raising foreign investment cap in bonds. But early receiving may be petering out. "There has been some profit-booking on receiving trades at lower levels. Traders do not want to receive below 7.00%," trader says. Tips 1-year OIS rate in 6.40%-6.55% band in week, 5-year OIS in 6.95%-7.15% band. Yield on benchmark 10-year bond has fallen as much as 8 basis points since Thursday close. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0649 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.1% at 22,068.87 despite Wall Street losses, as resilience in local developers and \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) offsets losses in other blue chips. Hutch +1.8% at HK$66.35, now +25% since interim results out early August, in strong re-rating as long-suffering 3G \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  finally turning around, parent Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +1.8% at HK$113.50. Taifook Securities says Hutchison "truly diversified conglomerate," turnaround at 3 Group "providing the major catalyst." \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK), territory's biggest property developer by market cap, +2.1% at HK$127.80. HKEx (0388.HK) another outperformer, +2.3% at HK$145.80, as HK market volume remains strong, last at HK$42.57 billion. \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) underperforming, down 1.5% at HK$11.58 after \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  cuts stock to Sell from Buy. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 84.50 on selling by Japanese exporters, who are taking advantage of earlier rise in pair to lock in more attractive prices, says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Japanese investment manager. Pair has given up most of its earlier gains, last at 84.69; around 0415 GMT, pair rode sharply to intraday high at 85.40; jump fueled speculation that Tokyo may have conducted USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again after doing so for first time in more than six years last Wednesday. No confirmation either way from MOF on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Finance Minister Noda offering no comment. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] Canadian banks venturing into Australia in pursuit of covered bond issuance could open door to new market, though likely tepid response from local investors and limited number of banks who can issue this debt with the AAA stamp mean the market may remain limited in size. Key will be response of Asia-based reserve managers who buy highly-rated bonds. Absence of local buyers and an uncertain response from banks mean the trek Down Under could be short lived. =FOCUS: Canadian Banks To Test Covered Bonds Down Under (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] December bunds are unchanged at 131.39, consolidating after the gains made in past few sessions. WestLB sees next resistance at 131.69, followed by 132.14, and 132.89. Support is at 131.14, 130.41, and 130.29. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW down at 4-month low of 1,155.2 late Friday in Seoul vs 1,161.3 Monday (local markets shut Tuesday-Thursday), but pair off intraday low at 1,153.5 on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  towards end of domestic trade. Two senior Seoul-based traders suspect BOK of buying USD largely around KRW1,153.5-KRW1,155 area, with one of those traders estimating buying to have been roughly $300 million-$500 million worth. Pair's downward momentum likely to stay next week, "but it's not going to be a lot of fun shorting the dollar from here, because it will be real slow in heading down," as BOK likely to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says foreign bank trader. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0637 GMT [Dow Jones] The December 10-year UST is relatively stable in early European trading at 125-225, around the middle of the post-FOMC range of the last few days. The contract briefly broke higher to 126-025 Thursday but fell back after the initial surprise of the weak jobless claims data wore off. "Technicals remain mixed, so we would not be surprised to see more interim range trading," says Jason Hunter, \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  technical strategist. Resistance stands at 125-305, 126-025, 126-115 and 127-015 while support lies at 125-11, 125-03, 124-265 and 124-21. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed Friday, with yields at their lowest level in two weeks. Signs of weakening growth in the euro zone, coupled with a reduction in debt issuance by Germany, pushed bunds sharply higher Thursday. The market will be eyeing the Ifo index at 0800 GMT, which is likely to moderate but still show growth, in contrast to the unexpected contraction in Irish GDP that raised concerns that the recovery in peripheral countries is faltering. "This clearly underpins the divergence in the euro zone and is likely to provide further solid support for bunds both in outright as well as in relative value terms," says David Schnautz at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . December bunds are unchanged at 131.39, and the 10-year bund is marginally up at 99.70 to yield 2.284%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume steady vs Wednesday's half-day at HK$1.793 billion, 4.5% of total market volume, also little changed vs 4.3% at Wednesday's half-day, suggesting bears remain deterred by market strength with HSI rising 10 out of past 11 sessions. Hutchison (0013.HK) leads with HK$212.48 million, followed by parent Cheung Kong (0001.HK) HK$146.87 million -- duo continue recent outperformance toady. Next come \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$91.45 million, China Life (2628.HK) with HK$59.94 million, HKEx (0388.HK) with HK$56.53 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mixed; roller coaster for Nikkei, spends all morning more than 1% lower before rebounding after JPY falls sharply vs USD; index briefly turned positive in afternoon session after JPY's fall, before falling back, ends off 1.0%; S&P/ASX 200 down 0.6%, Kospi +0.6%, HSI +0.3%, STI +0.2%, KLCI off 0.6%, SET +0.4%, NZX-50 down 0.5%, Taiex down 0.4%, PSEi +0.3%, Sensex +0.4%. China markets shut for holidays. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY falls sharply vs USD, EUR, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around 0415 GMT; some senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks say drop in JPY looks like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen directly. Kyodo, citing market sources, says BOJ appears to have sold JPY. But Nikkei report says some market players doubting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting big JPY selling orders by banks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda declined to comment on whether government had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; "I have no comment," Noda says; USD/JPY last at 84.94; pair rose sharply around 0415 GMT from 84.52 to 85.40 high vs 84.34 in late NY trade. EUR/JPY last at 113.36, rose in tandem with USD/JPY to 113.61 from 112.77 vs 112.32 late NY. EUR/USD at 1.3341 vs 1.3317 late NY. Earlier Friday, Japan's finance minister Noda said government will take "decisive" measures in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary; "Our fundamental view is that excessive fluctuation or disorderly movement in exchange rates have a negative impact on the stability of economic and financial conditions;" comments were at news conference after regular cabinet, came before yen's sharp fall. Traders in Seoul suspect BOK of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb KRW's rise, trading at 4-month highs vs USD; one says BOK may have bought USD at levels below KRW1,155; USD/KRW last at 1,155.2 vs 1,161.3 late Monday in Seoul. Aussie government renews commitment to strict fiscal discipline to restore budget to surplus by year starting July 1, 2012, after unveiling narrower-than-expected deficit for last financial year. Singapore August industrial production +8.1% on-year, below +14.6% tipped in Dow Jones poll, vs +9.9% in July. Output down 6.3% on-month seasonally adjusted vs 2.3% fall forecast, revised +1.5% in July. Spot gold at $1,292.90/oz, up 40 cents from NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures down 30 cents at $74.88/bbl on Globex. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] India three-day call rates up as banks borrow to cover cash positions on RBI's reporting Friday, says dealer with state-run bank; interbank call rates on RBI's negotiated dealing system at 5.90% vs 5.00% at Thursday's close, collateralized borrowing and lending obligations or CBLO flat at 4.00% since open. Banks must report their cash positions to RBI on Friday of every fortnight. "Call rates may ease a bit from here as most banks have more or less borrowed cash in the first half of day," says dealer. Tips call rates in 5.50%-6.00% range in session, CBLO likely to be in 4.00%-5.00% range in session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com) [ 09-24-10 0256ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100924e69o0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
